{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HV1V83dqgFATPpKF5AyncU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The routing backend\n2\t\n3\tThe delegation backend that routes each subtask to the model that matches its\n4\tshape. Install it, then select it in the active profile's config.\n5\t\n6\t## What it does\n7\t\n8\tCore calls `route_task(task, config)` once per delegated subtask. The\n9\tbackend inspects that subtask's goal, picks a tier, and returns the model,\n10\tprovider, endpoint, and request overrides for that subtask alone.\n11\t\n12\tWithout it, `delegation.model` pins one model for every child in the batch.\n13\t\n14\t**It chooses; it never loads.** The device's resident set is a shared, finite\n15\tresource and a load is a swap that evicts whatever was resident, so the\n16\tloading half of the job lives in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` -- a step the manager\n17\tagent takes explicitly. See [lifecycle.md](lifecycle.md).\n18\t\n19\tThe two halves meet at the **plan** (`~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json`): the\n20\tcontroller writes it when it plans a batch, and the router reads it when it\n21\troutes each subtask. That is how a specialist the controller just loaded\n22\tbecomes the specialist the child actually runs on.\n23\t\n24\t## Install\n25\t\n26\t1. Install the package. It copies one directory, holding the backend\n27\t   (`__init__.py`, `plugin.yaml`, `tiers.py`, `thinking.py`), the controller\n28\t   scripts and this skill, to `$HERMES_HOME/plugins/tiiny-router/`\n29\t   (`HERMES_HOME` defaults to `~/.hermes`), then runs\n30\t   `hermes plugins validate` on it:\n31\t\n32\t   ```bash\n33\t   npx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter install\n34\t   ```\n35\t\n36\t2. Add the device as a provider in the active profile's `config.yaml`.\n37\t   `hermesrouter install --configure` does this and the backend\n38\t   selection in step 3 through `hermes config set` (everything except\n39\t   `fallback_providers`, which depends on your paid provider):\n40\t\n41\t   ```yaml\n42\t   providers:\n43\t     tiiny:\n44\t       name: tiiny\n45\t       api: http://openai.api.tiiny/v1\n46\t       key_env: TIINY_API_KEY\n47\t       transport: chat_completions\n48\t       default_model: Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\n49\t       context_length: 262144\n50\t       models_discovered: false\n51\t       extra_body:\n52\t         chat_template_kwargs:\n53\t           enable_thinking: false\n54\t       models:\n55\t         Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B: {context_length: 262144}\n56\t         Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B: {context_length: 262144}\n57\t         Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B: {context_length: 131072}\n58\t         openai/gpt-oss-20b: {context_length: 131072}\n59\t         ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n60\t         InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n61\t   ```\n62\t\n63\t   The bearer goes in the environment as `TIINY_API_KEY`.\n64\t\n65\t   `models_discovered: false` matters: with it on, a live probe can wipe the\n66\t   metadata above and leave a model with the wrong context length.\n67\t\n68\t3. Select the backend in the same `config.yaml`:\n69\t\n70\t   ```yaml\n71\t   delegation:\n72\t     backend: tiiny-router\n73\t     fallback_providers:\n74\t       - provider: minimax\n75\t         model: MiniMax-M3\n76\t   ```\n77\t\n78\t   The fallback matters: if the device is unreachable, the batch should still\n79\t   complete on a paid provider rather than fail. It should also be visible\n80\t   when it happens -- a silent fallback means the local tier is not actually\n81\t   being used.\n82\t\n83\t4. Restart the agent so the plugin and the new schema are picked up. If the\n84\t   agent runs under a gateway that cannot be restarted, the gateway's\n85\t   control socket reloads plugins in place:\n86\t\n87\t   ```python\n88\t   from pathlib import Path\n89\t   from gateway.control_socket import reload_gateway_plugins\n90\t   from hermes_constants import get_hermes_home\n91\t\n92\t   reload_gateway_plugins(Path(get_hermes_home()))\n93\t   ```\n94\t\n95\t## Verify\n96\t\n97\t```bash\n98\tnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter doctor\n99\tnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter \\\n100\t  route-check \"index every file in the notes folder and classify each\"\n101\tnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter \\\n102\t  route-check --thinking off \"index every file in the notes folder\"\n103\tnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter ctl status\n104\t```\n105\t\n106\t`doctor` checks the installed directory, the Hermes config and the device.\n107\t`route-check` and `ctl` run `scripts/route_check.py` and\n108\t`scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` from the installed directory with the system\n109\t`python3`.\n110\t\n111\tThe checker prints the tier, the model, that model's NPU share and context\n112\twindow, what the model can do with reasoning, and whether it is currently\n113\tresident. The controller prints the device, the resident set, the lock, the\n114\thold and the plan.\n115\t\n116\tTo confirm the backend is live, delegate a batch with mixed subtasks and read\n117\tthe `route` on each child's result -- it carries the backend name and the\n118\ttier label, with the achieved reasoning state on it.\n119\t\n120\t## Thinking mode\n121\t\n122\tEach task may carry `thinking: \"auto\" | \"on\" | \"off\"`. The default is\n123\t`auto`, which changes nothing and leaves the model's native behaviour alone.\n124\t\n125\tThe router reads the device's own capability for the model it picked, and\n126\tapplies the control that model actually honours. Two knobs exist and they\n127\tare not interchangeable:\n128\t\n129\t| Control | Where it goes | Who honours it |\n130\t|---|---|---|\n131\t| `enable_thinking` | `extra_body.chat_template_kwargs` | Models whose chat template has the switch |\n132\t| `reasoning_effort` | Top level of the request | The inference server, for models whose chat template has no switch |\n133\t\n134\tA level placed inside `chat_template_kwargs` is a **different** knob: some\n135\tchat templates read it, others ignore it silently. Put the level at the top\n136\tlevel of the request.\n137\t\n138\tWhere a model has no working switch, `off` means **its cheapest reasoning\n139\tlevel**, not a refusal. The model still reasons; it just reasons less.\n140\t\n141\tThe route's label carries the achieved state, never the requested one:\n142\t\n143\t```\n144\tindex [think:off→low]\n145\tindex [think:off]\n146\tgeneral [think:unsupported]\n147\t```\n148\t\n149\tA reasoning model also needs an output floor. With a small `max_tokens` the\n150\ttrace eats the budget and the request comes back HTTP 200 with an empty\n151\tanswer. The router applies a floor of 4096; raise it with\n152\t`delegation.backends.tiiny-router.thinking_max_tokens` if you raise your own\n153\tbudget.\n154\t\n155\t## The tiers\n156\t\n157\t| Tier | Model | NPU | Context | Reasoning |\n158\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n159\t| `research` | `InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo` | 48% | 262144 | switch |\n160\t| `research` (alt) | `endless-frontier/BigBang-v1-Turbo` | 54% | 131072 | switch |\n161\t| `index` | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | 27% | 131072 | levels only |\n162\t| `code` | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo` | 55% | 65536 | none |\n163\t| `code` (alt) | `ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo` | 54% | 262144 | switch |\n164\t| `code` (alt) | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch |\n165\t| `code` (heavy) | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next` | 94% | 131072 | none |\n166\t| `browser` | `Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B` | 52% | 262144 | switch |\n167\t| `draft` | `Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B` | 29% | 131072 | switch |\n168\t| `general` | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch |\n169\t\n170\t**Re-read these numbers before trusting them.** They came from one device's\n171\tcatalogue endpoint on 2026-11-09, when the store held 92 models. The store\n172\tchanges with every TiinyOS update, and the NPU shares have already moved\n173\tonce.\n174\t\n175\tThe reasoning column is the same kind of snapshot, and the device is the\n176\tauthority on it: the router reads the capability at route time, so a stale\n177\trow here cannot break a route.\n178\t\n179\tThey live in **`tiers.py`**, which is the single source of truth: the\n180\tplugin, the controller and the checker all read that one file, so they cannot\n181\tdrift apart. It is loaded by path rather than by package name, because the\n182\tHermes plugin loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`\n183\t-- which does not establish package semantics, so a relative import would\n184\tfail there. `thinking.py` is loaded the same way, and is kept free of\n185\tHermes imports so the standalone checker can use it too.\n186\t\n187\t## Changing a tier\n188\t\n189\tThree edits, in this order, all in `tiers.py`:\n190\t\n191\t1. `TIER_CHOICES` -- the model id, its NPU share, and its context window. All\n192\t   three come from the device; `tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh` tells you\n193\t   when they have moved.\n194\t2. `TIER_MODEL` -- derived from `TIER_CHOICES`; it needs no separate edit\n195\t   unless a tier's default changes.\n196\t3. `SHAPE_SIGNALS` -- the signals that route a task to that tier. More\n197\t   specific shapes come first; the first tier with a matching signal wins.\n198\t\n199\tMake the change in the package, not in the installed directory: `install`\n200\trefuses to overwrite a locally edited file, and `doctor` reports one. Then\n201\tupdate the same table in `SKILL.md`, reinstall, and re-run the checker on a\n202\tknown goal.\n203\t\n204\t## Things that will bite\n205\t\n206\t- **The route's shape is the credential path.** The backend returns the\n207\t  delegation section's own shape with only the model overridden -- the way\n208\t  the default backend returns `dict(config)`. This is not cosmetic. A route\n209\t  with `base_url` set and `api_key` empty is a *direct endpoint*, and an\n210\t  empty key means **inherit the parent's key**. The parent runs on the paid\n211\t  provider, so the device would receive the wrong bearer and answer\n212\t  `401 auth_failed` -- and the child would fall back to the paid provider,\n213\t  invisibly. Returning the section's shape puts this backend on the\n214\t  provider-resolution branch, where the device's `key_env` is honoured.\n215\t- **The router reads residency, and that changes the route.** It queries\n216\t  `GET /v1/models` on the inference endpoint (30-second cache) and picks a\n217\t  model that is actually loaded: the plan's assignment if there is one, then\n218\t  the tier's first choice, then another candidate from the same tier, then\n219\t  the generalist. A model that is not resident answers `404 \"is not loaded\"`,\n220\t  which Hermes treats as terminal and falls back to the paid provider -- so\n221\t  routing to an unloaded model silently moves work off the device. The\n222\t  route's `label` records any substitution (`code->generalist`). If the\n223\t  lookup fails, the router fails open to the tier's first choice.\n224\t- **The router never loads a model.** A load is a swap, and it evicts\n225\t  whatever was resident -- including the model a sibling task is using. The\n226\t  controller does the loading, explicitly, under a lock.\n227\t- **An unknown tier is refused.** The override fails loudly rather than\n228\t  falling back to the generalist, because a silent fallback is invisible in\n229\t  the output and expensive in the tokens.\n230\t- **The task schema is cached.** `task_parameters()` and `description()` must\n231\t  be deterministic for a given config -- the schema is part of the cached\n232\t  prompt prefix, so a non-deterministic result changes the prompt between\n233\t  calls.\n234\t- **Thinking mode is per task, not per config.** `thinking: \"auto\" | \"on\" |\n235\t  \"off\"` is a task parameter, and the route's label reports the state the\n236\t  model actually reached. Do not read \"off\" in the request as \"off\" in the\n237\t  model: a model with no switch ends up at its cheapest level instead, and\n238\t  the label says `off→low`.\n239\t- **Two reasoning knobs, and they go in different places.**\n240\t  `enable_thinking` belongs in `extra_body.chat_template_kwargs`; a named\n241\t  level belongs at the **top level of the request**, not inside\n242\t  `chat_template_kwargs`. The same level name inside the chat template is a\n243\t  different knob that some model families read and others ignore silently --\n244\t  which looks configured and changes nothing.\n245\t- **The device's `toggleable` flag has been wrong.** A model flagged as\n246\t  having no on/off switch honours it anyway. Send the boolean whenever it\n247\t  can be sent, and let the label report what actually took effect.\n248\t- **A reasoning model with a small `max_tokens` returns an empty response**\n249\t  with the budget spent in the trace, and the status code is 200. The router\n250\t  applies an output floor for this reason.\n251\t- **Hermes secrets are not in the environment.** The device key lives in\n252\t  `~/.hermes/.env` and is read through `hermes_cli.config`'s accessor. A\n253\t  plugin that reads `os.environ` finds nothing, and the residency check\n254\t  fails open on every call.\n255\t- **A partially-installed plugin fails at load, not at use.** The plugin\n256\t  loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`, so\n257\t  `__init__.py` cannot use a relative import to reach `tiers.py` -- it loads\n258\t  that file by path. `install` copies the whole directory; a hand copy of\n259\t  just `__init__.py` and `plugin.yaml` is not enough. A tree with `__init__.py` but no\n260\t  `tiers.py` raises `ImportError` at load, and the backend is then simply\n261\t  absent from `hermes plugins list` -- which reads as \"never installed\"\n262\t  rather than \"installed wrong\". Validate with\n263\t  `hermes plugins validate <dir>`. The same applies to `thinking.py`.\n264\t- **There is one copy of everything.** The skill is served from the plugin\n265\t  directory (the backend registers it as `tiiny-router:tiiny-model-routing`),\n266\t  and nothing is written under `~/.hermes/skills/`. A copy of the backend\n267\t  or the skill installed by hand somewhere else is not part of this install\n268\t  and can carry a stale tier table; remove it by hand.\n269\t- **A saved `delegation.backend` does not change a running chat.** The\n270\t  `delegate_task` schema is cached in the prompt prefix, so a config edit\n271\t  applies from the next session (`/new`), not the current one. Verify the\n272\t  backend in a fresh process rather than expecting the current session to\n273\t  re-route:\n274\t  `python3 -c \"from agent import delegation_backends as db; print(db.list_backends())\"`\n275\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b63ad6aa-1dd3-487c-8e9f-f16892a2390c","uuid":"d77951ec-2fe2-45e7-b56f-1a533e162dbc","timestamp":"2026-11-22T01:18:03.066Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.install-workflow-fixes-04-router-plan-path-base-home/tiiny-router/references/backend.md","content":"# The routing backend\n\nThe delegation backend that routes each subtask to the model that matches its\nshape. Install it, then select it in the active profile's config.\n\n## What it does\n\nCore calls `route_task(task, config)` once per delegated subtask. The\nbackend inspects that subtask's goal, picks a tier, and returns the model,\nprovider, endpoint, and request overrides for that subtask alone.\n\nWithout it, `delegation.model` pins one model for every child in the batch.\n\n**It chooses; it never loads.** The device's resident set is a shared, finite\nresource and a load is a swap that evicts whatever was resident, so the\nloading half of the job lives in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` -- a step the manager\nagent takes explicitly. See [lifecycle.md](lifecycle.md).\n\nThe two halves meet at the **plan** (`~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json`): the\ncontroller writes it when it plans a batch, and the router reads it when it\nroutes each subtask. That is how a specialist the controller just loaded\nbecomes the specialist the child actually runs on.\n\n## Install\n\n1. Install the package. It copies one directory, holding the backend\n   (`__init__.py`, `plugin.yaml`, `tiers.py`, `thinking.py`), the controller\n   scripts and this skill, to `$HERMES_HOME/plugins/tiiny-router/`\n   (`HERMES_HOME` defaults to `~/.hermes`), then runs\n   `hermes plugins validate` on it:\n\n   ```bash\n   npx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter install\n   ```\n\n2. Add the device as a provider in the active profile's `config.yaml`.\n   `hermesrouter install --configure` does this and the backend\n   selection in step 3 through `hermes config set` (everything except\n   `fallback_providers`, which depends on your paid provider):\n\n   ```yaml\n   providers:\n     tiiny:\n       name: tiiny\n       api: http://openai.api.tiiny/v1\n       key_env: TIINY_API_KEY\n       transport: chat_completions\n       default_model: Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\n       context_length: 262144\n       models_discovered: false\n       extra_body:\n         chat_template_kwargs:\n           enable_thinking: false\n       models:\n         Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B: {context_length: 262144}\n         Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B: {context_length: 262144}\n         Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B: {context_length: 131072}\n         openai/gpt-oss-20b: {context_length: 131072}\n         ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n         InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n   ```\n\n   The bearer goes in the environment as `TIINY_API_KEY`.\n\n   `models_discovered: false` matters: with it on, a live probe can wipe the\n   metadata above and leave a model with the wrong context length.\n\n3. Select the backend in the same `config.yaml`:\n\n   ```yaml\n   delegation:\n     backend: tiiny-router\n     fallback_providers:\n       - provider: minimax\n         model: MiniMax-M3\n   ```\n\n   The fallback matters: if the device is unreachable, the batch should still\n   complete on a paid provider rather than fail. It should also be visible\n   when it happens -- a silent fallback means the local tier is not actually\n   being used.\n\n4. Restart the agent so the plugin and the new schema are picked up. If the\n   agent runs under a gateway that cannot be restarted, the gateway's\n   control socket reloads plugins in place:\n\n   ```python\n   from pathlib import Path\n   from gateway.control_socket import reload_gateway_plugins\n   from hermes_constants import get_hermes_home\n\n   reload_gateway_plugins(Path(get_hermes_home()))\n   ```\n\n## Verify\n\n```bash\nnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter doctor\nnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter \\\n  route-check \"index every file in the notes folder and classify each\"\nnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter \\\n  route-check --thinking off \"index every file in the notes folder\"\nnpx --package hermes-tiiny-router hermesrouter ctl status\n```\n\n`doctor` checks the installed directory, the Hermes config and the device.\n`route-check` and `ctl` run `scripts/route_check.py` and\n`scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` from the installed directory with the system\n`python3`.\n\nThe checker prints the tier, the model, that model's NPU share and context\nwindow, what the model can do with reasoning, and whether it is currently\nresident. The controller prints the device, the resident set, the lock, the\nhold and the plan.\n\nTo confirm the backend is live, delegate a batch with mixed subtasks and read\nthe `route` on each child's result -- it carries the backend name and the\ntier label, with the achieved reasoning state on it.\n\n## Thinking mode\n\nEach task may carry `thinking: \"auto\" | \"on\" | \"off\"`. The default is\n`auto`, which changes nothing and leaves the model's native behaviour alone.\n\nThe router reads the device's own capability for the model it picked, and\napplies the control that model actually honours. Two knobs exist and they\nare not interchangeable:\n\n| Control | Where it goes | Who honours it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `enable_thinking` | `extra_body.chat_template_kwargs` | Models whose chat template has the switch |\n| `reasoning_effort` | Top level of the request | The inference server, for models whose chat template has no switch |\n\nA level placed inside `chat_template_kwargs` is a **different** knob: some\nchat templates read it, others ignore it silently. Put the level at the top\nlevel of the request.\n\nWhere a model has no working switch, `off` means **its cheapest reasoning\nlevel**, not a refusal. The model still reasons; it just reasons less.\n\nThe route's label carries the achieved state, never the requested one:\n\n```\nindex [think:off→low]\nindex [think:off]\ngeneral [think:unsupported]\n```\n\nA reasoning model also needs an output floor. With a small `max_tokens` the\ntrace eats the budget and the request comes back HTTP 200 with an empty\nanswer. The router applies a floor of 4096; raise it with\n`delegation.backends.tiiny-router.thinking_max_tokens` if you raise your own\nbudget.\n\n## The tiers\n\n| Tier | Model | NPU | Context | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `research` | `InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo` | 48% | 262144 | switch |\n| `research` (alt) | `endless-frontier/BigBang-v1-Turbo` | 54% | 131072 | switch |\n| `index` | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | 27% | 131072 | levels only |\n| `code` | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo` | 55% | 65536 | none |\n| `code` (alt) | `ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo` | 54% | 262144 | switch |\n| `code` (alt) | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch |\n| `code` (heavy) | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next` | 94% | 131072 | none |\n| `browser` | `Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B` | 52% | 262144 | switch |\n| `draft` | `Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B` | 29% | 131072 | switch |\n| `general` | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch |\n\n**Re-read these numbers before trusting them.** They came from one device's\ncatalogue endpoint on 2026-11-09, when the store held 92 models. The store\nchanges with every TiinyOS update, and the NPU shares have already moved\nonce.\n\nThe reasoning column is the same kind of snapshot, and the device is the\nauthority on it: the router reads the capability at route time, so a stale\nrow here cannot break a route.\n\nThey live in **`tiers.py`**, which is the single source of truth: the\nplugin, the controller and the checker all read that one file, so they cannot\ndrift apart. It is loaded by path rather than by package name, because the\nHermes plugin loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`\n-- which does not establish package semantics, so a relative import would\nfail there. `thinking.py` is loaded the same way, and is kept free of\nHermes imports so the standalone checker can use it too.\n\n## Changing a tier\n\nThree edits, in this order, all in `tiers.py`:\n\n1. `TIER_CHOICES` -- the model id, its NPU share, and its context window. All\n   three come from the device; `tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh` tells you\n   when they have moved.\n2. `TIER_MODEL` -- derived from `TIER_CHOICES`; it needs no separate edit\n   unless a tier's default changes.\n3. `SHAPE_SIGNALS` -- the signals that route a task to that tier. More\n   specific shapes come first; the first tier with a matching signal wins.\n\nMake the change in the package, not in the installed directory: `install`\nrefuses to overwrite a locally edited file, and `doctor` reports one. Then\nupdate the same table in `SKILL.md`, reinstall, and re-run the checker on a\nknown goal.\n\n## Things that will bite\n\n- **The route's shape is the credential path.** The backend returns the\n  delegation section's own shape with only the model overridden -- the way\n  the default backend returns `dict(config)`. This is not cosmetic. A route\n  with `base_url` set and `api_key` empty is a *direct endpoint*, and an\n  empty key means **inherit the parent's key**. The parent runs on the paid\n  provider, so the device would receive the wrong bearer and answer\n  `401 auth_failed` -- and the child would fall back to the paid provider,\n  invisibly. Returning the section's shape puts this backend on the\n  provider-resolution branch, where the device's `key_env` is honoured.\n- **The router reads residency, and that changes the route.** It queries\n  `GET /v1/models` on the inference endpoint (30-second cache) and picks a\n  model that is actually loaded: the plan's assignment if there is one, then\n  the tier's first choice, then another candidate from the same tier, then\n  the generalist. A model that is not resident answers `404 \"is not loaded\"`,\n  which Hermes treats as terminal and falls back to the paid provider -- so\n  routing to an unloaded model silently moves work off the device. The\n  route's `label` records any substitution (`code->generalist`). If the\n  lookup fails, the router fails open to the tier's first choice.\n- **The router never loads a model.** A load is a swap, and it evicts\n  whatever was resident -- including the model a sibling task is using. The\n  controller does the loading, explicitly, under a lock.\n- **An unknown tier is refused.** The override fails loudly rather than\n  falling back to the generalist, because a silent fallback is invisible in\n  the output and expensive in the tokens.\n- **The task schema is cached.** `task_parameters()` and `description()` must\n  be deterministic for a given config -- the schema is part of the cached\n  prompt prefix, so a non-deterministic result changes the prompt between\n  calls.\n- **Thinking mode is per task, not per config.** `thinking: \"auto\" | \"on\" |\n  \"off\"` is a task parameter, and the route's label reports the state the\n  model actually reached. Do not read \"off\" in the request as \"off\" in the\n  model: a model with no switch ends up at its cheapest level instead, and\n  the label says `off→low`.\n- **Two reasoning knobs, and they go in different places.**\n  `enable_thinking` belongs in `extra_body.chat_template_kwargs`; a named\n  level belongs at the **top level of the request**, not inside\n  `chat_template_kwargs`. The same level name inside the chat template is a\n  different knob that some model families read and others ignore silently --\n  which looks configured and changes nothing.\n- **The device's `toggleable` flag has been wrong.** A model flagged as\n  having no on/off switch honours it anyway. Send the boolean whenever it\n  can be sent, and let the label report what actually took effect.\n- **A reasoning model with a small `max_tokens` returns an empty response**\n  with the budget spent in the trace, and the status code is 200. The router\n  applies an output floor for this reason.\n- **Hermes secrets are not in the environment.** The device key lives in\n  `~/.hermes/.env` and is read through `hermes_cli.config`'s accessor. A\n  plugin that reads `os.environ` finds nothing, and the residency check\n  fails open on every call.\n- **A partially-installed plugin fails at load, not at use.** The plugin\n  loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`, so\n  `__init__.py` cannot use a relative import to reach `tiers.py` -- it loads\n  that file by path. `install` copies the whole directory; a hand copy of\n  just `__init__.py` and `plugin.yaml` is not enough. A tree with `__init__.py` but no\n  `tiers.py` raises `ImportError` at load, and the backend is then simply\n  absent from `hermes plugins list` -- which reads as \"never installed\"\n  rather than \"installed wrong\". Validate with\n  `hermes plugins validate <dir>`. The same applies to `thinking.py`.\n- **There is one copy of everything.** The skill is served from the plugin\n  directory (the backend registers it as `tiiny-router:tiiny-model-routing`),\n  and nothing is written under `~/.hermes/skills/`. A copy of the backend\n  or the skill installed by hand somewhere else is not part of this install\n  and can carry a stale tier table; remove it by hand.\n- **A saved `delegation.backend` does not change a running chat.** The\n  `delegate_task` schema is cached in the prompt prefix, so a config edit\n  applies from the next session (`/new`), not the current one. Verify the\n  backend in a fresh process rather than expecting the current session to\n  re-route:\n  `python3 -c \"from agent import delegation_backends as db; print(db.list_backends())\"`\n","numLines":275,"startLine":1,"totalLines":275}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HV1V83dqgFATPpKF5AyncU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011ChH5tVieoUoDGBEA8NXW5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014um414gi1pmKJMj7MFTLiJ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.install-workflow-fixes-04-router-plan-path-base-home/test/test_register.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4600,"cache_read_input_tokens":36334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4600},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b63ad6aa-1dd3-487c-8e9f-f16892a2390c","uuid":"df1587b1-6ee4-4cb5-9444-89ad07e534d2","timestamp":"2026-11-22T01:18:03.461Z","request_id":"req_011ChH5tVaUfmwNBbqtmymgc","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014um414gi1pmKJMj7MFTLiJ":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.install-workflow-fixes-04-router-plan-path-base-home/test/test_register.py"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gn8VzgQEiHcpZ8NEBt5dwq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Tiiny routing delegation backend (``delegation.backend: tiiny-router``).\n2\t\n3\tRoutes each ``delegate_task`` subtask to the Tiiny model that suits its shape,\n4\tinstead of sending every subtask to one pinned model.\n5\t\n6\tWhy a backend rather than a config value\n7\t----------------------------------------\n8\t``delegation.model`` pins ONE model for every child. That is the right default\n9\twhen every subtask is the same kind of work. It is the wrong default when a\n10\tsingle delegation batch mixes a web-research sweep, a filesystem index, a\n11\tterminal code edit, and a browser-automation run: the cheap subtasks pay for\n12\tthe expensive model's context, and the expensive subtask gets a model that is\n13\tnot specialised for it.\n14\t\n15\tA backend solves that at the routing layer. ``route_task`` is called once per\n16\ttask with that task's goal and context, and returns the model, provider, and\n17\trequest overrides for that subtask alone.\n18\t\n19\tRouting is by task shape, not by keyword\n20\t-----------------------------------------\n21\tThe route is chosen from the task's *shape* — what kind of work it is — because\n22\tshape is what predicts which model does well. A goal that mentions \"index the\n23\tvault\" is an indexing job; a goal that says \"refactor these three modules\" is\n24\ta coding job. Matching on keywords in the prompt is brittle in a different way:\n25\ta research task about code will match code keywords and get the coder.\n26\t\n27\tThe shape is read from the goal text, with the explicit ``tier`` parameter as\n28\tan override. When neither gives a confident answer, the task goes to the\n29\tgeneralist, which is the right default: it is the model that is good at\n30\teverything in the catalogue and mediocre at each, and being wrong about a\n31\tspecialist is more expensive than being mediocre at a general task.\n32\t\n33\tThinking mode is a per-task parameter\n34\t--------------------------------------\n35\tThe ``thinking`` parameter (``\"auto\" | \"on\" | \"off\"``) controls whether the\n36\tmodel reasons before it answers. Default is ``\"auto\"`` — the model's native\n37\tbehaviour, untouched.\n38\t\n39\t``\"off\"`` is for mechanical work: indexing, formatting, classification. A\n40\treasoning model spends its budget on a trace before the answer, and on a small\n41\tbudget that trace can eat the whole budget and return an empty response.\n42\t\n43\t``\"on\"`` is for genuinely novel reasoning, where the model's work is worth\n44\tseeing.\n45\t\n46\tWhat the request asks for and what the model does are not the same thing, and\n47\tthe route reports the difference. Some models have a working on/off switch;\n48\tsome have no switch and can only be moved between named reasoning levels;\n49\tsome do not reason at all. So the router reads the device's own capability\n50\tfor the model it picked, applies the control that model actually honours, and\n51\tputs the **achieved** state in the route label — ``off`` when the switch\n52\tworked, ``off→low`` when the model could only be turned down to its cheapest\n53\tlevel, ``unsupported`` when the model does not reason, ``unknown`` when the\n54\tdevice would not say.\n55\t\n56\tSee ``thinking.py`` for how the capability is read and which control each\n57\tkind of model honours.\n58\t\n59\tThe router chooses; the lifecycle loads\n60\t---------------------------------------\n61\t**This plugin never loads, unloads, or otherwise mutates the device.** That is\n62\tnot caution, it is a division of labour: the device's resident set is a\n63\t*shared, finite* resource, and a load is a swap that evicts whatever was\n64\tresident.\n65\t\n66\tThe loading half lives in ``scripts/tiiny_ctl.py``, which the manager agent\n67\tdrives explicitly: plan the batch, take the cross-agent lock, load what the\n68\tbatch needs, delegate, then release. Between those two calls the router and\n69\tthe controller agree on one file — the **plan** (``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``).\n70\t\n71\tThe plan is what makes the two halves agree. When the controller has loaded a\n72\tspecialist for a batch, the plan records which model it assigned to which\n73\tgoal, and this plugin routes that goal to that model. Without the plan the\n74\trouter falls back to the residency-only rule below, which is safe but cannot\n75\tuse a specialist that is not loaded yet.\n76\t\n77\tHowever, when **no plan is used** (a single task, or ad-hoc delegation), the\n78\trouter calls ``ctl.ensure_model()`` automatically after choosing the model.\n79\tThis puts the specialist on the device before the child runs, without an extra\n80\tmanual step. The call is idempotent — when the model is already resident, it\n81\tcosts one HTTP round-trip.\n82\t\n83\tThe two halves therefore compose like this:\n84\t\n85\t    controller: plan -> reserve -> load      |  plugin: route_task + ensure\n86\t    controller: release                      |  child runs on the plan's model\n87\t\n88\tResidency is read, never changed\n89\t--------------------------------\n90\tThe plugin does read the device's resident set, and it uses it. A model that\n91\tis not resident cannot answer — the device replies ``404 \"is not loaded\"``,\n92\twhich Hermes classifies as terminal and falls back to the paid provider. On a\n93\tdevice whose shared NPU pool holds only a few models at a time, that turns a\n94\trouting decision into a silent move off the local device.\n95\t\n96\tSo the router picks a model that is actually resident when it can: the plan's\n97\tassignment if there is one, then the tier's own first choice, then another\n98\tcandidate from the same tier, then the generalist the watchdog keeps resident.\n99\tThe label on the route records any substitution (``code→generalist``), so the\n100\toperator can see that the specialist was not available. When residency cannot\n101\tbe determined at all the router behaves exactly as it did before: the tier's\n102\tfirst choice.\n103\t\"\"\"\n104\t\n105\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n106\t\n107\timport importlib.util\n108\timport json\n109\timport os\n110\timport subprocess\n111\timport sys\n112\timport time\n113\timport urllib.request\n114\tfrom pathlib import Path\n115\tfrom typing import Any, Mapping\n116\t\n117\tfrom agent.delegation_provider import DelegationProvider, DelegationRouteError\n118\t\n119\t#: The shared tier table. Loaded by path — see the note in ``tiers.py`` for\n120\t#: why a relative import would break under the Hermes plugin loader.\n121\t_TIERS_PATH = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"tiers.py\"\n122\t_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"tiiny_router_tiers\", _TIERS_PATH)\n123\tif _spec is None or _spec.loader is None:  # pragma: no cover - packaging error\n124\t    raise ImportError(f\"could not load the tier table from {_TIERS_PATH}\")\n125\ttiers = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_spec)\n126\t_spec.loader.exec_module(tiers)\n127\t\n128\t#: The reasoning-mode control. Also loaded by path, and deliberately free of\n129\t#: Hermes imports so the standalone checker can use it too.\n130\t_THINKING_PATH = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"thinking.py\"\n131\t_thinking_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"tiiny_router_thinking\", _THINKING_PATH)\n132\tif _thinking_spec is None or _thinking_spec.loader is None:  # pragma: no cover\n133\t    raise ImportError(f\"could not load the thinking control from {_THINKING_PATH}\")\n134\tthinking = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_thinking_spec)\n135\t_thinking_spec.loader.exec_module(thinking)\n136\t\n137\tTIER_CHOICES = tiers.TIER_CHOICES\n138\tTIER_MODEL = tiers.TIER_MODEL\n139\tVALID_TIERS = tiers.VALID_TIERS\n140\tGENERALIST = tiers.GENERALIST\n141\tSHAPE_SIGNALS = tiers.SHAPE_SIGNALS\n142\tdetect_tier = tiers.detect_tier\n143\t\n144\t#: The device's OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Local, on the LAN.\n145\tTIINY_BASE_URL = tiers.TIINY_BASE_URL\n146\t\n147\t#: Provider name as configured in ``providers:`` in config.yaml.\n148\tTIINY_PROVIDER = tiers.TIINY_PROVIDER\n149\t\n150\t#: The thinking modes a task may ask for.\n151\tTHINKING_MODES = (\"auto\", \"on\", \"off\")\n152\t\n153\t#: The default output floor for a task that asks a model to reason.\n154\tTHINKING_MAX_TOKENS = thinking.THINKING_MAX_TOKENS\n155\t\n156\t#: How long a residency lookup is reused, in seconds. The device's resident\n157\t#: set changes on a swap, not on a heartbeat, so a short cache is enough and\n158\t#: keeps the routing path cheap.\n159\tRESIDENCY_TTL = 30\n160\t\n161\t#: Cached residency lookup: (monotonic timestamp, frozenset of model ids).\n162\t_residency_cache: tuple[float, frozenset[str]] | None = None\n163\t\n164\t#: The plan the lifecycle controller writes. It lives in the BASE Hermes home\n165\t#: on purpose: every profile's manager agent must see the same plan, and the\n166\t#: base home is what they share.\n167\tPLAN_FILE = Path(\n168\t    os.environ.get(\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\", \"~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json\")\n169\t).expanduser()\n170\t\n171\t#: Whether the router calls ``ensure`` on every route.  When ``True`` the\n172\t#: router silently loads any model that is not yet resident (idempotent, so\n173\t#: a resident model costs only an HTTP round-trip).  When ``False`` the\n174\t#: router behaves as before — picking what is resident, falling back on\n175\t#: what is not, with no loading.\n176\t#:\n177\t#: This is the config toggle for Stage 3.  The default is ``True`` — the\n178\t#: ``ensure`` call makes the device ready for the child without an extra\n179\t#: manual step.\n180\tENSURE_ON_ROUTE = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_ENSURE_ON_ROUTE\", \"true\").lower() != \"false\"\n181\t\n182\t#: The controller module. Loaded by path (see ``tiers.py`` for why a\n183\t#: relative import would break under the Hermes plugin loader). It sits in\n184\t#: ``scripts/`` inside this plugin directory.\n185\t_CTL_SPEC = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n186\t    \"tiiny_controller\",\n187\t    Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"scripts\" / \"tiiny_ctl.py\",\n188\t)\n189\tif _CTL_SPEC is None or _CTL_SPEC.loader is None:\n190\t    ctl = None\n191\telse:\n192\t    ctl = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_CTL_SPEC)\n193\t    try:\n194\t        _CTL_SPEC.loader.exec_module(ctl)\n195\t    except Exception:\n196\t        ctl = None\n197\t\n198\t\n199\tdef device_key() -> str:\n200\t    \"\"\"Return the device API key, or an empty string if it cannot be found.\n201\t\n202\t    Hermes keeps its secrets in ``~/.hermes/.env`` and resolves them through\n203\t    its own accessor — they are **not** in the process environment. Reading\n204\t    ``os.environ`` alone therefore finds nothing, and the router would\n205\t    silently fail open on every call, which is exactly the failure this\n206\t    lookup exists to prevent. Read it the way Hermes reads it, and fall back\n207\t    to the environment for a shell that exported it.\n208\t    \"\"\"\n209\t    value = \"\"\n210\t    try:\n211\t        from hermes_cli.config import get_env_value_prefer_dotenv\n212\t\n213\t        value = get_env_value_prefer_dotenv(\"TIINY_API_KEY\") or \"\"\n214\t    except Exception:\n215\t        value = \"\"\n216\t    return (value or os.environ.get(\"TIINY_API_KEY\", \"\") or \"\").strip()\n217\t\n218\t\n219\tdef resident_models() -> frozenset[str] | None:\n220\t    \"\"\"Return the model ids currently resident on the device.\n221\t\n222\t    Returns ``None`` when residency cannot be determined — the device is\n223\t    offline, the key is missing, or the lookup timed out. The caller then\n224\t    falls back to the tier's default choice, which is exactly the behaviour\n225\t    from before residency was considered. This path must never raise: a\n226\t    routing decision is not a place to fail.\n227\t    \"\"\"\n228\t    global _residency_cache\n229\t    now = time.monotonic()\n230\t    if _residency_cache is not None and now - _residency_cache[0] < RESIDENCY_TTL:\n231\t        return _residency_cache[1]\n232\t\n233\t    key = device_key()\n234\t    if not key:\n235\t        return None\n236\t    try:\n237\t        # This must be the SAME box the request will go to. The provider\n238\t        # is pinned to one appliance by address; asking a forwarder or a\n239\t        # mDNS hostname instead reads a DIFFERENT device's resident set,\n240\t        # and the router then picks a model the target cannot serve.\n241\t        request = urllib.request.Request(\n242\t            TIINY_BASE_URL + \"/models\",\n243\t            headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + key},\n244\t        )\n245\t        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=3) as response:\n246\t            payload = json.load(response)\n247\t    except Exception:\n248\t        return None\n249\t\n250\t    ids: set[str] = set()\n251\t    for entry in payload.get(\"data\") or []:\n252\t        if not isinstance(entry, dict):\n253\t            continue\n254\t        value = entry.get(\"id\")\n255\t        if isinstance(value, str) and \"/\" in value:\n256\t            ids.add(value)\n257\t    resident = frozenset(ids)\n258\t    _residency_cache = (now, resident)\n259\t    return resident\n260\t\n261\t\n262\tdef _normalise_goal(goal: str) -> str:\n263\t    \"\"\"Collapse whitespace so a goal survives a copy-paste with different spacing.\"\"\"\n264\t    return \" \".join((goal or \"\").split())\n265\t\n266\t\n267\tdef planned_models() -> dict[str, str]:\n268\t    \"\"\"Return the plan's ``goal -> model`` assignments, or an empty mapping.\n269\t\n270\t    The plan is written by ``scripts/tiiny_ctl.py plan`` and lives at\n271\t    ``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``. A missing, unreadable or malformed plan is\n272\t    not an error: it simply means the lifecycle did not run, and the router\n273\t    falls back to the residency-only rule. Routing must never fail because a\n274\t    sidecar file is wrong.\n275\t    \"\"\"\n276\t    try:\n277\t        payload = json.loads(PLAN_FILE.read_text())\n278\t    except Exception:\n279\t        return {}\n280\t    entries = payload.get(\"entries\")\n281\t    if not isinstance(entries, list):\n282\t        return {}\n283\t    planned: dict[str, str] = {}\n284\t    for entry in entries:\n285\t        if not isinstance(entry, dict):\n286\t            continue\n287\t        goal = entry.get(\"goal\")\n288\t        model = entry.get(\"model\")\n289\t        if isinstance(goal, str) and isinstance(model, str) and goal and model:\n290\t            planned[_normalise_goal(goal)] = model\n291\t    return planned\n292\t\n293\t\n294\tdef choose_model(tier: str, goal: str = \"\") -> tuple[str, str]:\n295\t    \"\"\"Pick the model for a task and say how it was picked.\n296\t\n297\t    Returns ``(model, label)``.\n298\t\n299\t    The order is: the plan's assignment for this goal, if there is one and\n300\t    its model is resident; then the tier's own first choice if it is\n301\t    resident; then any other candidate in the tier that is resident; then the\n302\t    generalist, if it is resident; then the tier's first choice regardless.\n303\t\n304\t    The point of the later steps is that a model which is not resident cannot\n305\t    answer — the device answers ``404 \"is not loaded\"``, which Hermes treats as\n306\t    terminal and falls back to the paid provider. On a device whose NPU pool\n307\t    holds only a few models at a time, routing to a specialist that is not\n308\t    loaded would quietly move the work off the device. Preferring a resident\n309\t    model keeps the work local, and the label records the substitution so the\n310\t    operator can see it happened.\n311\t    \"\"\"\n312\t    choices = TIER_CHOICES[tier]\n313\t    default_model = choices[0][0]\n314\t\n315\t    resident = resident_models()\n316\t    plan = planned_models()\n317\t    planned = plan.get(_normalise_goal(goal)) if goal else None\n318\t\n319\t    # The plan wins, but only if what it names is actually there. A plan is a\n320\t    # promise about the device's state, and the device is the authority: if\n321\t    # the planned model is gone, something evicted it and the plan is stale.\n322\t    if planned and (resident is None or planned in resident):\n323\t        if planned == default_model:\n324\t            return planned, tier\n325\t        return planned, f\"{tier}→{planned.split('/')[-1]}\"\n326\t\n327\t    if resident is None:\n328\t        # Residency unknown - the lookup timed out, the key was missing,\n329\t        # or the device answered with something unreadable.\n330\t        #\n331\t        # The generalist is the device's own default model and the one it\n332\t        # serves, so it is the safest thing to ask for. Returning the\n333\t        # tier's first choice here is what made a lookup failure turn\n334\t        # into \"not a valid model ID\" on the device.\n335\t        return GENERALIST, f\"{tier}->generalist\"\n336\t\n337\t    for model, _npu, _ctx in choices:\n338\t        if model in resident:\n339\t            if model == default_model:\n340\t                return model, tier\n341\t            return model, f\"{tier}→{model.split('/')[-1]}\"\n342\t\n343\t    if GENERALIST in resident:\n344\t        return GENERALIST, f\"{tier}→generalist\"\n345\t\n346\t    return default_model, tier\n347\t\n348\t\n349\tdef merge_request_overrides(\n350\t    base: Mapping[str, Any] | None,\n351\t    additions: Mapping[str, Any],\n352\t) -> dict[str, Any]:\n353\t    \"\"\"Merge new request overrides over the ones the route already carries.\n354\t\n355\t    ``extra_body`` is merged one level deep, the way Hermes merges the\n356\t    delegation section's own overrides over the provider's. Everything else is\n357\t    replaced wholesale, because ``reasoning_effort`` and ``max_tokens`` are\n358\t    single values and a half-applied one is worse than either.\n359\t\n360\t    The base matters: the delegation section may already carry an\n361\t    ``extra_body`` of its own, and dropping it would silently change the\n362\t    request for every other key in it.\n363\t    \"\"\"\n364\t    merged: dict[str, Any] = dict(base or {})\n365\t    base_extra = merged.get(\"extra_body\")\n366\t    base_extra = dict(base_extra) if isinstance(base_extra, Mapping) else {}\n367\t\n368\t    extra = additions.get(\"extra_body\")\n369\t    extra = dict(extra) if isinstance(extra, Mapping) else {}\n370\t\n371\t    if base_extra and extra:\n372\t        for key, value in extra.items():\n373\t            if isinstance(value, Mapping) and isinstance(base_extra.get(key), Mapping):\n374\t                nested = dict(base_extra[key])\n375\t                nested.update(value)\n376\t                base_extra[key] = nested\n377\t            else:\n378\t                base_extra[key] = value\n379\t        extra = base_extra\n380\t    elif base_extra:\n381\t        extra = base_extra\n382\t\n383\t    for key, value in additions.items():\n384\t        if key != \"extra_body\":\n385\t            merged[key] = value\n386\t    merged[\"extra_body\"] = extra\n387\t    return merged\n388\t\n389\t\n390\tclass TiinyRouterDelegationProvider(DelegationProvider):\n391\t    \"\"\"Route each subtask to the Tiiny model that fits its shape.\"\"\"\n392\t\n393\t    @property\n394\t    def name(self) -> str:\n395\t        return \"tiiny-router\"\n396\t\n397\t    @property\n398\t    def display_name(self) -> str:\n399\t        return \"Tiiny Router\"\n400\t\n401\t    @property\n402\t    def summary(self) -> str:\n403\t        return (\"Route each subtask to the Tiiny model that matches its shape: \"\n404\t                \"research, index, code, browser, draft, general.\")\n405\t\n406\t    def task_parameters(self, config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> dict[str, dict[str, Any]]:\n407\t        return {\n408\t            \"tier\": {\n409\t                \"type\": \"string\",\n410\t                \"enum\": list(VALID_TIERS),\n411\t                \"description\": (\n412\t                    \"Force a routing tier for this task instead of inferring it from the goal. \"\n413\t                    \"Use when the goal understates the work: a task that says 'read these logs' \"\n414\t                    \"but means 'summarise what went wrong' is a 'draft' task, not a 'general' one.\"\n415\t                ),\n416\t            },\n417\t            \"thinking\": {\n418\t                \"type\": \"string\",\n419\t                \"enum\": list(THINKING_MODES),\n420\t                \"description\": (\n421\t                    \"Reasoning mode for this task. 'auto' (default) leaves the model's native \"\n422\t                    \"behaviour alone. 'off' turns reasoning off for mechanical work - indexing, \"\n423\t                    \"formatting, classification - so the token budget is spent on the answer \"\n424\t                    \"rather than on a trace nobody asked for. 'on' turns reasoning on for novel \"\n425\t                    \"problems where the model's work is worth seeing. Where a model has no \"\n426\t                    \"on/off switch, 'off' means its cheapest reasoning level rather than a \"\n427\t                    \"refusal, and the route label reports what was actually achieved.\"\n428\t                ),\n429\t            },\n430\t        }\n431\t\n432\t    def description(self, config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> str:\n433\t        return (\n434\t            \"Set 'tier' on a task to force a model: \" + \", \".join(VALID_TIERS) + \". \"\n435\t            \"When tier is omitted the model is inferred from the task's shape, so name the \"\n436\t            \"work honestly ('index every file under X') and the cheap specialist gets it. \"\n437\t            \"Set 'thinking' to 'off', 'on', or 'auto' (default) to control reasoning per task: \"\n438\t            \"'off' for mechanical work, 'on' for novel reasoning. The route label reports the \"\n439\t            \"state the model actually reached, which is not always the one that was asked for.\"\n440\t        )\n441\t\n442\t    def route_task(self, task: Mapping[str, Any], config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n443\t        goal = str(task.get(\"goal\") or \"\")\n444\t        forced = task.get(\"tier\")\n445\t        requested_thinking = str(task.get(\"thinking\") or \"auto\").strip().lower()\n446\t\n447\t        if requested_thinking not in THINKING_MODES:\n448\t            raise DelegationRouteError(\n449\t                f\"Unknown thinking mode {requested_thinking!r}. \"\n450\t                f\"Valid modes are: {', '.join(THINKING_MODES)}.\"\n451\t            )\n452\t\n453\t        if forced:\n454\t            tier = str(forced)\n455\t            if tier not in TIER_MODEL:\n456\t                raise DelegationRouteError(\n457\t                    f\"Unknown tier {tier!r}. Valid tiers are: {', '.join(VALID_TIERS)}.\"\n458\t                )\n459\t        else:\n460\t            tier = detect_tier(goal)\n461\t\n462\t        model, label = choose_model(tier, goal)\n463\t\n464\t        # Ensure the chosen model is resident.  Idempotent — when the model\n465\t        # is already running this is one HTTP round-trip; when it is not,\n466\t        # ``ensure_model`` loads it and verifies the load before returning.\n467\t        # Exceptions are caught inside the helper so a load failure cannot\n468\t        # break the routing decision — the device will return 404 and Hermes\n469\t        # will fall back to the paid provider as expected.\n470\t        _ensure_model(model)\n471\t\n472\t        # Return the delegation section's own shape and override only the\n473\t        # model — the way the default backend returns ``dict(config)``.\n474\t        #\n475\t        # This is not cosmetic. Hermes resolves a route's credentials from\n476\t        # ``base_url`` / ``api_key`` / ``provider``:\n477\t        #\n478\t        #   * ``base_url`` set, ``api_key`` empty -> a direct endpoint, and\n479\t        #     ``api_key`` None means INHERIT THE PARENT'S KEY. The parent runs\n480\t        #     on the paid provider, so the device would receive the wrong\n481\t        #     bearer and answer ``401 auth_failed`` — and the child would fall\n482\t        #     back to the paid provider, invisibly.\n483\t        #   * ``base_url`` empty, ``provider`` set -> the runtime provider\n484\t        #     system, which resolves the device's ``key_env`` correctly.\n485\t        #\n486\t        # The default backend lives on the second branch. Returning the\n487\t        # section's shape puts this backend on it too, so the device key is\n488\t        # the device key.\n489\t        route = {k: v for k, v in config.items() if k != \"service_tier\"}\n490\t        route[\"model\"] = model\n491\t        route[\"label\"] = label\n492\t\n493\t        # Reasoning mode. The capability is read from the device at route\n494\t        # time, so a model that gained or lost reasoning support since the\n495\t        # tier table was written is still handled correctly.\n496\t        try:\n497\t            overrides, achieved = thinking.resolve_thinking(\n498\t                model, requested_thinking, max_tokens_floor=self._thinking_floor(config)\n499\t            )\n500\t        except Exception:\n501\t            # A capability read must never take a routing decision down with\n502\t            # it. Fall back to the model's native behaviour.\n503\t            overrides, achieved = None, \"unknown\"\n504\t\n505\t        if overrides:\n506\t            route[\"request_overrides\"] = merge_request_overrides(\n507\t                route.get(\"request_overrides\"), overrides\n508\t            )\n509\t\n510\t        # The label carries the achieved state, not the requested one. A route\n511\t        # that asked for 'off' and got 'off→low' did not turn reasoning off,\n512\t        # and saying otherwise is how a token bill becomes a surprise.\n513\t        route[\"label\"] = f\"{label} [think:{achieved}]\"\n514\t\n515\t        return route\n516\t\n517\t    def _thinking_floor(self, config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> int:\n518\t        \"\"\"Return the output floor for a task that asks a model to reason.\n519\t\n520\t        A reasoning model spends its budget on the trace first. With a small\n521\t        budget the request comes back HTTP 200 with an empty answer and the\n522\t        budget gone — a silent failure that looks like the model produced\n523\t        nothing. So the floor is applied whenever a model's reasoning is in\n524\t        play.\n525\t\n526\t        The floor defaults to the budget children already run with. An\n527\t        operator who raises their own budget should raise this with it, or a\n528\t        reasoning child is quietly clamped back down. That is what\n529\t        ``delegation.backends.tiiny-router.thinking_max_tokens`` is for.\n530\t        \"\"\"\n531\t        floor = THINKING_MAX_TOKENS\n532\t        try:\n533\t            settings = self.settings(config)\n534\t        except Exception:\n535\t            settings = {}\n536\t        if isinstance(settings, Mapping):\n537\t            configured = settings.get(\"thinking_max_tokens\")\n538\t            if isinstance(configured, int) and not isinstance(configured, bool) and configured > 0:\n539\t                floor = configured\n540\t        return floor\n541\t\n542\t\n543\tdef _ensure_model(model: str) -> None:\n544\t    \"\"\"Ensure the chosen model is resident before the child runs.\n545\t\n546\t    Calls ``ctl.ensure_model()`` when the controller is loaded and\n547\t    ``ENSURE_ON_ROUTE`` is ``True``.  Idempotent — a resident model\n548\t    costs only one HTTP round-trip to confirm.  Exceptions are caught\n549\t    and silently ignored so a routing decision is never broken by a\n550\t    load failure.\n551\t    \"\"\"\n552\t    if not ENSURE_ON_ROUTE:\n553\t        return\n554\t    if ctl is None:\n555\t        return\n556\t    try:\n557\t        ctl.ensure_model(model, quiet=True)\n558\t    except Exception:\n559\t        # A load must never break routing.  If ensure_model raises,\n560\t        # the model may still be resident (http_load polls for up to\n561\t        # 180 s), or the device may be unreachable — both cases the\n562\t        # caller already handles via 404 / timeout.\n563\t        pass\n564\t\n565\t\n566\t# The skill ships in this directory, beside the backend. Registering it\n567\t# here makes Hermes resolve it as ``tiiny-router:tiiny-model-routing``,\n568\t# so no copy is written under ``~/.hermes/skills/``.\n569\tSKILL_NAME = \"tiiny-model-routing\"\n570\tSKILL_PATH = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"SKILL.md\"\n571\t\n572\t\n573\tdef register(ctx) -> None:\n574\t    ctx.register_delegation_provider(TiinyRouterDelegationProvider())\n575\t    ctx.register_skill(SKILL_NAME, SKILL_PATH)\n576\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b63ad6aa-1dd3-487c-8e9f-f16892a2390c","uuid":"7d986e9b-a424-4514-a0d6-644f3010052b","timestamp":"2026-11-22T01:18:03.450Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.install-workflow-fixes-04-router-plan-path-base-home/tiiny-router/__init__.py","content":"\"\"\"Tiiny routing delegation backend (``delegation.backend: tiiny-router``).\n\nRoutes each ``delegate_task`` subtask to the Tiiny model that suits its shape,\ninstead of sending every subtask to one pinned model.\n\nWhy a backend rather than a config value\n----------------------------------------\n``delegation.model`` pins ONE model for every child. That is the right default\nwhen every subtask is the same kind of work. It is the wrong default when a\nsingle delegation batch mixes a web-research sweep, a filesystem index, a\nterminal code edit, and a browser-automation run: the cheap subtasks pay for\nthe expensive model's context, and the expensive subtask gets a model that is\nnot specialised for it.\n\nA backend solves that at the routing layer. ``route_task`` is called once per\ntask with that task's goal and context, and returns the model, provider, and\nrequest overrides for that subtask alone.\n\nRouting is by task shape, not by keyword\n-----------------------------------------\nThe route is chosen from the task's *shape* — what kind of work it is — because\nshape is what predicts which model does well. A goal that mentions \"index the\nvault\" is an indexing job; a goal that says \"refactor these three modules\" is\na coding job. Matching on keywords in the prompt is brittle in a different way:\na research task about code will match code keywords and get the coder.\n\nThe shape is read from the goal text, with the explicit ``tier`` parameter as\nan override. When neither gives a confident answer, the task goes to the\ngeneralist, which is the right default: it is the model that is good at\neverything in the catalogue and mediocre at each, and being wrong about a\nspecialist is more expensive than being mediocre at a general task.\n\nThinking mode is a per-task parameter\n--------------------------------------\nThe ``thinking`` parameter (``\"auto\" | \"on\" | \"off\"``) controls whether the\nmodel reasons before it answers. Default is ``\"auto\"`` — the model's native\nbehaviour, untouched.\n\n``\"off\"`` is for mechanical work: indexing, formatting, classification. A\nreasoning model spends its budget on a trace before the answer, and on a small\nbudget that trace can eat the whole budget and return an empty response.\n\n``\"on\"`` is for genuinely novel reasoning, where the model's work is worth\nseeing.\n\nWhat the request asks for and what the model does are not the same thing, and\nthe route reports the difference. Some models have a working on/off switch;\nsome have no switch and can only be moved between named reasoning levels;\nsome do not reason at all. So the router reads the device's own capability\nfor the model it picked, applies the control that model actually honours, and\nputs the **achieved** state in the route label — ``off`` when the switch\nworked, ``off→low`` when the model could only be turned down to its cheapest\nlevel, ``unsupported`` when the model does not reason, ``unknown`` when the\ndevice would not say.\n\nSee ``thinking.py`` for how the capability is read and which control each\nkind of model honours.\n\nThe router chooses; the lifecycle loads\n---------------------------------------\n**This plugin never loads, unloads, or otherwise mutates the device.** That is\nnot caution, it is a division of labour: the device's resident set is a\n*shared, finite* resource, and a load is a swap that evicts whatever was\nresident.\n\nThe loading half lives in ``scripts/tiiny_ctl.py``, which the manager agent\ndrives explicitly: plan the batch, take the cross-agent lock, load what the\nbatch needs, delegate, then release. Between those two calls the router and\nthe controller agree on one file — the **plan** (``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``).\n\nThe plan is what makes the two halves agree. When the controller has loaded a\nspecialist for a batch, the plan records which model it assigned to which\ngoal, and this plugin routes that goal to that model. Without the plan the\nrouter falls back to the residency-only rule below, which is safe but cannot\nuse a specialist that is not loaded yet.\n\nHowever, when **no plan is used** (a single task, or ad-hoc delegation), the\nrouter calls ``ctl.ensure_model()`` automatically after choosing the model.\nThis puts the specialist on the device before the child runs, without an extra\nmanual step. The call is idempotent — when the model is already resident, it\ncosts one HTTP round-trip.\n\nThe two halves therefore compose like this:\n\n    controller: plan -> reserve -> load      |  plugin: route_task + ensure\n    controller: release                      |  child runs on the plan's model\n\nResidency is read, never changed\n--------------------------------\nThe plugin does read the device's resident set, and it uses it. A model that\nis not resident cannot answer — the device replies ``404 \"is not loaded\"``,\nwhich Hermes classifies as terminal and falls back to the paid provider. On a\ndevice whose shared NPU pool holds only a few models at a time, that turns a\nrouting decision into a silent move off the local device.\n\nSo the router picks a model that is actually resident when it can: the plan's\nassignment if there is one, then the tier's own first choice, then another\ncandidate from the same tier, then the generalist the watchdog keeps resident.\nThe label on the route records any substitution (``code→generalist``), so the\noperator can see that the specialist was not available. When residency cannot\nbe determined at all the router behaves exactly as it did before: the tier's\nfirst choice.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport importlib.util\nimport json\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport urllib.request\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, Mapping\n\nfrom agent.delegation_provider import DelegationProvider, DelegationRouteError\n\n#: The shared tier table. Loaded by path — see the note in ``tiers.py`` for\n#: why a relative import would break under the Hermes plugin loader.\n_TIERS_PATH = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"tiers.py\"\n_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"tiiny_router_tiers\", _TIERS_PATH)\nif _spec is None or _spec.loader is None:  # pragma: no cover - packaging error\n    raise ImportError(f\"could not load the tier table from {_TIERS_PATH}\")\ntiers = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_spec)\n_spec.loader.exec_module(tiers)\n\n#: The reasoning-mode control. Also loaded by path, and deliberately free of\n#: Hermes imports so the standalone checker can use it too.\n_THINKING_PATH = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"thinking.py\"\n_thinking_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"tiiny_router_thinking\", _THINKING_PATH)\nif _thinking_spec is None or _thinking_spec.loader is None:  # pragma: no cover\n    raise ImportError(f\"could not load the thinking control from {_THINKING_PATH}\")\nthinking = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_thinking_spec)\n_thinking_spec.loader.exec_module(thinking)\n\nTIER_CHOICES = tiers.TIER_CHOICES\nTIER_MODEL = tiers.TIER_MODEL\nVALID_TIERS = tiers.VALID_TIERS\nGENERALIST = tiers.GENERALIST\nSHAPE_SIGNALS = tiers.SHAPE_SIGNALS\ndetect_tier = tiers.detect_tier\n\n#: The device's OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Local, on the LAN.\nTIINY_BASE_URL = tiers.TIINY_BASE_URL\n\n#: Provider name as configured in ``providers:`` in config.yaml.\nTIINY_PROVIDER = tiers.TIINY_PROVIDER\n\n#: The thinking modes a task may ask for.\nTHINKING_MODES = (\"auto\", \"on\", \"off\")\n\n#: The default output floor for a task that asks a model to reason.\nTHINKING_MAX_TOKENS = thinking.THINKING_MAX_TOKENS\n\n#: How long a residency lookup is reused, in seconds. The device's resident\n#: set changes on a swap, not on a heartbeat, so a short cache is enough and\n#: keeps the routing path cheap.\nRESIDENCY_TTL = 30\n\n#: Cached residency lookup: (monotonic timestamp, frozenset of model ids).\n_residency_cache: tuple[float, frozenset[str]] | None = None\n\n#: The plan the lifecycle controller writes. It lives in the BASE Hermes home\n#: on purpose: every profile's manager agent must see the same plan, and the\n#: base home is what they share.\nPLAN_FILE = Path(\n    os.environ.get(\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\", \"~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json\")\n).expanduser()\n\n#: Whether the router calls ``ensure`` on every route.  When ``True`` the\n#: router silently loads any model that is not yet resident (idempotent, so\n#: a resident model costs only an HTTP round-trip).  When ``False`` the\n#: router behaves as before — picking what is resident, falling back on\n#: what is not, with no loading.\n#:\n#: This is the config toggle for Stage 3.  The default is ``True`` — the\n#: ``ensure`` call makes the device ready for the child without an extra\n#: manual step.\nENSURE_ON_ROUTE = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_ENSURE_ON_ROUTE\", \"true\").lower() != \"false\"\n\n#: The controller module. Loaded by path (see ``tiers.py`` for why a\n#: relative import would break under the Hermes plugin loader). It sits in\n#: ``scripts/`` inside this plugin directory.\n_CTL_SPEC = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n    \"tiiny_controller\",\n    Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"scripts\" / \"tiiny_ctl.py\",\n)\nif _CTL_SPEC is None or _CTL_SPEC.loader is None:\n    ctl = None\nelse:\n    ctl = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_CTL_SPEC)\n    try:\n        _CTL_SPEC.loader.exec_module(ctl)\n    except Exception:\n        ctl = None\n\n\ndef device_key() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the device API key, or an empty string if it cannot be found.\n\n    Hermes keeps its secrets in ``~/.hermes/.env`` and resolves them through\n    its own accessor — they are **not** in the process environment. Reading\n    ``os.environ`` alone therefore finds nothing, and the router would\n    silently fail open on every call, which is exactly the failure this\n    lookup exists to prevent. Read it the way Hermes reads it, and fall back\n    to the environment for a shell that exported it.\n    \"\"\"\n    value = \"\"\n    try:\n        from hermes_cli.config import get_env_value_prefer_dotenv\n\n        value = get_env_value_prefer_dotenv(\"TIINY_API_KEY\") or \"\"\n    except Exception:\n        value = \"\"\n    return (value or os.environ.get(\"TIINY_API_KEY\", \"\") or \"\").strip()\n\n\ndef resident_models() -> frozenset[str] | None:\n    \"\"\"Return the model ids currently resident on the device.\n\n    Returns ``None`` when residency cannot be determined — the device is\n    offline, the key is missing, or the lookup timed out. The caller then\n    falls back to the tier's default choice, which is exactly the behaviour\n    from before residency was considered. This path must never raise: a\n    routing decision is not a place to fail.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _residency_cache\n    now = time.monotonic()\n    if _residency_cache is not None and now - _residency_cache[0] < RESIDENCY_TTL:\n        return _residency_cache[1]\n\n    key = device_key()\n    if not key:\n        return None\n    try:\n        # This must be the SAME box the request will go to. The provider\n        # is pinned to one appliance by address; asking a forwarder or a\n        # mDNS hostname instead reads a DIFFERENT device's resident set,\n        # and the router then picks a model the target cannot serve.\n        request = urllib.request.Request(\n            TIINY_BASE_URL + \"/models\",\n            headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + key},\n        )\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=3) as response:\n            payload = json.load(response)\n    except Exception:\n        return None\n\n    ids: set[str] = set()\n    for entry in payload.get(\"data\") or []:\n        if not isinstance(entry, dict):\n            continue\n        value = entry.get(\"id\")\n        if isinstance(value, str) and \"/\" in value:\n            ids.add(value)\n    resident = frozenset(ids)\n    _residency_cache = (now, resident)\n    return resident\n\n\ndef _normalise_goal(goal: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Collapse whitespace so a goal survives a copy-paste with different spacing.\"\"\"\n    return \" \".join((goal or \"\").split())\n\n\ndef planned_models() -> dict[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"Return the plan's ``goal -> model`` assignments, or an empty mapping.\n\n    The plan is written by ``scripts/tiiny_ctl.py plan`` and lives at\n    ``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``. A missing, unreadable or malformed plan is\n    not an error: it simply means the lifecycle did not run, and the router\n    falls back to the residency-only rule. Routing must never fail because a\n    sidecar file is wrong.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        payload = json.loads(PLAN_FILE.read_text())\n    except Exception:\n        return {}\n    entries = payload.get(\"entries\")\n    if not isinstance(entries, list):\n        return {}\n    planned: dict[str, str] = {}\n    for entry in entries:\n        if not isinstance(entry, dict):\n            continue\n        goal = entry.get(\"goal\")\n        model = entry.get(\"model\")\n        if isinstance(goal, str) and isinstance(model, str) and goal and model:\n            planned[_normalise_goal(goal)] = model\n    return planned\n\n\ndef choose_model(tier: str, goal: str = \"\") -> tuple[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"Pick the model for a task and say how it was picked.\n\n    Returns ``(model, label)``.\n\n    The order is: the plan's assignment for this goal, if there is one and\n    its model is resident; then the tier's own first choice if it is\n    resident; then any other candidate in the tier that is resident; then the\n    generalist, if it is resident; then the tier's first choice regardless.\n\n    The point of the later steps is that a model which is not resident cannot\n    answer — the device answers ``404 \"is not loaded\"``, which Hermes treats as\n    terminal and falls back to the paid provider. On a device whose NPU pool\n    holds only a few models at a time, routing to a specialist that is not\n    loaded would quietly move the work off the device. Preferring a resident\n    model keeps the work local, and the label records the substitution so the\n    operator can see it happened.\n    \"\"\"\n    choices = TIER_CHOICES[tier]\n    default_model = choices[0][0]\n\n    resident = resident_models()\n    plan = planned_models()\n    planned = plan.get(_normalise_goal(goal)) if goal else None\n\n    # The plan wins, but only if what it names is actually there. A plan is a\n    # promise about the device's state, and the device is the authority: if\n    # the planned model is gone, something evicted it and the plan is stale.\n    if planned and (resident is None or planned in resident):\n        if planned == default_model:\n            return planned, tier\n        return planned, f\"{tier}→{planned.split('/')[-1]}\"\n\n    if resident is None:\n        # Residency unknown - the lookup timed out, the key was missing,\n        # or the device answered with something unreadable.\n        #\n        # The generalist is the device's own default model and the one it\n        # serves, so it is the safest thing to ask for. Returning the\n        # tier's first choice here is what made a lookup failure turn\n        # into \"not a valid model ID\" on the device.\n        return GENERALIST, f\"{tier}->generalist\"\n\n    for model, _npu, _ctx in choices:\n        if model in resident:\n            if model == default_model:\n                return model, tier\n            return model, f\"{tier}→{model.split('/')[-1]}\"\n\n    if GENERALIST in resident:\n        return GENERALIST, f\"{tier}→generalist\"\n\n    return default_model, tier\n\n\ndef merge_request_overrides(\n    base: Mapping[str, Any] | None,\n    additions: Mapping[str, Any],\n) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Merge new request overrides over the ones the route already carries.\n\n    ``extra_body`` is merged one level deep, the way Hermes merges the\n    delegation section's own overrides over the provider's. Everything else is\n    replaced wholesale, because ``reasoning_effort`` and ``max_tokens`` are\n    single values and a half-applied one is worse than either.\n\n    The base matters: the delegation section may already carry an\n    ``extra_body`` of its own, and dropping it would silently change the\n    request for every other key in it.\n    \"\"\"\n    merged: dict[str, Any] = dict(base or {})\n    base_extra = merged.get(\"extra_body\")\n    base_extra = dict(base_extra) if isinstance(base_extra, Mapping) else {}\n\n    extra = additions.get(\"extra_body\")\n    extra = dict(extra) if isinstance(extra, Mapping) else {}\n\n    if base_extra and extra:\n        for key, value in extra.items():\n            if isinstance(value, Mapping) and isinstance(base_extra.get(key), Mapping):\n                nested = dict(base_extra[key])\n                nested.update(value)\n                base_extra[key] = nested\n            else:\n                base_extra[key] = value\n        extra = base_extra\n    elif base_extra:\n        extra = base_extra\n\n    for key, value in additions.items():\n        if key != \"extra_body\":\n            merged[key] = value\n    merged[\"extra_body\"] = extra\n    return merged\n\n\nclass TiinyRouterDelegationProvider(DelegationProvider):\n    \"\"\"Route each subtask to the Tiiny model that fits its shape.\"\"\"\n\n    @property\n    def name(self) -> str:\n        return \"tiiny-router\"\n\n    @property\n    def display_name(self) -> str:\n        return \"Tiiny Router\"\n\n    @property\n    def summary(self) -> str:\n        return (\"Route each subtask to the Tiiny model that matches its shape: \"\n                \"research, index, code, browser, draft, general.\")\n\n    def task_parameters(self, config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> dict[str, dict[str, Any]]:\n        return {\n            \"tier\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"enum\": list(VALID_TIERS),\n                \"description\": (\n                    \"Force a routing tier for this task instead of inferring it from the goal. \"\n                    \"Use when the goal understates the work: a task that says 'read these logs' \"\n                    \"but means 'summarise what went wrong' is a 'draft' task, not a 'general' one.\"\n                ),\n            },\n            \"thinking\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"enum\": list(THINKING_MODES),\n                \"description\": (\n                    \"Reasoning mode for this task. 'auto' (default) leaves the model's native \"\n                    \"behaviour alone. 'off' turns reasoning off for mechanical work - indexing, \"\n                    \"formatting, classification - so the token budget is spent on the answer \"\n                    \"rather than on a trace nobody asked for. 'on' turns reasoning on for novel \"\n                    \"problems where the model's work is worth seeing. Where a model has no \"\n                    \"on/off switch, 'off' means its cheapest reasoning level rather than a \"\n                    \"refusal, and the route label reports what was actually achieved.\"\n                ),\n            },\n        }\n\n    def description(self, config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> str:\n        return (\n            \"Set 'tier' on a task to force a model: \" + \", \".join(VALID_TIERS) + \". \"\n            \"When tier is omitted the model is inferred from the task's shape, so name the \"\n            \"work honestly ('index every file under X') and the cheap specialist gets it. \"\n            \"Set 'thinking' to 'off', 'on', or 'auto' (default) to control reasoning per task: \"\n            \"'off' for mechanical work, 'on' for novel reasoning. The route label reports the \"\n            \"state the model actually reached, which is not always the one that was asked for.\"\n        )\n\n    def route_task(self, task: Mapping[str, Any], config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n        goal = str(task.get(\"goal\") or \"\")\n        forced = task.get(\"tier\")\n        requested_thinking = str(task.get(\"thinking\") or \"auto\").strip().lower()\n\n        if requested_thinking not in THINKING_MODES:\n            raise DelegationRouteError(\n                f\"Unknown thinking mode {requested_thinking!r}. \"\n                f\"Valid modes are: {', '.join(THINKING_MODES)}.\"\n            )\n\n        if forced:\n            tier = str(forced)\n            if tier not in TIER_MODEL:\n                raise DelegationRouteError(\n                    f\"Unknown tier {tier!r}. Valid tiers are: {', '.join(VALID_TIERS)}.\"\n                )\n        else:\n            tier = detect_tier(goal)\n\n        model, label = choose_model(tier, goal)\n\n        # Ensure the chosen model is resident.  Idempotent — when the model\n        # is already running this is one HTTP round-trip; when it is not,\n        # ``ensure_model`` loads it and verifies the load before returning.\n        # Exceptions are caught inside the helper so a load failure cannot\n        # break the routing decision — the device will return 404 and Hermes\n        # will fall back to the paid provider as expected.\n        _ensure_model(model)\n\n        # Return the delegation section's own shape and override only the\n        # model — the way the default backend returns ``dict(config)``.\n        #\n        # This is not cosmetic. Hermes resolves a route's credentials from\n        # ``base_url`` / ``api_key`` / ``provider``:\n        #\n        #   * ``base_url`` set, ``api_key`` empty -> a direct endpoint, and\n        #     ``api_key`` None means INHERIT THE PARENT'S KEY. The parent runs\n        #     on the paid provider, so the device would receive the wrong\n        #     bearer and answer ``401 auth_failed`` — and the child would fall\n        #     back to the paid provider, invisibly.\n        #   * ``base_url`` empty, ``provider`` set -> the runtime provider\n        #     system, which resolves the device's ``key_env`` correctly.\n        #\n        # The default backend lives on the second branch. Returning the\n        # section's shape puts this backend on it too, so the device key is\n        # the device key.\n        route = {k: v for k, v in config.items() if k != \"service_tier\"}\n        route[\"model\"] = model\n        route[\"label\"] = label\n\n        # Reasoning mode. The capability is read from the device at route\n        # time, so a model that gained or lost reasoning support since the\n        # tier table was written is still handled correctly.\n        try:\n            overrides, achieved = thinking.resolve_thinking(\n                model, requested_thinking, max_tokens_floor=self._thinking_floor(config)\n            )\n        except Exception:\n            # A capability read must never take a routing decision down with\n            # it. Fall back to the model's native behaviour.\n            overrides, achieved = None, \"unknown\"\n\n        if overrides:\n            route[\"request_overrides\"] = merge_request_overrides(\n                route.get(\"request_overrides\"), overrides\n            )\n\n        # The label carries the achieved state, not the requested one. A route\n        # that asked for 'off' and got 'off→low' did not turn reasoning off,\n        # and saying otherwise is how a token bill becomes a surprise.\n        route[\"label\"] = f\"{label} [think:{achieved}]\"\n\n        return route\n\n    def _thinking_floor(self, config: Mapping[str, Any]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Return the output floor for a task that asks a model to reason.\n\n        A reasoning model spends its budget on the trace first. With a small\n        budget the request comes back HTTP 200 with an empty answer and the\n        budget gone — a silent failure that looks like the model produced\n        nothing. So the floor is applied whenever a model's reasoning is in\n        play.\n\n        The floor defaults to the budget children already run with. An\n        operator who raises their own budget should raise this with it, or a\n        reasoning child is quietly clamped back down. That is what\n        ``delegation.backends.tiiny-router.thinking_max_tokens`` is for.\n        \"\"\"\n        floor = THINKING_MAX_TOKENS\n        try:\n            settings = self.settings(config)\n        except Exception:\n            settings = {}\n        if isinstance(settings, Mapping):\n            configured = settings.get(\"thinking_max_tokens\")\n            if isinstance(configured, int) and not isinstance(configured, bool) and configured > 0:\n                floor = configured\n        return floor\n\n\ndef _ensure_model(model: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Ensure the chosen model is resident before the child runs.\n\n    Calls ``ctl.ensure_model()`` when the controller is loaded and\n    ``ENSURE_ON_ROUTE`` is ``True``.  Idempotent — a resident model\n    costs only one HTTP round-trip to confirm.  Exceptions are caught\n    and silently ignored so a routing decision is never broken by a\n    load failure.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not ENSURE_ON_ROUTE:\n        return\n    if ctl is None:\n        return\n    try:\n        ctl.ensure_model(model, quiet=True)\n    except Exception:\n        # A load must never break routing.  If ensure_model raises,\n        # the model may still be resident (http_load polls for up to\n        # 180 s), or the device may be unreachable — both cases the\n        # caller already handles via 404 / timeout.\n        pass\n\n\n# The skill ships in this directory, beside the backend. Registering it\n# here makes Hermes resolve it as ``tiiny-router:tiiny-model-routing``,\n# so no copy is written under ``~/.hermes/skills/``.\nSKILL_NAME = \"tiiny-model-routing\"\nSKILL_PATH = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"SKILL.md\"\n\n\ndef register(ctx) -> None:\n    ctx.register_delegation_provider(TiinyRouterDelegationProvider())\n    ctx.register_skill(SKILL_NAME, SKILL_PATH)\n","numLines":576,"startLine":1,"totalLines":576}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Gn8VzgQEiHcpZ8NEBt5dwq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012NB5Eqzt1oM7FPVPj2Fk9k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"tiiny_ctl.py — the device controller for the Tiiny model-routing skill.\n3\t\n4\tThe routing plugin chooses a model per subtask. This script owns everything\n5\taround that choice: the plan, the cross-agent lock, the load, the restore, and\n6\tthe catalogue refresh.\n7\t\n8\tWhy the split\n9\t-------------\n10\tThe delegation plugin has exactly one hook, ``route_task``, and it is called\n11\tonce per subtask *before* any child runs. There is no \"batch finished\" hook,\n12\tso a plugin cannot know when it is safe to evict a model, and it cannot know\n13\twhether a sibling agent is already using the device. Loading therefore has to\n14\tlive outside the plugin, in a step the manager agent drives explicitly and can\n15\tsee:\n16\t\n17\t    plan  ->  reserve  ->  load  ->  delegate  ->  release\n18\t\n19\tBetween ``plan`` and ``load`` the plan file (``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``) is\n20\twhat the two halves share: it records which model was assigned to which goal,\n21\tso the plugin routes the child to the model this script just loaded.\n22\t\n23\tThe lock\n24\t--------\n25\tTiiny is one shared box. Two agents delegating at the same time will fight\n26\tover the same resident models and the same NPU pool. The lock makes that\n27\tvisible instead of silent:\n28\t\n29\t  * ``~/.hermes/tiiny-lock.json`` is the mutex. It is taken with\n30\t    ``flock`` over a file created with ``O_EXCL`` semantics, so two agents\n31\t    cannot both believe they hold it. It expires, so a crashed agent does not\n32\t    lock the device forever.\n33\t  * The vault's ``hot.md`` carries the announcement — the AMC's own board of\n34\t    what is in flight — so agents that are not Hermes profiles can see that\n35\t    the device is spoken for.\n36\t\n37\tThe sandbox this often runs in cannot unlink or rename files. That is why the\n38\tlock is *released by writing a released state into it* rather than by deleting\n39\tit, and why the hold file is truncated rather than removed.\n40\t\n41\tCommands\n42\t--------\n43\t    status                      device, resident set, lock, hold, plan\n44\t    plan --goal G [--goal G]    pick models, check the NPU fit, write the plan\n45\t    reserve --owner NAME        take the lock and announce it\n46\t    load [--model M] [--wave N] load what the batch needs, verify residency\n47\t    release [--force]           restore the device, clear the lock and the plan\n48\t    catalog [--refresh]         diff the live Model Store against the snapshot\n49\t    image \"a job description\"   pick the right image model for the job\n50\t\n51\tExit codes: 0 success, 1 the operation failed, 2 the usage was wrong.\n52\t\"\"\"\n53\t\n54\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n55\t\n56\timport argparse\n57\timport fcntl\n58\timport json\n59\timport os\n60\timport re\n61\timport subprocess\n62\timport sys\n63\timport time\n64\timport urllib.error\n65\timport urllib.request\n66\tfrom pathlib import Path\n67\t\n68\t# --- paths ------------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\n70\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n71\t#: The plugin directory: the routing backend, the tier table and the skill.\n72\tSKILL_ROOT = HERE.parent\n73\tHERMES_HOME = Path(os.environ.get(\"HERMES_HOME\", \"~/.hermes\")).expanduser()\n74\t\n75\ttry:\n76\t    import devices as D\n77\texcept Exception:\n78\t    try:\n79\t        import importlib.util\n80\t        for cand in (HERE / \"devices.py\", HERMES_HOME / \"mesh\" / \"devices.py\"):\n81\t            if cand.exists():\n82\t                spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"devices\", cand)\n83\t                if spec and spec.loader:\n84\t                    D = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n85\t                    spec.loader.exec_module(D)\n86\t                    break\n87\t        else:\n88\t            D = None\n89\t    except Exception:\n90\t        D = None\n91\t\n92\t\n93\tdef _shared_home() -> Path:\n94\t    \"\"\"The BASE Hermes home, even when HERMES_HOME points at a profile.\n95\t\n96\t    Four files are shared state across every profile: the plan, the\n97\t    cross-agent lock, the hold file, and the catalogue snapshot.\n98\t\n99\t    Two readers live in the base home and will not look anywhere else:\n100\t\n101\t    - the delegation plugin reads the plan from ``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``\n102\t      on purpose — \"every profile's manager agent must see the same plan\";\n103\t    - every profile writes the hold to ``~/.hermes/tiiny-hold``, so one\n104\t      agent's record of what it loaded is visible to the next.\n105\t\n106\t    When HERMES_HOME is a profile directory (``.../hermes/profiles/<name>``)\n107\t    a naive ``HERMES_HOME / \"tiiny-hold\"`` writes the hold somewhere the other\n108\t    agents never read, so two agents each believe they hold the device. The\n109\t    shared files have to be resolved against the base home, not the profile.\n110\t    \"\"\"\n111\t    home = HERMES_HOME\n112\t    if home.name != \"profiles\" and home.parent.name == \"profiles\":\n113\t        return home.parent.parent\n114\t    return home\n115\t\n116\t\n117\tdef _dotenv_value(name: str) -> str:\n118\t    \"\"\"Read one value out of the BASE Hermes home's ``.env``.\n119\t\n120\t    The base home, not the profile's. The profile directory is where\n121\t    HERMES_HOME points when a profile runs, and a profile's own .env\n122\t    does not carry the shared settings - the appliance bearer and the\n123\t    vault path live in ``~/.hermes/.env``, where every profile reads\n124\t    them from.\n125\t\n126\t    Reading the profile's .env here is why the lock announcement was\n127\t    silent: ``TIINY_VAULT`` is set in the base home, so the controller\n128\t    never found the vault, never wrote the \"device is BUSY\" notice into\n129\t    hot.md, and every other agent read a board that said the device was\n130\t    free while it was held.\n131\t\n132\t    Nothing is printed but the one value asked for, and it is never\n133\t    logged.\n134\t    \"\"\"\n135\t    try:\n136\t        for line in (_shared_home() / \".env\").read_text().splitlines():\n137\t            line = line.strip()\n138\t            if line.startswith(name + \"=\"):\n139\t                return line.split(\"=\", 1)[1].strip().strip(\"'\\\"\")\n140\t    except Exception:\n141\t        pass\n142\t    return \"\"\n143\t\n144\t\n145\tdef _caller_owner() -> str:\n146\t    \"\"\"Who is calling.\n147\t\n148\t    The lock is between AGENTS, not between processes: two Hermes profiles on\n149\t    one machine are two agents sharing one appliance. TIINY_OWNER wins;\n150\t    otherwise the active profile's name, because that is what identifies the\n151\t    agent. Falls back to the OS user.\n152\t\n153\t    An empty string means \"unknown\", and unknown callers are treated as\n154\t    NOT the owner of anything — an unidentified agent must not be able to\n155\t    take a device another agent is holding.\n156\t    \"\"\"\n157\t    env = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\").strip()\n158\t    if env:\n159\t        return env\n160\t    home = HERMES_HOME\n161\t    if home.name != \"profiles\" and home.parent.name == \"profiles\":\n162\t        return home.name\n163\t    return os.environ.get(\"USER\", \"\").strip()\n164\t\n165\t\n166\tdef _lock_holder() -> dict | None:\n167\t    \"\"\"The lock payload while it is held by someone, or None.\n168\t\n169\t    \"Held\" means: state is held AND it has not expired. An expired lock is\n170\t    free for the taking — that is how a crashed agent does not hold the\n171\t    device forever.\n172\t    \"\"\"\n173\t    current = lock_read() or {}\n174\t    if current.get(\"state\") != \"held\":\n175\t        return None\n176\t    try:\n177\t        expires = float(current.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n178\t    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n179\t        expires = 0\n180\t    if expires <= time.time():\n181\t        return None\n182\t    return current\n183\t\n184\t\n185\tdef _held_by_other(owner: str, holder: dict | None) -> bool:\n186\t    \"\"\"True when the device is held by an agent that is not ``owner``.\n187\t\n188\t    An empty ``owner`` is \"unknown caller\" and never matches the holder, so\n189\t    an agent that cannot identify itself cannot disturb another agent's\n190\t    device.\n191\t    \"\"\"\n192\t    if not holder:\n193\t        return False\n194\t    held_by = holder.get(\"owner\") or \"\"\n195\t    return held_by != owner\n196\t\n197\t\n198\t\n199\tdef _find_vault() -> Path | None:\n200\t    \"\"\"Find the vault root — the directory that holds ``hot.md``.\n201\t\n202\t    The controller runs from two places: the vault's skill directory (the\n203\t    master copy) and an installed copy under ``~/.hermes/scripts``. In the\n204\t    first case the vault is two levels up; in the second it is nowhere near.\n205\t    So look for the marker instead of assuming a depth: the vault root is the\n206\t    directory that has both ``hot.md`` and ``skills/`` in it.\n207\t\n208\t    Three ways to find it, in order: the ``TIINY_VAULT`` environment\n209\t    variable, the same name in ``~/.hermes/.env``, and a walk up from this\n210\t    file. The walk is what finds it for the master copy; the other two are\n211\t    what an installed copy uses.\n212\t\n213\t    No machine path is hard-coded here. This file is published, and a\n214\t    published file must not carry the path of the machine it was written on.\n215\t    \"\"\"\n216\t    override = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_VAULT\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_VAULT\")\n217\t    if override:\n218\t        candidate = Path(override).expanduser()\n219\t        return candidate if candidate.exists() else None\n220\t    for base in (SKILL_ROOT, HERE):\n221\t        for parent in (base, *base.parents):\n222\t            try:\n223\t                if (parent / \"hot.md\").exists() and (parent / \"skills\").exists():\n224\t                    return parent\n225\t            except OSError:\n226\t                continue\n227\t    return None\n228\t\n229\t\n230\tVAULT = _find_vault()\n231\t\n232\t#: Shared state. These live in the BASE Hermes home, not the profile — see\n233\t#: `_shared_home` for why, and for the failure that follows if they do not.\n234\tSHARED_HOME = _shared_home()\n235\t\n236\t\n237\tdef _address_from(url: str) -> str:\n238\t    \"\"\"The host part of a base URL as a bare address.\n239\t\n240\t    ``http://192.0.2.51/v1`` -> ``192.0.2.51``. A value with no scheme\n241\t    is returned as its first path segment, so ``tiinyhost.local`` survives\n242\t    intact.\n243\t    \"\"\"\n244\t    text = url.strip()\n245\t    if \"://\" in text:\n246\t        text = text.split(\"://\", 1)[1]\n247\t    return text.split(\"/\")[0].split(\":\")[0]\n248\t\n249\t\n250\t_ACTIVE_DEVICE: Any | None = None\n251\t\n252\t\n253\tdef set_active_device(target: str | None = None) -> Any | None:\n254\t    \"\"\"Set and return the active device from the registry.\"\"\"\n255\t    global _ACTIVE_DEVICE, _SESSION_CHECKED\n256\t    _SESSION_CHECKED = None\n257\t    if D is None:\n258\t        _ACTIVE_DEVICE = None\n259\t        return None\n260\t    if target:\n261\t        dev = D.get_device(target)\n262\t        if dev:\n263\t            _ACTIVE_DEVICE = dev\n264\t            return dev\n265\t        reg = D.load_registry()\n266\t        for d in reg.values():\n267\t            if d.address == target:\n268\t                _ACTIVE_DEVICE = d\n269\t                return d\n270\t    env_target = (\n271\t        os.environ.get(\"TIINY_DEVICE_NAME\")\n272\t        or os.environ.get(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\")\n273\t        or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_DEVICE_NAME\")\n274\t        or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\")\n275\t    )\n276\t    if env_target:\n277\t        dev = D.get_device(env_target)\n278\t        if dev:\n279\t            _ACTIVE_DEVICE = dev\n280\t            return dev\n281\t    host_env = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOST\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_HOST\")\n282\t    if host_env:\n283\t        host = _address_from(host_env)\n284\t        if host and host != \"tiinyhost.local\":\n285\t            dev = D.get_device(host)\n286\t            if dev:\n287\t                _ACTIVE_DEVICE = dev\n288\t                return dev\n289\t    reg = D.load_registry()\n290\t    if \"tiiny-ant\" in reg:\n291\t        _ACTIVE_DEVICE = reg[\"tiiny-ant\"]\n292\t        return _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n293\t    elif reg:\n294\t        _ACTIVE_DEVICE = next(iter(reg.values()))\n295\t        return _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n296\t    _ACTIVE_DEVICE = None\n297\t    return None\n298\t\n299\t\n300\tdef active_device() -> Any | None:\n301\t    global _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n302\t    if _ACTIVE_DEVICE is None:\n303\t        set_active_device()\n304\t    return _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n305\t\n306\t\n307\tdef lock_file() -> Path:\n308\t    if os.environ.get(\"TIINY_LOCK_FILE\"):\n309\t        return Path(os.environ[\"TIINY_LOCK_FILE\"]).expanduser()\n310\t    dev = active_device()\n311\t    if dev:\n312\t        return SHARED_HOME / f\"tiiny-lock-{dev.name}.json\"\n313\t    return SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-lock.json\"\n314\t\n315\t\n316\tdef plan_file() -> Path:\n317\t    if os.environ.get(\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\"):\n318\t        return Path(os.environ[\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\"]).expanduser()\n319\t    dev = active_device()\n320\t    if dev:\n321\t        return SHARED_HOME / f\"tiiny-plan-{dev.name}.json\"\n322\t    return SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\"\n323\t\n324\t\n325\tdef hold_file() -> Path:\n326\t    if os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOLD_FILE\"):\n327\t        return Path(os.environ[\"TIINY_HOLD_FILE\"]).expanduser()\n328\t    dev = active_device()\n329\t    if dev:\n330\t        return SHARED_HOME / f\"tiiny-hold-{dev.name}\"\n331\t    return SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-hold\"\n332\t\n333\t\n334\tLOCK_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_LOCK_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-lock.json\")))\n335\tPLAN_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\")))\n336\tHOLD_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOLD_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-hold\")))\n337\tSNAPSHOT_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_SNAPSHOT_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-catalogue.json\")))\n338\t\n339\tTIINY_BIN = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_BIN\", \"~/.local/tiiny/bin/tiiny\")).expanduser()\n340\t\n341\t\n342\tdef inference_base() -> str:\n343\t    dev = active_device()\n344\t    if dev:\n345\t        return dev.base_url()\n346\t    return (os.environ.get(\"TIINY_BASE_URL\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_BASE_URL\")\n347\t            or \"http://openai.api.tiiny/v1\").rstrip(\"/\")\n348\t\n349\t\n350\tdef management_base() -> str:\n351\t    dev = active_device()\n352\t    if dev:\n353\t        return dev.admin_url()\n354\t    return (os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOST\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_HOST\")\n355\t            or \"http://tiinyhost.local\").rstrip(\"/\")\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tINFERENCE_BASE = inference_base()\n359\tMANAGEMENT_BASE = management_base()\n360\t\n361\t\n362\tdef _cli_config_device() -> str:\n363\t    \"\"\"The device the control CLI is currently pointed at.\"\"\"\n364\t    try:\n365\t        import json\n366\t        path = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.local/tiiny/config.json\")\n367\t        with open(path) as fh:\n368\t            return str(json.load(fh).get(\"deviceAddress\") or \"\")\n369\t    except Exception:\n370\t        return \"\"\n371\t\n372\t\n373\tdef cli_config_path() -> Path | None:\n374\t    dev = active_device()\n375\t    if dev:\n376\t        p = dev.cli_config_path()\n377\t        if p.exists():\n378\t            return p\n379\t    return None\n380\t\n381\t\n382\tdef device_address() -> str:\n383\t    \"\"\"The address every control call has to name.\"\"\"\n384\t    dev = active_device()\n385\t    if dev:\n386\t        return dev.address\n387\t    explicit = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\")\n388\t    if explicit:\n389\t        return explicit.strip()\n390\t    host = _address_from(management_base())\n391\t    if host and host != \"tiinyhost.local\":\n392\t        return host\n393\t    addr = _cli_config_device()\n394\t    if addr:\n395\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: warning: per-device registry not available, falling back to CLI config device {addr}\", file=sys.stderr)\n396\t    return addr\n397\t\n398\t\n399\t#: Probed once per run: (address, is_signed_in).\n400\t_SESSION_CHECKED: tuple[str, bool] | None = None\n401\t\n402\t\n403\tdef cli_session_ok() -> bool:\n404\t    \"\"\"True when the control CLI is signed in for the device it must manage.\"\"\"\n405\t    global _SESSION_CHECKED\n406\t    addr = device_address()\n407\t    cfg_path = cli_config_path()\n408\t    if not addr or not TIINY_BIN.exists():\n409\t        return True\n410\t    cache_key = f\"{addr}:{cfg_path}\"\n411\t    if _SESSION_CHECKED and _SESSION_CHECKED[0] == cache_key:\n412\t        return _SESSION_CHECKED[1]\n413\t    cmd = [str(TIINY_BIN)]\n414\t    if cfg_path:\n415\t        cmd += [\"--config\", str(cfg_path)]\n416\t    cmd += [\"ls\", \"-d\"]\n417\t    if addr and not cfg_path:\n418\t        cmd += [\"--device-address\", addr]\n419\t    try:\n420\t        result = subprocess.run(\n421\t            cmd,\n422\t            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120,\n423\t        )\n424\t    except Exception:\n425\t        return True\n426\t    out = (result.stdout or \"\") + (result.stderr or \"\")\n427\t    signed_in = \"Not logged in\" not in out\n428\t    _SESSION_CHECKED = (cache_key, signed_in)\n429\t    if not signed_in:\n430\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the control CLI is not signed in for {addr}.\", file=sys.stderr)\n431\t        if cfg_path:\n432\t            print(f\"  Using per-device config: {cfg_path}\", file=sys.stderr)\n433\t        else:\n434\t            print(\"  'tiiny login' is a human step, and the CLI holds ONE session\", file=sys.stderr)\n435\t            print(\"  bound to ONE device: signing in here signs the other box out.\", file=sys.stderr)\n436\t    return signed_in\n437\t\n438\t#: The models `release` restores. There is no watchdog any more — the cron\n439\t#: poller was retired on 2026-11-10 and `ensure` replaced it — so this list is\n440\t#: now written in exactly ONE place: this file. Every other copy is a symlink\n441\t#: into this one, which is what stops the set drifting between scripts.\n442\t#:\n443\t#: Speech is NOT here. ASR and TTS moved to Off Grid AI Desktop (the local\n444\t#: Mac gateway) on 2026-11-10, because the appliance serves one heavy stream\n445\t#: at a time — TTS queued behind the appliance's own LLM turn — and because\n446\t#: OGAD's speech models cost no NPU on the device at all. Keeping them here\n447\t#: meant every release reloaded 12% of a shared pool that nothing used.\n448\tREQUIRED_MODELS = (\n449\t    \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\",\n450\t    \"openai/gpt-oss-20b\",\n451\t)\n452\t\n453\t#: The lock's announcement block in hot.md.\n454\tLOCK_MARK_START = \"<!-- tiiny-device-lock:start -->\"\n455\tLOCK_MARK_END = \"<!-- tiiny-device-lock:end -->\"\n456\t\n457\t#: Default lock lifetime, in seconds. Long enough for a long research batch,\n458\t#: short enough that a crashed agent does not lock the device overnight.\n459\tDEFAULT_TTL = 1800\n460\t\n461\t\n462\tdef _load_tiers():\n463\t    \"\"\"Load the shared tier table by path (see tiers.py for why).\n464\t\n465\t    The table sits in the plugin directory, one level above this script,\n466\t    and is found relative to this file only.\n467\t    \"\"\"\n468\t    import importlib.util\n469\t\n470\t    path = SKILL_ROOT / \"tiers.py\"\n471\t    if not path.exists():\n472\t        raise SystemExit(f\"could not find the tier table at {path}\")\n473\t    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"tiiny_tiers\", path)\n474\t    if spec is None or spec.loader is None:\n475\t        raise SystemExit(f\"could not load the tier table from {path}\")\n476\t    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n477\t    spec.loader.exec_module(module)\n478\t    return module\n479\t\n480\t\n481\ttiers = _load_tiers()\n482\t\n483\t\n484\t# --- device access ----------------------------------------------------------\n485\t\n486\tdef device_key() -> str:\n487\t    \"\"\"Return the device bearer, or an empty string.\n488\t\n489\t    Tried in order: explicit environment variable TIINY_API_KEY, the active\n490\t    device's configured key_env via the registry, ``~/.hermes/.env`` (where\n491\t    Hermes keeps it), and the vault's ``pocketlab/CREDENTIALS.md``.\n492\t    The value is never printed.\n493\t    \"\"\"\n494\t    value = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_API_KEY\", \"\").strip()\n495\t    if value:\n496\t        return value\n497\t\n498\t    dev = active_device()\n499\t    if dev:\n500\t        k = dev.key(env_file=_shared_home() / \".env\")\n501\t        if k:\n502\t            return k\n503\t\n504\t    dotenv = _shared_home() / \".env\"\n505\t    try:\n506\t        for line in dotenv.read_text().splitlines():\n507\t            line = line.strip()\n508\t            if line.startswith(\"TIINY_API_KEY=\"):\n509\t                value = line.split(\"=\", 1)[1].strip().strip(\"'\\\"\")\n510\t                if value:\n511\t                    return value\n512\t    except Exception:\n513\t        pass\n514\t\n515\t    credentials = (VAULT / \"pocketlab\" / \"CREDENTIALS.md\") if VAULT else None\n516\t    try:\n517\t        match = re.search(\n518\t            r\"TIINY_API_KEY=\\s*([0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12})\",\n519\t            credentials.read_text(),\n520\t        )\n521\t        if match:\n522\t            return match.group(1)\n523\t    except Exception:\n524\t        pass\n525\t    return \"\"\n526\t\n527\t\n528\tdef _request(url: str, key: str, timeout: int = 20) -> dict:\n529\t    request = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"})\n530\t    try:\n531\t        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=timeout) as response:\n532\t            return json.loads(response.read())\n533\t    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:\n534\t        raise SystemExit(f\"HTTP {exc.code} from {url}: {exc.read().decode('utf8', 'replace')[:200]}\")\n535\t    except Exception as exc:\n536\t        raise SystemExit(f\"could not reach {url}: {exc}\")\n537\t\n538\t\n539\tdef resident_ids() -> list[str]:\n540\t    \"\"\"The model ids the inference endpoint can serve right now.\"\"\"\n541\t    key = device_key()\n542\t    if not key:\n543\t        return []\n544\t    payload = _request(inference_base() + \"/models\", key, timeout=10)\n545\t    ids = []\n546\t    for entry in payload.get(\"data\") or []:\n547\t        value = entry.get(\"id\") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None\n548\t        if isinstance(value, str) and \"/\" in value:\n549\t            ids.append(value)\n550\t    return sorted(ids)\n551\t\n552\t\n553\tdef catalogue() -> list[dict]:\n554\t    \"\"\"The live Model Store, normalised to the fields the skill cares about.\"\"\"\n555\t    key = device_key()\n556\t    if not key:\n557\t        raise SystemExit(\"no device key found (TIINY_API_KEY, ~/.hermes/.env, or the vault)\")\n558\t    payload = _request(management_base() + \"/api/v1/models/online_models\", key, timeout=30)\n559\t    items = payload[\"items\"] if isinstance(payload, dict) and \"items\" in payload else payload\n560\t    rows = []\n561\t    for item in items if isinstance(items, list) else []:\n562\t        if not isinstance(item, dict):\n563\t            continue\n564\t        rows.append({\n565\t            \"id\": item.get(\"fullname\"),\n566\t            \"name\": item.get(\"name\"),\n567\t            \"type\": item.get(\"type\"),\n568\t            \"npu\": item.get(\"npu_usage\"),\n569\t            \"context\": item.get(\"context_window\"),\n570\t            \"status\": item.get(\"status\"),\n571\t            \"desc\": (item.get(\"desc\") or \"\").strip(),\n572\t        })\n573\t    rows.sort(key=lambda r: (str(r.get(\"type\")), -(r.get(\"npu\") or 0), str(r.get(\"id\"))))\n574\t    return rows\n575\t\n576\t\n577\tdef catalogue_index() -> dict[str, dict]:\n578\t    \"\"\"The live catalogue keyed by model id.\"\"\"\n579\t    return {row[\"id\"]: row for row in catalogue() if row.get(\"id\")}\n580\t\n581\t\n582\tdef npu_of(model: str, index: dict[str, dict] | None = None) -> int:\n583\t    \"\"\"A model's NPU share, from the device when possible, else the table.\"\"\"\n584\t    if index and model in index and index[model].get(\"npu\") is not None:\n585\t        return int(index[model][\"npu\"])\n586\t    value = tiers.model_npu(model)\n587\t    return int(value) if value is not None else 0\n588\t\n589\t\n590\t# --- device state -----------------------------------------------------------\n591\t\n592\tdef http_load(model: str) -> bool:\n593\t    \"\"\"Load a model via the device's native HTTP lifecycle API.\"\"\"\n594\t    dev = active_device()\n595\t    if not dev:\n596\t        return False\n597\t    key = dev.key()\n598\t    if not key:\n599\t        return False\n600\t    url = dev.admin_url(f\"/api/v1/models/{model}/start\")\n601\t    req = urllib.request.Request(\n602\t        url,\n603\t        data=b\"{}\",\n604\t        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n605\t        method=\"POST\",\n606\t    )\n607\t    try:\n608\t        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as resp:\n609\t            if resp.status == 200:\n610\t                t0 = time.time()\n611\t                while time.time() - t0 < 180:\n612\t                    status, running = D.probe_running(dev, timeout=5) if D else (0, [])\n613\t                    if model in running:\n614\t                        return True\n615\t                    time.sleep(2)\n616\t                return False\n617\t    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:\n618\t        if exc.code == 401:\n619\t            print(f\"tiiny_ctl: HTTP 401 auth_failed for {dev.name} via {dev.key_env}\", file=sys.stderr)\n620\t            return False\n621\t        return False\n622\t    except Exception:\n623\t        return False\n624\t    return False\n625\t\n626\t\n627\tdef http_unload(model: str) -> bool:\n628\t    \"\"\"Unload a model via the device's native HTTP lifecycle API.\"\"\"\n629\t    dev = active_device()\n630\t    if not dev:\n631\t        return False\n632\t    key = dev.key()\n633\t    if not key:\n634\t        return False\n635\t    url = dev.admin_url(f\"/api/v1/models/{model}/stop\")\n636\t    req = urllib.request.Request(\n637\t        url,\n638\t        data=b\"{}\",\n639\t        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n640\t        method=\"POST\",\n641\t    )\n642\t    try:\n643\t        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as resp:\n644\t            if resp.status == 200:\n645\t                t0 = time.time()\n646\t                while time.time() - t0 < 60:\n647\t                    status, running = D.probe_running(dev, timeout=5) if D else (0, [])\n648\t                    if model not in running:\n649\t                        return True\n650\t                    time.sleep(1)\n651\t                return True\n652\t    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:\n653\t        if exc.code == 401:\n654\t            print(f\"tiiny_ctl: HTTP 401 auth_failed for {dev.name} via {dev.key_env}\", file=sys.stderr)\n655\t            return False\n656\t        return False\n657\t    except Exception:\n658\t        return False\n659\t    return False\n660\t\n661\t\n662\tdef cli_load(model: str) -> bool:\n663\t    \"\"\"Load a model onto the device.\n664\t\n665\t    Prefers HTTP lifecycle (/api/v1/models/{model}/start) which is fast,\n666\t    needs no pty, and isolates credentials. Falls back to CLI with isolated\n667\t    per-device configuration if HTTP lifecycle is unavailable.\n668\t    \"\"\"\n669\t    if http_load(model):\n670\t        return True\n671\t\n672\t    if not TIINY_BIN.exists():\n673\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the tiiny CLI is missing at {TIINY_BIN}\", file=sys.stderr)\n674\t        return False\n675\t    addr = device_address()\n676\t    cfg_path = cli_config_path()\n677\t    if not cli_session_ok():\n678\t        return False\n679\t    cmd = [\"script\", \"-q\", \"/dev/null\", str(TIINY_BIN)]\n680\t    if cfg_path:\n681\t        cmd += [\"--config\", str(cfg_path)]\n682\t    cmd += [\"load\", model, \"-y\"]\n683\t    if addr and not cfg_path:\n684\t        cmd += [\"--device-address\", addr]\n685\t    try:\n686\t        result = subprocess.run(\n687\t            cmd,\n688\t            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=900,\n689\t        )\n690\t    except Exception as exc:\n691\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: load of {model} failed: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n692\t        return False\n693\t    if result.returncode != 0:\n694\t        tail = (result.stderr or result.stdout or \"\").strip().splitlines()[-3:]\n695\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: load of {model} failed: {' '.join(tail)}\", file=sys.stderr)\n696\t        return False\n697\t    return True\n698\t\n699\t\n700\tdef cli_unload(model: str) -> bool:\n701\t    \"\"\"Unload a model from the device, freeing its NPU share.\n702\t\n703\t    Prefers HTTP lifecycle (/api/v1/models/{model}/stop), falls back to CLI.\n704\t    \"\"\"\n705\t    if http_unload(model):\n706\t        return True\n707\t\n708\t    if not TIINY_BIN.exists():\n709\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the tiiny CLI is missing at {TIINY_BIN}\", file=sys.stderr)\n710\t        return False\n711\t    addr = device_address()\n712\t    cfg_path = cli_config_path()\n713\t    cmd = [\"script\", \"-q\", \"/dev/null\", str(TIINY_BIN)]\n714\t    if cfg_path:\n715\t        cmd += [\"--config\", str(cfg_path)]\n716\t    cmd += [\"unload\", model, \"-y\"]\n717\t    if addr and not cfg_path:\n718\t        cmd += [\"--device-address\", addr]\n719\t    try:\n720\t        result = subprocess.run(\n721\t            cmd,\n722\t            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300,\n723\t        )\n724\t    except Exception as exc:\n725\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: unload of {model} failed: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n726\t        return False\n727\t    if result.returncode != 0:\n728\t        tail = (result.stderr or result.stdout or \"\").strip().splitlines()[-3:]\n729\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: unload of {model} failed: {' '.join(tail)}\", file=sys.stderr)\n730\t        return False\n731\t    return True\n732\t\n733\t\n734\tdef ensure_room(wave: list[str], index: dict[str, dict] | None = None) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:\n735\t    \"\"\"Decide what must be evicted so a wave's models can all be resident.\n736\t\n737\t    Returns ``(to evict, kept)``.\n738\t\n739\t    **Why the controller decides this and not the device.** When a load does\n740\t    not fit, the device picks what to evict, and it does not know what the\n741\t    batch needs. Left to itself it kept the 47% generalist and evicted the\n742\t    27% model the wave had just asked for — so the wave's own model was\n743\t    missing and its child would have answered ``404 \"is not loaded\"``.\n744\t\n745\t    So the controller works out what stays before it loads anything: the\n746\t    wave's models are sacred, and of everything else the cheapest are kept\n747\t    while they fit in what is left. A 5% TTS model is worth keeping; a 47%\n748\t    generalist is not, when a wave needs the pool. Everything dropped here\n749\t    is restored by ``release``.\n750\t    \"\"\"\n751\t    resident = resident_ids()\n752\t    wave_set = set(wave)\n753\t    wave_share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in wave)\n754\t    budget = 100 - wave_share\n755\t\n756\t    kept: list[str] = []\n757\t    kept_share = 0\n758\t    evict: list[str] = []\n759\t    others = [m for m in resident if m not in wave_set]\n760\t    for model in sorted(others, key=lambda m: npu_of(m, index)):\n761\t        share = npu_of(model, index)\n762\t        if kept_share + share <= budget:\n763\t            kept.append(model)\n764\t            kept_share += share\n765\t        else:\n766\t            evict.append(model)\n767\t    return evict, kept\n768\t\n769\t\n770\tdef hold_write(models: list[str]) -> None:\n771\t    \"\"\"Record what this agent deliberately loaded.\"\"\"\n772\t    hf = hold_file()\n773\t    hf.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n774\t    hf.write_text(\"\".join(f\"{m}\\n\" for m in models))\n775\t\n776\t\n777\tdef hold_clear() -> None:\n778\t    \"\"\"Release the hold.\"\"\"\n779\t    hf = hold_file()\n780\t    try:\n781\t        hf.write_text(\"\")\n782\t    except Exception:\n783\t        pass\n784\t    try:\n785\t        hf.unlink()\n786\t    except Exception:\n787\t        pass\n788\t    try:\n789\t        if hf.exists() and hf.stat().st_size > 0:\n790\t            raise SystemExit(\n791\t                f\"tiiny_ctl: could not release the hold at {hf} — \"\n792\t                \"`status` will keep reporting it as held. Truncate it by hand.\"\n793\t            )\n794\t    except FileNotFoundError:\n795\t        pass\n796\t\n797\t\n798\tdef hold_state() -> list[str]:\n799\t    \"\"\"The models the hold file names.\"\"\"\n800\t    hf = hold_file()\n801\t    try:\n802\t        return [line.strip() for line in hf.read_text().splitlines() if line.strip()]\n803\t    except Exception:\n804\t        return []\n805\t\n806\t\n807\t# --- the lock ---------------------------------------------------------------\n808\t\n809\tdef lock_read() -> dict | None:\n810\t    \"\"\"The lock's current contents, or None if there is no lock file.\"\"\"\n811\t    lf = lock_file()\n812\t    try:\n813\t        return json.loads(lf.read_text())\n814\t    except Exception:\n815\t        return None\n816\t\n817\t\n818\tdef _lock_fd():\n819\t    lf = lock_file()\n820\t    lf.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n821\t    return os.open(lf, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o600)\n822\t\n823\t\n824\tdef _lock_write(fd: int, payload: dict) -> None:\n825\t    data = json.dumps(payload, indent=1).encode()\n826\t    os.ftruncate(fd, 0)\n827\t    os.lseek(fd, 0, os.SEEK_SET)\n828\t    os.write(fd, data)\n829\t    os.fsync(fd)\n830\t\n831\t\n832\tdef lock_acquire(owner: str, task: str, ttl: int, models: list[str] | None = None) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:\n833\t    \"\"\"Take the lock. Returns ``(payload, error)`` — payload is None on failure.\n834\t\n835\t    The critical section is an ``flock`` on the lock file, so two agents\n836\t    racing for it are serialised by the kernel rather than by hope. The lock\n837\t    itself outlives the process: it is the file's contents, and it expires.\n838\t    \"\"\"\n839\t    fd = _lock_fd()\n840\t    try:\n841\t        try:\n842\t            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n843\t        except OSError:\n844\t            return None, \"another process is holding the lock right now\"\n845\t\n846\t        now = time.time()\n847\t        current = lock_read()\n848\t        if current and current.get(\"state\") == \"held\":\n849\t            expires = float(current.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n850\t            if expires > now:\n851\t                if current.get(\"owner\") == owner:\n852\t                    # RENEW. The holder extends its own lock. Without this a\n853\t                    # long build has to either release the device mid-build\n854\t                    # or lose the device to an agent that takes over when the\n855\t                    # TTL runs out.\n856\t                    payload = dict(current)\n857\t                    payload[\"expires\"] = now + ttl\n858\t                    payload[\"models\"] = models or current.get(\"models\") or []\n859\t                    payload[\"task\"] = task or current.get(\"task\", \"\")\n860\t                    payload[\"pid\"] = os.getpid()\n861\t                    _lock_write(fd, payload)\n862\t                    return payload, \"\"\n863\t                return None, (\n864\t                    f\"the device is held by {current.get('owner')} \"\n865\t                    f\"({current.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) \"\n866\t                    f\"until {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))}\"\n867\t                )\n868\t            print(\n869\t                f\"tiiny_ctl: taking over an expired lock from {current.get('owner')} \"\n870\t                f\"(expired {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))}).\"\n871\t            )\n872\t\n873\t        payload = {\n874\t            \"state\": \"held\",\n875\t            \"owner\": owner,\n876\t            \"task\": task,\n877\t            \"models\": models or [],\n878\t            \"created\": now,\n879\t            \"expires\": now + ttl,\n880\t            \"pid\": os.getpid(),\n881\t        }\n882\t        _lock_write(fd, payload)\n883\t        return payload, \"\"\n884\t    finally:\n885\t        try:\n886\t            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n887\t        finally:\n888\t            os.close(fd)\n889\t\n890\t\n891\tdef lock_release(owner: str, force: bool = False) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n892\t    \"\"\"Release the lock. The file stays; its contents record the release.\"\"\"\n893\t    fd = _lock_fd()\n894\t    try:\n895\t        try:\n896\t            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n897\t        except OSError:\n898\t            return False, \"another process is holding the lock right now\"\n899\t        current = lock_read() or {}\n900\t        if current.get(\"state\") == \"held\":\n901\t            # Strict: the owner must match exactly. An empty owner is not a\n902\t            # wildcard — it is \"unknown caller\", and an unknown caller must\n903\t            # not be able to release somebody else's lock.\n904\t            if not force and current.get(\"owner\") != owner:\n905\t                return False, (\n906\t                    f\"the lock is held by {current.get('owner')}, not {owner}. \"\n907\t                    \"Use --force if that is wrong.\"\n908\t                )\n909\t        _lock_write(fd, {\n910\t            \"state\": \"released\",\n911\t            \"owner\": current.get(\"owner\", owner),\n912\t            \"task\": current.get(\"task\", \"\"),\n913\t            \"released\": time.time(),\n914\t            \"pid\": os.getpid(),\n915\t        })\n916\t        return True, \"\"\n917\t    finally:\n918\t        try:\n919\t            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n920\t        finally:\n921\t            os.close(fd)\n922\t\n923\t\n924\t# --- the announcement -------------------------------------------------------\n925\t\n926\tdef _hot_md() -> Path | None:\n927\t    if VAULT is None:\n928\t        return None\n929\t    hot = VAULT / \"hot.md\"\n930\t    return hot if hot.exists() else None\n931\t\n932\t\n933\tdef announce_set(text: str) -> bool:\n934\t    \"\"\"Write the announcement block into hot.md. Returns True if it landed.\"\"\"\n935\t    hot = _hot_md()\n936\t    if hot is None:\n937\t        return False\n938\t    body = hot.read_text()\n939\t    block = f\"{LOCK_MARK_START}\\n{text}\\n{LOCK_MARK_END}\"\n940\t    if LOCK_MARK_START in body and LOCK_MARK_END in body:\n941\t        head = body[: body.index(LOCK_MARK_START)]\n942\t        tail = body[body.index(LOCK_MARK_END) + len(LOCK_MARK_END):]\n943\t        updated = head + block + tail\n944\t    else:\n945\t        updated = body.rstrip() + \"\\n\\n\" + block + \"\\n\"\n946\t    updated = re.sub(r\"(?m)^updated:.*$\", f\"updated: {time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}\", updated, count=1)\n947\t    hot.write_text(updated)\n948\t    return True\n949\t\n950\t\n951\tdef announce_clear() -> bool:\n952\t    \"\"\"Remove the announcement block from hot.md.\"\"\"\n953\t    hot = _hot_md()\n954\t    if hot is None:\n955\t        return False\n956\t    body = hot.read_text()\n957\t    if LOCK_MARK_START not in body or LOCK_MARK_END not in body:\n958\t        return False\n959\t    head = body[: body.index(LOCK_MARK_START)]\n960\t    tail = body[body.index(LOCK_MARK_END) + len(LOCK_MARK_END):]\n961\t    hot.write_text((head.rstrip() + \"\\n\\n\" + tail.lstrip()).rstrip() + \"\\n\")\n962\t    return True\n963\t\n964\t\n965\t# --- the plan ---------------------------------------------------------------\n966\t\n967\tdef plan_write(entries: list[dict], waves: list[list[str]], owner: str, task: str) -> dict:\n968\t    payload = {\n969\t        \"batch\": time.strftime(\"%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ\", time.gmtime()) + f\"-{os.getpid():x}\",\n970\t        \"created\": time.time(),\n971\t        \"owner\": owner,\n972\t        \"task\": task,\n973\t        \"entries\": entries,\n974\t        \"waves\": waves,\n975\t    }\n976\t    target_plan = plan_file()\n977\t    target_plan.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n978\t    target_plan.write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=1))\n979\t    default_plan = SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\"\n980\t    if target_plan != default_plan:\n981\t        try:\n982\t            default_plan.write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=1))\n983\t        except Exception:\n984\t            pass\n985\t    return payload\n986\t\n987\t\n988\tdef plan_read() -> dict | None:\n989\t    try:\n990\t        return json.loads(plan_file().read_text())\n991\t    except Exception:\n992\t        try:\n993\t            return json.loads((SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\").read_text())\n994\t        except Exception:\n995\t            return None\n996\t\n997\t\n998\tdef plan_clear() -> None:\n999\t    target_plan = plan_file()\n1000\t    try:\n1001\t        target_plan.write_text(\"\")\n1002\t    except Exception:\n1003\t        pass\n1004\t    try:\n1005\t        target_plan.unlink()\n1006\t    except Exception:\n1007\t        pass\n1008\t    default_plan = SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\"\n1009\t    if target_plan != default_plan:\n1010\t        try:\n1011\t            default_plan.write_text(\"\")\n1012\t            default_plan.unlink()\n1013\t        except Exception:\n1014\t            pass\n1015\t\n1016\t\n1017\t# --- commands ---------------------------------------------------------------\n1018\t\n1019\tdef cmd_status(args) -> int:\n1020\t    dev = active_device()\n1021\t    dev_hdr = f\" — {dev.name} ({dev.address})\" if dev else \"\"\n1022\t    print(f\"TIINY DEVICE STATUS{dev_hdr}\\n\")\n1023\t\n1024\t    key = device_key()\n1025\t    key_src = f\" ({dev.key_env})\" if dev and dev.key_env else \"\"\n1026\t    print(f\"  key        : {'found' + key_src if key else 'NOT FOUND'}\")\n1027\t    print(f\"  inference  : {inference_base()}\")\n1028\t    print(f\"  management : {management_base()}\")\n1029\t\n1030\t    try:\n1031\t        resident = resident_ids()\n1032\t    except SystemExit as exc:\n1033\t        print(f\"  device     : UNREACHABLE — {exc}\")\n1034\t        resident = []\n1035\t    else:\n1036\t        print(f\"  device     : reachable\")\n1037\t\n1038\t    index = {}\n1039\t    try:\n1040\t        index = catalogue_index()\n1041\t    except SystemExit:\n1042\t        pass\n1043\t\n1044\t    if resident:\n1045\t        print(f\"\\n  RESIDENT ({len(resident)} models, \"\n1046\t              f\"{sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in resident)}% NPU):\")\n1047\t        for model in resident:\n1048\t            print(f\"    {npu_of(model, index):>3}%  {model}\")\n1049\t    else:\n1050\t        print(\"\\n  RESIDENT: none known\")\n1051\t\n1052\t    lock = lock_read() or {}\n1053\t    state = lock.get(\"state\", \"none\")\n1054\t    if state == \"held\":\n1055\t        expires = float(lock.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n1056\t        remaining = int(expires - time.time())\n1057\t        print(f\"\\n  LOCK: HELD by {lock.get('owner')} — {lock.get('task') or 'no task'} \"\n1058\t              f\"({remaining}s left)\")\n1059\t        print(f\"        models: {', '.join(lock.get('models') or []) or '(none recorded)'}\")\n1060\t    else:\n1061\t        print(f\"\\n  LOCK: {state}\")\n1062\t\n1063\t    held = hold_state()\n1064\t    print(f\"  HOLD: {', '.join(held) if held else '(none)'}\")\n1065\t\n1066\t    plan = plan_read()\n1067\t    if plan and plan.get(\"entries\"):\n1068\t        print(f\"\\n  PLAN {plan.get('batch')}: {len(plan['entries'])} task(s), \"\n1069\t              f\"{len(plan.get('waves') or [])} wave(s), owner {plan.get('owner')}\")\n1070\t    else:\n1071\t        print(\"\\n  PLAN: none\")\n1072\t\n1073\t    return 0\n1074\t\n1075\t\n1076\tdef cmd_plan(args) -> int:\n1077\t    goals = list(args.goal or [])\n1078\t    if not goals:\n1079\t        print(\"tiiny_ctl: plan needs at least one --goal\", file=sys.stderr)\n1080\t        return 2\n1081\t\n1082\t    index = catalogue_index()\n1083\t    resident = set(resident_ids())\n1084\t\n1085\t    # Which candidate can this device actually run? A model that is not\n1086\t    # downloaded cannot be loaded, so choosing it would produce a plan that\n1087\t    # fails at the load step.\n1088\t    def candidates(tier: str) -> list[str]:\n1089\t        return [name for name, _npu, _ctx in tiers.TIER_CHOICES[tier]]\n1090\t\n1091\t    entries = []\n1092\t    for goal in goals:\n1093\t        tier = args.tier or tiers.detect_tier(goal)\n1094\t        if tier not in tiers.TIER_MODEL:\n1095\t            print(f\"tiiny_ctl: unknown tier {tier!r}. Valid: {', '.join(tiers.VALID_TIERS)}\",\n1096\t                  file=sys.stderr)\n1097\t            return 2\n1098\t        forced_model = None\n1099\t        if args.model:\n1100\t            forced_model = args.model\n1101\t        chosen = None\n1102\t        reason = \"\"\n1103\t        for name in candidates(tier):\n1104\t            if forced_model and name != forced_model:\n1105\t                continue\n1106\t            status = (index.get(name) or {}).get(\"status\")\n1107\t            if name in resident:\n1108\t                chosen, reason = name, \"resident\"\n1109\t                break\n1110\t            if status in (\"downloaded\", \"running\"):\n1111\t                chosen, reason = name, \"downloaded\"\n1112\t                break\n1113\t        if chosen is None:\n1114\t            chosen = tiers.GENERALIST\n1115\t            reason = \"no downloadable candidate — the generalist\"\n1116\t        entries.append({\n1117\t            \"goal\": goal,\n1118\t            \"tier\": tier,\n1119\t            \"model\": chosen,\n1120\t            \"npu\": npu_of(chosen, index),\n1121\t            \"reason\": reason,\n1122\t        })\n1123\t\n1124\t    # Waves: the device serves one generation-heavy stream at a time, and the\n1125\t    # models of a wave must fit inside one shared NPU pool. A batch whose\n1126\t    # models do not fit is split into waves, and each wave is a separate\n1127\t    # delegation call.\n1128\t    distinct: list[str] = []\n1129\t    for entry in entries:\n1130\t        if entry[\"model\"] not in distinct:\n1131\t            distinct.append(entry[\"model\"])\n1132\t\n1133\t    # First-fit decreasing, with the models that are already resident placed\n1134\t    # first. Resident-first matters: a wave that loads a new specialist\n1135\t    # evicts whatever does not fit, so keeping the already-loaded model in\n1136\t    # the first wave saves a load and a reload. Ties break on the larger\n1137\t    # model, because a large model is the one that constrains the packing.\n1138\t    ordered = sorted(distinct, key=lambda m: (0 if m in resident else 1, -npu_of(m, index)))\n1139\t    waves: list[list[str]] = []\n1140\t    for model in ordered:\n1141\t        share = npu_of(model, index)\n1142\t        placed = False\n1143\t        for wave in waves:\n1144\t            if sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in wave) + share <= 100:\n1145\t                wave.append(model)\n1146\t                placed = True\n1147\t                break\n1148\t        if not placed:\n1149\t            waves.append([model])\n1150\t\n1151\t    for entry in entries:\n1152\t        for number, wave in enumerate(waves, start=1):\n1153\t            if entry[\"model\"] in wave:\n1154\t                entry[\"wave\"] = number\n1155\t                break\n1156\t\n1157\t    payload = plan_write(entries, waves, args.owner or \"unassigned\", args.task or \"\")\n1158\t\n1159\t    print(f\"BATCH PLAN  {payload['batch']}\")\n1160\t    print(f\"  owner: {payload['owner']}    task: {payload['task'] or '(none recorded)'}\\n\")\n1161\t    print(f\"  {'#':<3}{'tier':<10}{'model':<42}{'NPU':>5}  {'wave':<5}{'state'}\")\n1162\t    print(f\"  {'-'*78}\")\n1163\t    for number, entry in enumerate(entries, start=1):\n1164\t        print(f\"  {number:<3}{entry['tier']:<10}{entry['model']:<42}\"\n1165\t              f\"{entry['npu']:>4}%  {entry.get('wave', 1):<5}{entry['reason']}\")\n1166\t\n1167\t    print()\n1168\t    for number, wave in enumerate(waves, start=1):\n1169\t        share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in wave)\n1170\t        fits = \"fits\" if share <= 100 else \"DOES NOT FIT\"\n1171\t        evict, kept = ensure_room(wave, index)\n1172\t        print(f\"  WAVE {number}: {share}% NPU — {fits}\")\n1173\t        for model in wave:\n1174\t            print(f\"      {model}\")\n1175\t        if evict:\n1176\t            print(f\"      evicts to make room: {', '.join(evict)}\")\n1177\t        if kept:\n1178\t            print(f\"      stays alongside: {', '.join(kept)}\")\n1179\t        print(\"      (displacement is computed from the residency now; the\")\n1180\t        print(\"       controller re-decides it when it actually loads)\")\n1181\t\n1182\t    resident_share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in resident)\n1183\t    batch_share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in distinct)\n1184\t    displaced = [m for m in resident if m not in distinct]\n1185\t    print(f\"\\n  batch models total : {batch_share}% NPU\")\n1186\t    print(f\"  resident now       : {resident_share}% NPU across {len(resident)} models\")\n1187\t    if displaced:\n1188\t        print(f\"  may be displaced   : {', '.join(displaced)}\")\n1189\t        print(\"      (release restores the required set when the batch is done)\")\n1190\t    print(\"\\n  The device serves one generation-heavy stream at a time: run one\")\n1191\t    print(\"  heavy child per wave, not every wave's children at once.\")\n1192\t    return 0\n1193\t\n1194\t\n1195\tdef cmd_reserve(args) -> int:\n1196\t    payload, error = lock_acquire(args.owner, args.task or \"\", args.ttl, args.model or [])\n1197\t    if payload is None:\n1198\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: cannot take the lock — {error}\", file=sys.stderr)\n1199\t        return 1\n1200\t\n1201\t    held = \", \".join(args.model or []) or \"(not decided yet)\"\n1202\t    text = (\n1203\t        \"**Tiiny is BUSY — do not schedule device work.**\\n\"\n1204\t        f\"Held by `{payload['owner']}` for {payload['task'] or 'an unnamed task'} \"\n1205\t        f\"since {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S')}. Models: {held}. \"\n1206\t        f\"Lock expires {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(payload['expires']))}. \"\n1207\t        \"This notice disappears when the device is released.\"\n1208\t    )\n1209\t    announced = announce_set(text)\n1210\t\n1211\t    print(f\"lock held by {payload['owner']} until \"\n1212\t          f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(payload['expires']))}\")\n1213\t    print(f\"  announcement: {'hot.md updated' if announced else 'hot.md not reachable — skipped'}\")\n1214\t    return 0\n1215\t\n1216\t\n1217\tdef cmd_load(args) -> int:\n1218\t    # GUARD FIRST. A load is a swap on a device with one shared NPU pool, so\n1219\t    # loading while another agent holds the device silently evicts whatever\n1220\t    # that agent deliberately loaded. Check before touching anything.\n1221\t    owner = args.owner or _caller_owner()\n1222\t    holder = _lock_holder()\n1223\t    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not args.force:\n1224\t        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n1225\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the device is held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n1226\t              f\"({holder.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) until \"\n1227\t              f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))} — \"\n1228\t              f\"refusing to load. Use --force if that is wrong.\",\n1229\t              file=sys.stderr)\n1230\t        return 1\n1231\t\n1232\t    models = list(args.model or [])\n1233\t    if not models:\n1234\t        plan = plan_read()\n1235\t        if not plan:\n1236\t            print(\"tiiny_ctl: no --model and no plan. Run 'plan' first.\", file=sys.stderr)\n1237\t            return 2\n1238\t        waves = plan.get(\"waves\") or []\n1239\t        if args.wave:\n1240\t            if args.wave < 1 or args.wave > len(waves):\n1241\t                print(f\"tiiny_ctl: wave {args.wave} is outside this plan \"\n1242\t                      f\"(1..{len(waves)})\", file=sys.stderr)\n1243\t                return 2\n1244\t            models = list(waves[args.wave - 1])\n1245\t        else:\n1246\t            models = [model for wave in waves for model in wave]\n1247\t\n1248\t    if not models:\n1249\t        print(\"tiiny_ctl: nothing to load\", file=sys.stderr)\n1250\t        return 2\n1251\t\n1252\t    before = set(resident_ids())\n1253\t\n1254\t    # Record the batch before loading, so `status` shows what is resident\n1255\t    # because of this run rather than as a leftover from an earlier one.\n1256\t    hold_write(models)\n1257\t\n1258\t    # Make room explicitly. When a load does not fit, the device picks what to\n1259\t    # evict and it does not know what the batch needs — left to itself it\n1260\t    # kept the 47% generalist and evicted the 27% model the wave had just\n1261\t    # asked for. The controller decides what stays.\n1262\t    index = {}\n1263\t    try:\n1264\t        index = catalogue_index()\n1265\t    except SystemExit:\n1266\t        pass\n1267\t    evict, kept = ensure_room(models, index)\n1268\t    for model in evict:\n1269\t        print(f\"  evicting {model} to make room for the wave\", flush=True)\n1270\t        cli_unload(model)\n1271\t    if evict or kept:\n1272\t        print(f\"  staying alongside: {', '.join(kept) if kept else '(nothing)'}\")\n1273\t\n1274\t    for model in models:\n1275\t        if model in resident_ids():\n1276\t            print(f\"  already resident: {model}\")\n1277\t            continue\n1278\t        print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n1279\t        cli_load(model)\n1280\t\n1281\t    after = set(resident_ids())\n1282\t    missing = [m for m in models if m not in after]\n1283\t    evicted = sorted(before - after)\n1284\t\n1285\t    landed = [m for m in models if m not in missing]\n1286\t    if landed:\n1287\t        print(f\"\\n  loaded      : {', '.join(landed)}\")\n1288\t    if missing:\n1289\t        # Never print \"loaded: X\" next to \"NOT RESIDENT: X\". The old wording\n1290\t        # claimed the load and then denied it in the same breath, which is\n1291\t        # exactly the ambiguity that lets a batch fall back to the paid\n1292\t        # provider without anybody noticing.\n1293\t        print(f\"\\n  NOT RESIDENT: {', '.join(missing)}\")\n1294\t    if evicted:\n1295\t        print(f\"  displaced   : {', '.join(evicted)}\")\n1296\t    print(f\"  hold        : {', '.join(hold_state())}\")\n1297\t\n1298\t    if missing:\n1299\t        print(\"\\n  A model that did not land cannot answer: the device will return\")\n1300\t        print(\"  404 \\\"is not loaded\\\" and the child will fall back to the paid\")\n1301\t        print(\"  provider. If the CLI says 'Not logged in', the device session is\")\n1302\t        print(\"  logged out and 'tiiny login' is a human step. Check that the\")\n1303\t        print(\"  model is downloaded, and that its NPU share leaves room for what\")\n1304\t        print(\"  else the wave needs.\", file=sys.stderr)\n1305\t        return 1\n1306\t    return 0\n1307\t\n1308\t\n1309\tdef ensure_model(model: str, owner: str | None = None,\n1310\t                 force: bool = False, quiet: bool = False) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n1311\t    \"\"\"Make a model resident on demand and return (success, detail).\n1312\t\n1313\t    This is the machine-readable interface used by the delegation router.\n1314\t    Calls ``cmd_ensure``'s logic directly (no subprocess, no CLI) so the\n1315\t    router can call it inside ``route_task``.\n1316\t\n1317\t    * **Idempotent.** A model already resident returns ``(True, \"resident\")``.\n1318\t    * **Guard-aware.** Refuses while another agent holds the lock.\n1319\t    * **Evicts only what this caller may evict.** Explicit eviction, not\n1320\t      device-guessed.\n1321\t    * **Verifies.** Returns ``(False, \"NOT RESIDENT: <model>\")`` when the\n1322\t      device answers 404.\n1323\t\n1324\t    Returns ``(True, \"resident\")`` when the model is already running,\n1325\t    ``(True, \"loaded\")`` when a load was needed and succeeded,\n1326\t    ``(False, \"locked: ...\")`` when the device is held by another agent,\n1327\t    ``(False, \"NOT RESIDENT: <model>\")`` when the model failed to load.\n1328\t    \"\"\"\n1329\t    owner = owner or _caller_owner()\n1330\t    holder = _lock_holder()\n1331\t    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not force:\n1332\t        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n1333\t        detail = (f\"locked: device held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n1334\t                  f\"until {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))}\")\n1335\t        return False, detail\n1336\t\n1337\t    resident = set(resident_ids())\n1338\t    if model in resident:\n1339\t        return True, \"resident\"\n1340\t\n1341\t    # Record what this call loaded.\n1342\t    hold_write([model])\n1343\t\n1344\t    index = {}\n1345\t    try:\n1346\t        index = catalogue_index()\n1347\t    except SystemExit:\n1348\t        pass\n1349\t\n1350\t    evict, kept = ensure_room([model], index)\n1351\t    for m in evict:\n1352\t        if not quiet:\n1353\t            print(f\"  evicting {m} to make room\", flush=True)\n1354\t        cli_unload(m)\n1355\t    if kept and not quiet:\n1356\t        print(f\"  staying alongside: {', '.join(kept)}\")\n1357\t\n1358\t    if not quiet:\n1359\t        print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n1360\t    cli_load(model)\n1361\t\n1362\t    after = set(resident_ids())\n1363\t    if model in after:\n1364\t        return True, \"loaded\"\n1365\t\n1366\t    return False, f\"NOT RESIDENT: {model}\"\n1367\t\n1368\t\n1369\tdef cmd_ensure(args) -> int:\n1370\t    \"\"\"Make models resident on demand — the replacement for the watchdog.\n1371\t\n1372\t    The appliance unloads its models on reboot, and nothing else puts them\n1373\t    back. A five-minute cron poll used to do it by restoring a fixed set,\n1374\t    which is what made that set the single most-drifted thing in the skill:\n1375\t    three scripts each carried a copy and each could re-assert an old one.\n1376\t\n1377\t    This is the demand-driven version. An agent that is about to delegate\n1378\t    calls `ensure <model>` and gets the model it actually needs, instead of\n1379\t    waiting for a poll to restore a set it may not want.\n1380\t\n1381\t    The contract:\n1382\t\n1383\t    * **Idempotent.** A model already resident is reported and not reloaded,\n1384\t      so calling it before every batch costs one HTTP round trip.\n1385\t    * **Guard-aware.** Refuses while another agent holds the lock, for the\n1386\t      same reason `load` does: a load is a swap, and swapping under another\n1387\t      agent's feet evicts the model its child is running on.\n1388\t    * **Evicts only what this caller may evict.** If the model does not fit,\n1389\t      the controller names what it will displace and unloads it explicitly.\n1390\t      It never leaves the choice to the device, which has no idea what the\n1391\t      next child needs.\n1392\t    * **Verifies.** A model that did not land is reported as a failure, not a\n1393\t      success, because the device answers `404 \"is not loaded\"` and Hermes\n1394\t      then falls back to the paid provider — silently.\n1395\t    \"\"\"\n1396\t    owner = args.owner or _caller_owner()\n1397\t    holder = _lock_holder()\n1398\t    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not args.force:\n1399\t        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n1400\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the device is held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n1401\t              f\"({holder.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) until \"\n1402\t              f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))} — \"\n1403\t              f\"refusing to ensure. Use --force if that is wrong.\",\n1404\t              file=sys.stderr)\n1405\t        return 1\n1406\t\n1407\t    wanted = [m for m in (args.model or []) if m]\n1408\t    if not wanted:\n1409\t        print(\"tiiny_ctl: ensure needs at least one --model\", file=sys.stderr)\n1410\t        return 2\n1411\t\n1412\t    resident = set(resident_ids())\n1413\t    missing = [m for m in wanted if m not in resident]\n1414\t    if not missing:\n1415\t        print(f\"  already resident: {', '.join(wanted)}\")\n1416\t        return 0\n1417\t\n1418\t    before = set(resident)\n1419\t\n1420\t    # Record what this call loaded. There is no watchdog to fight any more,\n1421\t    # so this is bookkeeping rather than an interlock.\n1422\t    hold_write(missing)\n1423\t\n1424\t    index = {}\n1425\t    try:\n1426\t        index = catalogue_index()\n1427\t    except SystemExit:\n1428\t        pass\n1429\t\n1430\t    evict, kept = ensure_room(missing, index)\n1431\t    for model in evict:\n1432\t        print(f\"  evicting {model} to make room\", flush=True)\n1433\t        cli_unload(model)\n1434\t    if kept:\n1435\t        print(f\"  staying alongside: {', '.join(kept)}\")\n1436\t\n1437\t    for model in missing:\n1438\t        print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n1439\t        cli_load(model)\n1440\t\n1441\t    after = set(resident_ids())\n1442\t    still_missing = [m for m in wanted if m not in after]\n1443\t    displaced = sorted(before - after)\n1444\t\n1445\t    print(f\"\\n  ensured     : {', '.join(wanted)}\")\n1446\t    if displaced:\n1447\t        print(f\"  displaced   : {', '.join(displaced)}\")\n1448\t    if still_missing:\n1449\t        print(f\"  NOT RESIDENT: {', '.join(still_missing)}\")\n1450\t        print(\"\\n  A model that did not land cannot answer: the device returns\"\n1451\t              \"\\n  404 \\\"is not loaded\\\" and the child falls back to the paid\"\n1452\t              \"\\n  provider. Check that it is downloaded, and that its NPU share\"\n1453\t              \"\\n  leaves room for what else must stay.\", file=sys.stderr)\n1454\t        return 1\n1455\t    return 0\n1456\t\n1457\t\n1458\tdef cmd_release(args) -> int:\n1459\t    # GUARD FIRST, and this used to be the whole bug.\n1460\t    #\n1461\t    # The old order was: clear the hold, restore the required set, THEN\n1462\t    # check the lock. So another agent running `release` cleared the hold and\n1463\t    # evicted the held model before it discovered the lock was not its own.\n1464\t    # Worse, the old code defaulted the owner to the lock's current owner, so\n1465\t    # a bare `release` adopted somebody else's lock and released it happily.\n1466\t    #\n1467\t    # Ownership is now checked before ANY mutation, and the owner is never\n1468\t    # inherited from the lock.\n1469\t    owner = args.owner or _caller_owner()\n1470\t    holder = _lock_holder()\n1471\t    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not args.force:\n1472\t        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n1473\t        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the device is held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n1474\t              f\"({holder.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) until \"\n1475\t              f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))} — \"\n1476\t              f\"refusing to release. Use --force if that is wrong.\",\n1477\t              file=sys.stderr)\n1478\t        return 1\n1479\t\n1480\t    # Clear the hold before restoring, so `status` reports the restore's own\n1481\t    # result rather than the batch's record of what it had loaded.\n1482\t    hold_clear()\n1483\t    print(\"hold cleared\")\n1484\t\n1485\t    resident = set(resident_ids())\n1486\t    missing = [m for m in REQUIRED_MODELS if m not in resident]\n1487\t    for model in missing:\n1488\t        print(f\"  restoring {model} ...\", flush=True)\n1489\t        cli_load(model)\n1490\t\n1491\t    restored = set(resident_ids())\n1492\t    still_missing = [m for m in REQUIRED_MODELS if m not in restored]\n1493\t    if still_missing:\n1494\t        print(f\"  WARNING: still missing {', '.join(still_missing)}\", file=sys.stderr)\n1495\t\n1496\t    plan_clear()\n1497\t    print(\"plan cleared\")\n1498\t\n1499\t    ok, error = lock_release(owner, force=args.force)\n1500\t    print(f\"lock released\" if ok else f\"lock NOT released — {error}\")\n1501\t\n1502\t    cleared = announce_clear()\n1503\t    print(f\"announcement: {'removed from hot.md' if cleared else 'nothing to remove'}\")\n1504\t\n1505\t    print(f\"\\nresident now: {', '.join(sorted(restored)) or '(none)'}\")\n1506\t    return 0 if ok and not still_missing else 1\n1507\t\n1508\t\n1509\tdef cmd_catalog(args) -> int:\n1510\t    rows = catalogue()\n1511\t    index = {row[\"id\"]: row for row in rows if row.get(\"id\")}\n1512\t    current = {rid: row for rid, row in index.items()}\n1513\t\n1514\t    previous = {}\n1515\t    try:\n1516\t        previous = json.loads(SNAPSHOT_FILE.read_text()).get(\"models\", {})\n1517\t    except Exception:\n1518\t        previous = {}\n1519\t\n1520\t    print(f\"MODEL STORE: {len(current)} models\\n\")\n1521\t\n1522\t    if not previous:\n1523\t        print(\"  No snapshot to compare against. Run with --update to record one.\")\n1524\t        for row in rows:\n1525\t            print(f\"    {str(row.get('npu')):>4}%  {row.get('status'):<16} {row['id']}\")\n1526\t        return 0\n1527\t\n1528\t    new = sorted(set(current) - set(previous))\n1529\t    gone = sorted(set(previous) - set(current))\n1530\t    changed = []\n1531\t    for rid in sorted(set(current) & set(previous)):\n1532\t        before, after = previous[rid], current[rid]\n1533\t        for field in (\"npu\", \"context\", \"status\"):\n1534\t            if before.get(field) != after.get(field):\n1535\t                changed.append((rid, field, before.get(field), after.get(field)))\n1536\t                break\n1537\t\n1538\t    print(f\"  snapshot had {len(previous)} models; the store has {len(current)}\")\n1539\t    if new:\n1540\t        print(f\"\\n  NEW ({len(new)}):\")\n1541\t        for rid in new:\n1542\t            row = current[rid]\n1543\t            print(f\"    + {rid}  [{row.get('type')}] {row.get('npu')}% NPU, \"\n1544\t                  f\"status {row.get('status')}\")\n1545\t    if gone:\n1546\t        print(f\"\\n  GONE ({len(gone)}):\")\n1547\t        for rid in gone:\n1548\t            print(f\"    - {rid}\")\n1549\t    if changed:\n1550\t        print(f\"\\n  CHANGED ({len(changed)}):\")\n1551\t        for rid, field, before, after in changed:\n1552\t            print(f\"    ~ {rid}: {field} {before} -> {after}\")\n1553\t    if not (new or gone or changed):\n1554\t        print(\"\\n  Nothing changed.\")\n1555\t\n1556\t    tier_models = {name for choices in tiers.TIER_CHOICES.values() for name, _n, _c in choices}\n1557\t    tier_drift = [rid for rid, *_ in changed if rid in tier_models]\n1558\t    if tier_drift:\n1559\t        print(f\"\\n  The tier table quotes figures for these models, which just changed:\")\n1560\t        for rid in tier_drift:\n1561\t            print(f\"    - {rid}\")\n1562\t\n1563\t    if args.update:\n1564\t        SNAPSHOT_FILE.write_text(json.dumps({\n1565\t            \"source\": management_base() + \"/api/v1/models/online_models\",\n1566\t            \"read\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"),\n1567\t            \"model_count\": len(current),\n1568\t            \"models\": current,\n1569\t        }, indent=1))\n1570\t        print(f\"\\n  snapshot written to {SNAPSHOT_FILE}\")\n1571\t\n1572\t    return 0\n1573\t\n1574\t\n1575\tdef cmd_image(args) -> int:\n1576\t    kind = args.kind\n1577\t    model, reason = tiers.pick_special_model(args.description, kind)\n1578\t    index = catalogue_index()\n1579\t    row = index.get(model) or {}\n1580\t\n1581\t    print(f\"IMAGE MODEL for: {args.description}\\n\")\n1582\t    print(f\"  pick    : {model}\")\n1583\t    print(f\"  reason  : {reason}\")\n1584\t    print(f\"  role    : {tiers.SPECIAL_MODELS.get(model, (0, '', 'n/a', ''))[2]}\")\n1585\t    print(f\"  NPU     : {row.get('npu', tiers.model_npu(model))}%\")\n1586\t    print(f\"  state   : {row.get('status', 'unknown')}\"\n1587\t          f\"{' — RESIDENT' if model in resident_ids() else ''}\")\n1588\t    if model in tiers.KNOWN_GOOD_SIZES:\n1589\t        print(f\"  sizes   : {', '.join(tiers.KNOWN_GOOD_SIZES[model])}\")\n1590\t\n1591\t    alternatives = [\n1592\t        rid for rid, entry in tiers.SPECIAL_MODELS.items()\n1593\t        if entry[1] == kind and rid != model\n1594\t    ]\n1595\t    if alternatives:\n1596\t        print(\"\\n  other candidates for this kind:\")\n1597\t        for rid in alternatives:\n1598\t            status = (index.get(rid) or {}).get(\"status\", \"?\")\n1599\t            npu = (index.get(rid) or {}).get(\"npu\", tiers.model_npu(rid))\n1600\t            note = \"\" if status in (\"downloaded\", \"running\") else \"  (not downloaded — download it first)\"\n1601\t            print(f\"    {rid}  {npu}% NPU, {status}{note}\")\n1602\t\n1603\t    if args.load:\n1604\t        print()\n1605\t        hold_write([model])\n1606\t        if model not in resident_ids():\n1607\t            print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n1608\t            cli_load(model)\n1609\t        if model in resident_ids():\n1610\t            print(f\"  loaded and resident. Release with 'tiiny_ctl.py release'.\")\n1611\t            return 0\n1612\t        print(\"  the model did not land\", file=sys.stderr)\n1613\t        return 1\n1614\t    return 0\n1615\t\n1616\t\n1617\t# --- entry point ------------------------------------------------------------\n1618\t\n1619\tdef main() -> int:\n1620\t    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n1621\t        description=\"Control the Tiiny device: plan, lock, load, release, refresh.\",\n1622\t    )\n1623\t    parser.add_argument(\"--device\", help=\"target device name (e.g. tiiny-ant, tiiny-octo) or IP address\")\n1624\t    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\")\n1625\t\n1626\t    sub.add_parser(\"status\", help=\"device, resident set, lock, hold, plan\")\n1627\t\n1628\t    plan = sub.add_parser(\"plan\", help=\"pick models, check the NPU fit, write the plan\")\n1629\t    plan.add_argument(\"--goal\", action=\"append\", help=\"a task goal; repeat for a batch\")\n1630\t    plan.add_argument(\"--tier\", choices=tiers.VALID_TIERS, help=\"force one tier for every goal\")\n1631\t    plan.add_argument(\"--model\", help=\"force one model for every goal\")\n1632\t    plan.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"), help=\"who is planning\")\n1633\t    plan.add_argument(\"--task\", default=\"\", help=\"what the batch is for\")\n1634\t\n1635\t    reserve = sub.add_parser(\"reserve\", help=\"take the cross-agent lock\")\n1636\t    reserve.add_argument(\"--owner\", required=True, help=\"who is taking the device\")\n1637\t    reserve.add_argument(\"--task\", default=\"\", help=\"what it is for\")\n1638\t    reserve.add_argument(\"--model\", action=\"append\", help=\"a model the batch needs\")\n1639\t    reserve.add_argument(\"--ttl\", type=int, default=DEFAULT_TTL, help=\"lock lifetime in seconds\")\n1640\t\n1641\t    load = sub.add_parser(\"load\", help=\"load what the batch needs\")\n1642\t    load.add_argument(\"--model\", action=\"append\", help=\"a model to load\")\n1643\t    load.add_argument(\"--wave\", type=int, help=\"load only this wave of the plan\")\n1644\t    load.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"),\n1645\t                      help=\"who is loading; defaults to the active profile\")\n1646\t    load.add_argument(\"--force\", action=\"store_true\",\n1647\t                      help=\"load even if another agent holds the device\")\n1648\t\n1649\t    ensure = sub.add_parser(\n1650\t        \"ensure\", help=\"make models resident on demand (replaces the watchdog)\")\n1651\t    ensure.add_argument(\"--model\", action=\"append\", required=True,\n1652\t                        help=\"a model that must be resident; repeat for several\")\n1653\t    ensure.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"),\n1654\t                        help=\"who is asking; defaults to the active profile\")\n1655\t    ensure.add_argument(\"--force\", action=\"store_true\",\n1656\t                        help=\"load even if another agent holds the device\")\n1657\t\n1658\t    release = sub.add_parser(\"release\", help=\"restore the device and clear the lock\")\n1659\t    release.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"),\n1660\t                         help=\"who is releasing; defaults to the active profile\")\n1661\t    release.add_argument(\"--force\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"release a lock held by someone else\")\n1662\t\n1663\t    catalog = sub.add_parser(\"catalog\", help=\"diff the live Model Store against the snapshot\")\n1664\t    catalog.add_argument(\"--update\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"record the snapshot\")\n1665\t    catalog.add_argument(\"--refresh\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"refresh and record in one step\")\n1666\t\n1667\t    image = sub.add_parser(\"image\", help=\"pick the right image model for a job\")\n1668\t    image.add_argument(\"description\", help=\"what the image is for\")\n1669\t    image.add_argument(\"--kind\", default=\"image\", choices=sorted(tiers.SPECIAL_DEFAULTS))\n1670\t    image.add_argument(\"--load\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"load the chosen model\")\n1671\t\n1672\t    args = parser.parse_args()\n1673\t    if getattr(args, \"device\", None):\n1674\t        set_active_device(args.device)\n1675\t\n1676\t    if not args.command:\n1677\t        parser.print_help()\n1678\t        return 2\n1679\t\n1680\t    if args.command == \"status\":\n1681\t        return cmd_status(args)\n1682\t    if args.command == \"plan\":\n1683\t        return cmd_plan(args)\n1684\t    if args.command == \"reserve\":\n1685\t        return cmd_reserve(args)\n1686\t    if args.command == \"load\":\n1687\t        return cmd_load(args)\n1688\t    if args.command == \"ensure\":\n1689\t        return cmd_ensure(args)\n1690\t    if args.command == \"release\":\n1691\t        return cmd_release(args)\n1692\t    if args.command == \"catalog\":\n1693\t        if args.refresh:\n1694\t            args.update = True\n1695\t        return cmd_catalog(args)\n1696\t    if args.command == \"image\":\n1697\t        return cmd_image(args)\n1698\t    return 2\n1699\t\n1700\t\n1701\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n1702\t    sys.exit(main())\n1703\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b63ad6aa-1dd3-487c-8e9f-f16892a2390c","uuid":"7c2e2b95-a301-4446-a41a-ef44fb1d2b39","timestamp":"2026-11-22T01:18:03.483Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.install-workflow-fixes-04-router-plan-path-base-home/tiiny-router/scripts/tiiny_ctl.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"tiiny_ctl.py — the device controller for the Tiiny model-routing skill.\n\nThe routing plugin chooses a model per subtask. This script owns everything\naround that choice: the plan, the cross-agent lock, the load, the restore, and\nthe catalogue refresh.\n\nWhy the split\n-------------\nThe delegation plugin has exactly one hook, ``route_task``, and it is called\nonce per subtask *before* any child runs. There is no \"batch finished\" hook,\nso a plugin cannot know when it is safe to evict a model, and it cannot know\nwhether a sibling agent is already using the device. Loading therefore has to\nlive outside the plugin, in a step the manager agent drives explicitly and can\nsee:\n\n    plan  ->  reserve  ->  load  ->  delegate  ->  release\n\nBetween ``plan`` and ``load`` the plan file (``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``) is\nwhat the two halves share: it records which model was assigned to which goal,\nso the plugin routes the child to the model this script just loaded.\n\nThe lock\n--------\nTiiny is one shared box. Two agents delegating at the same time will fight\nover the same resident models and the same NPU pool. The lock makes that\nvisible instead of silent:\n\n  * ``~/.hermes/tiiny-lock.json`` is the mutex. It is taken with\n    ``flock`` over a file created with ``O_EXCL`` semantics, so two agents\n    cannot both believe they hold it. It expires, so a crashed agent does not\n    lock the device forever.\n  * The vault's ``hot.md`` carries the announcement — the AMC's own board of\n    what is in flight — so agents that are not Hermes profiles can see that\n    the device is spoken for.\n\nThe sandbox this often runs in cannot unlink or rename files. That is why the\nlock is *released by writing a released state into it* rather than by deleting\nit, and why the hold file is truncated rather than removed.\n\nCommands\n--------\n    status                      device, resident set, lock, hold, plan\n    plan --goal G [--goal G]    pick models, check the NPU fit, write the plan\n    reserve --owner NAME        take the lock and announce it\n    load [--model M] [--wave N] load what the batch needs, verify residency\n    release [--force]           restore the device, clear the lock and the plan\n    catalog [--refresh]         diff the live Model Store against the snapshot\n    image \"a job description\"   pick the right image model for the job\n\nExit codes: 0 success, 1 the operation failed, 2 the usage was wrong.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport urllib.error\nimport urllib.request\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# --- paths ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n#: The plugin directory: the routing backend, the tier table and the skill.\nSKILL_ROOT = HERE.parent\nHERMES_HOME = Path(os.environ.get(\"HERMES_HOME\", \"~/.hermes\")).expanduser()\n\ntry:\n    import devices as D\nexcept Exception:\n    try:\n        import importlib.util\n        for cand in (HERE / \"devices.py\", HERMES_HOME / \"mesh\" / \"devices.py\"):\n            if cand.exists():\n                spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"devices\", cand)\n                if spec and spec.loader:\n                    D = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n                    spec.loader.exec_module(D)\n                    break\n        else:\n            D = None\n    except Exception:\n        D = None\n\n\ndef _shared_home() -> Path:\n    \"\"\"The BASE Hermes home, even when HERMES_HOME points at a profile.\n\n    Four files are shared state across every profile: the plan, the\n    cross-agent lock, the hold file, and the catalogue snapshot.\n\n    Two readers live in the base home and will not look anywhere else:\n\n    - the delegation plugin reads the plan from ``~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json``\n      on purpose — \"every profile's manager agent must see the same plan\";\n    - every profile writes the hold to ``~/.hermes/tiiny-hold``, so one\n      agent's record of what it loaded is visible to the next.\n\n    When HERMES_HOME is a profile directory (``.../hermes/profiles/<name>``)\n    a naive ``HERMES_HOME / \"tiiny-hold\"`` writes the hold somewhere the other\n    agents never read, so two agents each believe they hold the device. The\n    shared files have to be resolved against the base home, not the profile.\n    \"\"\"\n    home = HERMES_HOME\n    if home.name != \"profiles\" and home.parent.name == \"profiles\":\n        return home.parent.parent\n    return home\n\n\ndef _dotenv_value(name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Read one value out of the BASE Hermes home's ``.env``.\n\n    The base home, not the profile's. The profile directory is where\n    HERMES_HOME points when a profile runs, and a profile's own .env\n    does not carry the shared settings - the appliance bearer and the\n    vault path live in ``~/.hermes/.env``, where every profile reads\n    them from.\n\n    Reading the profile's .env here is why the lock announcement was\n    silent: ``TIINY_VAULT`` is set in the base home, so the controller\n    never found the vault, never wrote the \"device is BUSY\" notice into\n    hot.md, and every other agent read a board that said the device was\n    free while it was held.\n\n    Nothing is printed but the one value asked for, and it is never\n    logged.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        for line in (_shared_home() / \".env\").read_text().splitlines():\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line.startswith(name + \"=\"):\n                return line.split(\"=\", 1)[1].strip().strip(\"'\\\"\")\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    return \"\"\n\n\ndef _caller_owner() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Who is calling.\n\n    The lock is between AGENTS, not between processes: two Hermes profiles on\n    one machine are two agents sharing one appliance. TIINY_OWNER wins;\n    otherwise the active profile's name, because that is what identifies the\n    agent. Falls back to the OS user.\n\n    An empty string means \"unknown\", and unknown callers are treated as\n    NOT the owner of anything — an unidentified agent must not be able to\n    take a device another agent is holding.\n    \"\"\"\n    env = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\").strip()\n    if env:\n        return env\n    home = HERMES_HOME\n    if home.name != \"profiles\" and home.parent.name == \"profiles\":\n        return home.name\n    return os.environ.get(\"USER\", \"\").strip()\n\n\ndef _lock_holder() -> dict | None:\n    \"\"\"The lock payload while it is held by someone, or None.\n\n    \"Held\" means: state is held AND it has not expired. An expired lock is\n    free for the taking — that is how a crashed agent does not hold the\n    device forever.\n    \"\"\"\n    current = lock_read() or {}\n    if current.get(\"state\") != \"held\":\n        return None\n    try:\n        expires = float(current.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        expires = 0\n    if expires <= time.time():\n        return None\n    return current\n\n\ndef _held_by_other(owner: str, holder: dict | None) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True when the device is held by an agent that is not ``owner``.\n\n    An empty ``owner`` is \"unknown caller\" and never matches the holder, so\n    an agent that cannot identify itself cannot disturb another agent's\n    device.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not holder:\n        return False\n    held_by = holder.get(\"owner\") or \"\"\n    return held_by != owner\n\n\n\ndef _find_vault() -> Path | None:\n    \"\"\"Find the vault root — the directory that holds ``hot.md``.\n\n    The controller runs from two places: the vault's skill directory (the\n    master copy) and an installed copy under ``~/.hermes/scripts``. In the\n    first case the vault is two levels up; in the second it is nowhere near.\n    So look for the marker instead of assuming a depth: the vault root is the\n    directory that has both ``hot.md`` and ``skills/`` in it.\n\n    Three ways to find it, in order: the ``TIINY_VAULT`` environment\n    variable, the same name in ``~/.hermes/.env``, and a walk up from this\n    file. The walk is what finds it for the master copy; the other two are\n    what an installed copy uses.\n\n    No machine path is hard-coded here. This file is published, and a\n    published file must not carry the path of the machine it was written on.\n    \"\"\"\n    override = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_VAULT\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_VAULT\")\n    if override:\n        candidate = Path(override).expanduser()\n        return candidate if candidate.exists() else None\n    for base in (SKILL_ROOT, HERE):\n        for parent in (base, *base.parents):\n            try:\n                if (parent / \"hot.md\").exists() and (parent / \"skills\").exists():\n                    return parent\n            except OSError:\n                continue\n    return None\n\n\nVAULT = _find_vault()\n\n#: Shared state. These live in the BASE Hermes home, not the profile — see\n#: `_shared_home` for why, and for the failure that follows if they do not.\nSHARED_HOME = _shared_home()\n\n\ndef _address_from(url: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The host part of a base URL as a bare address.\n\n    ``http://192.0.2.51/v1`` -> ``192.0.2.51``. A value with no scheme\n    is returned as its first path segment, so ``tiinyhost.local`` survives\n    intact.\n    \"\"\"\n    text = url.strip()\n    if \"://\" in text:\n        text = text.split(\"://\", 1)[1]\n    return text.split(\"/\")[0].split(\":\")[0]\n\n\n_ACTIVE_DEVICE: Any | None = None\n\n\ndef set_active_device(target: str | None = None) -> Any | None:\n    \"\"\"Set and return the active device from the registry.\"\"\"\n    global _ACTIVE_DEVICE, _SESSION_CHECKED\n    _SESSION_CHECKED = None\n    if D is None:\n        _ACTIVE_DEVICE = None\n        return None\n    if target:\n        dev = D.get_device(target)\n        if dev:\n            _ACTIVE_DEVICE = dev\n            return dev\n        reg = D.load_registry()\n        for d in reg.values():\n            if d.address == target:\n                _ACTIVE_DEVICE = d\n                return d\n    env_target = (\n        os.environ.get(\"TIINY_DEVICE_NAME\")\n        or os.environ.get(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\")\n        or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_DEVICE_NAME\")\n        or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\")\n    )\n    if env_target:\n        dev = D.get_device(env_target)\n        if dev:\n            _ACTIVE_DEVICE = dev\n            return dev\n    host_env = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOST\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_HOST\")\n    if host_env:\n        host = _address_from(host_env)\n        if host and host != \"tiinyhost.local\":\n            dev = D.get_device(host)\n            if dev:\n                _ACTIVE_DEVICE = dev\n                return dev\n    reg = D.load_registry()\n    if \"tiiny-ant\" in reg:\n        _ACTIVE_DEVICE = reg[\"tiiny-ant\"]\n        return _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n    elif reg:\n        _ACTIVE_DEVICE = next(iter(reg.values()))\n        return _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n    _ACTIVE_DEVICE = None\n    return None\n\n\ndef active_device() -> Any | None:\n    global _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n    if _ACTIVE_DEVICE is None:\n        set_active_device()\n    return _ACTIVE_DEVICE\n\n\ndef lock_file() -> Path:\n    if os.environ.get(\"TIINY_LOCK_FILE\"):\n        return Path(os.environ[\"TIINY_LOCK_FILE\"]).expanduser()\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        return SHARED_HOME / f\"tiiny-lock-{dev.name}.json\"\n    return SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-lock.json\"\n\n\ndef plan_file() -> Path:\n    if os.environ.get(\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\"):\n        return Path(os.environ[\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\"]).expanduser()\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        return SHARED_HOME / f\"tiiny-plan-{dev.name}.json\"\n    return SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\"\n\n\ndef hold_file() -> Path:\n    if os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOLD_FILE\"):\n        return Path(os.environ[\"TIINY_HOLD_FILE\"]).expanduser()\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        return SHARED_HOME / f\"tiiny-hold-{dev.name}\"\n    return SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-hold\"\n\n\nLOCK_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_LOCK_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-lock.json\")))\nPLAN_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_PLAN_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\")))\nHOLD_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOLD_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-hold\")))\nSNAPSHOT_FILE = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_SNAPSHOT_FILE\", str(SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-catalogue.json\")))\n\nTIINY_BIN = Path(os.environ.get(\"TIINY_BIN\", \"~/.local/tiiny/bin/tiiny\")).expanduser()\n\n\ndef inference_base() -> str:\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        return dev.base_url()\n    return (os.environ.get(\"TIINY_BASE_URL\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_BASE_URL\")\n            or \"http://openai.api.tiiny/v1\").rstrip(\"/\")\n\n\ndef management_base() -> str:\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        return dev.admin_url()\n    return (os.environ.get(\"TIINY_HOST\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_HOST\")\n            or \"http://tiinyhost.local\").rstrip(\"/\")\n\n\nINFERENCE_BASE = inference_base()\nMANAGEMENT_BASE = management_base()\n\n\ndef _cli_config_device() -> str:\n    \"\"\"The device the control CLI is currently pointed at.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        import json\n        path = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.local/tiiny/config.json\")\n        with open(path) as fh:\n            return str(json.load(fh).get(\"deviceAddress\") or \"\")\n    except Exception:\n        return \"\"\n\n\ndef cli_config_path() -> Path | None:\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        p = dev.cli_config_path()\n        if p.exists():\n            return p\n    return None\n\n\ndef device_address() -> str:\n    \"\"\"The address every control call has to name.\"\"\"\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        return dev.address\n    explicit = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\") or _dotenv_value(\"TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS\")\n    if explicit:\n        return explicit.strip()\n    host = _address_from(management_base())\n    if host and host != \"tiinyhost.local\":\n        return host\n    addr = _cli_config_device()\n    if addr:\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: warning: per-device registry not available, falling back to CLI config device {addr}\", file=sys.stderr)\n    return addr\n\n\n#: Probed once per run: (address, is_signed_in).\n_SESSION_CHECKED: tuple[str, bool] | None = None\n\n\ndef cli_session_ok() -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True when the control CLI is signed in for the device it must manage.\"\"\"\n    global _SESSION_CHECKED\n    addr = device_address()\n    cfg_path = cli_config_path()\n    if not addr or not TIINY_BIN.exists():\n        return True\n    cache_key = f\"{addr}:{cfg_path}\"\n    if _SESSION_CHECKED and _SESSION_CHECKED[0] == cache_key:\n        return _SESSION_CHECKED[1]\n    cmd = [str(TIINY_BIN)]\n    if cfg_path:\n        cmd += [\"--config\", str(cfg_path)]\n    cmd += [\"ls\", \"-d\"]\n    if addr and not cfg_path:\n        cmd += [\"--device-address\", addr]\n    try:\n        result = subprocess.run(\n            cmd,\n            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120,\n        )\n    except Exception:\n        return True\n    out = (result.stdout or \"\") + (result.stderr or \"\")\n    signed_in = \"Not logged in\" not in out\n    _SESSION_CHECKED = (cache_key, signed_in)\n    if not signed_in:\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the control CLI is not signed in for {addr}.\", file=sys.stderr)\n        if cfg_path:\n            print(f\"  Using per-device config: {cfg_path}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        else:\n            print(\"  'tiiny login' is a human step, and the CLI holds ONE session\", file=sys.stderr)\n            print(\"  bound to ONE device: signing in here signs the other box out.\", file=sys.stderr)\n    return signed_in\n\n#: The models `release` restores. There is no watchdog any more — the cron\n#: poller was retired on 2026-11-10 and `ensure` replaced it — so this list is\n#: now written in exactly ONE place: this file. Every other copy is a symlink\n#: into this one, which is what stops the set drifting between scripts.\n#:\n#: Speech is NOT here. ASR and TTS moved to Off Grid AI Desktop (the local\n#: Mac gateway) on 2026-11-10, because the appliance serves one heavy stream\n#: at a time — TTS queued behind the appliance's own LLM turn — and because\n#: OGAD's speech models cost no NPU on the device at all. Keeping them here\n#: meant every release reloaded 12% of a shared pool that nothing used.\nREQUIRED_MODELS = (\n    \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\",\n    \"openai/gpt-oss-20b\",\n)\n\n#: The lock's announcement block in hot.md.\nLOCK_MARK_START = \"<!-- tiiny-device-lock:start -->\"\nLOCK_MARK_END = \"<!-- tiiny-device-lock:end -->\"\n\n#: Default lock lifetime, in seconds. Long enough for a long research batch,\n#: short enough that a crashed agent does not lock the device overnight.\nDEFAULT_TTL = 1800\n\n\ndef _load_tiers():\n    \"\"\"Load the shared tier table by path (see tiers.py for why).\n\n    The table sits in the plugin directory, one level above this script,\n    and is found relative to this file only.\n    \"\"\"\n    import importlib.util\n\n    path = SKILL_ROOT / \"tiers.py\"\n    if not path.exists():\n        raise SystemExit(f\"could not find the tier table at {path}\")\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"tiiny_tiers\", path)\n    if spec is None or spec.loader is None:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"could not load the tier table from {path}\")\n    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(module)\n    return module\n\n\ntiers = _load_tiers()\n\n\n# --- device access ----------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef device_key() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the device bearer, or an empty string.\n\n    Tried in order: explicit environment variable TIINY_API_KEY, the active\n    device's configured key_env via the registry, ``~/.hermes/.env`` (where\n    Hermes keeps it), and the vault's ``pocketlab/CREDENTIALS.md``.\n    The value is never printed.\n    \"\"\"\n    value = os.environ.get(\"TIINY_API_KEY\", \"\").strip()\n    if value:\n        return value\n\n    dev = active_device()\n    if dev:\n        k = dev.key(env_file=_shared_home() / \".env\")\n        if k:\n            return k\n\n    dotenv = _shared_home() / \".env\"\n    try:\n        for line in dotenv.read_text().splitlines():\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line.startswith(\"TIINY_API_KEY=\"):\n                value = line.split(\"=\", 1)[1].strip().strip(\"'\\\"\")\n                if value:\n                    return value\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\n    credentials = (VAULT / \"pocketlab\" / \"CREDENTIALS.md\") if VAULT else None\n    try:\n        match = re.search(\n            r\"TIINY_API_KEY=\\s*([0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12})\",\n            credentials.read_text(),\n        )\n        if match:\n            return match.group(1)\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    return \"\"\n\n\ndef _request(url: str, key: str, timeout: int = 20) -> dict:\n    request = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\"})\n    try:\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=timeout) as response:\n            return json.loads(response.read())\n    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"HTTP {exc.code} from {url}: {exc.read().decode('utf8', 'replace')[:200]}\")\n    except Exception as exc:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"could not reach {url}: {exc}\")\n\n\ndef resident_ids() -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"The model ids the inference endpoint can serve right now.\"\"\"\n    key = device_key()\n    if not key:\n        return []\n    payload = _request(inference_base() + \"/models\", key, timeout=10)\n    ids = []\n    for entry in payload.get(\"data\") or []:\n        value = entry.get(\"id\") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None\n        if isinstance(value, str) and \"/\" in value:\n            ids.append(value)\n    return sorted(ids)\n\n\ndef catalogue() -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"The live Model Store, normalised to the fields the skill cares about.\"\"\"\n    key = device_key()\n    if not key:\n        raise SystemExit(\"no device key found (TIINY_API_KEY, ~/.hermes/.env, or the vault)\")\n    payload = _request(management_base() + \"/api/v1/models/online_models\", key, timeout=30)\n    items = payload[\"items\"] if isinstance(payload, dict) and \"items\" in payload else payload\n    rows = []\n    for item in items if isinstance(items, list) else []:\n        if not isinstance(item, dict):\n            continue\n        rows.append({\n            \"id\": item.get(\"fullname\"),\n            \"name\": item.get(\"name\"),\n            \"type\": item.get(\"type\"),\n            \"npu\": item.get(\"npu_usage\"),\n            \"context\": item.get(\"context_window\"),\n            \"status\": item.get(\"status\"),\n            \"desc\": (item.get(\"desc\") or \"\").strip(),\n        })\n    rows.sort(key=lambda r: (str(r.get(\"type\")), -(r.get(\"npu\") or 0), str(r.get(\"id\"))))\n    return rows\n\n\ndef catalogue_index() -> dict[str, dict]:\n    \"\"\"The live catalogue keyed by model id.\"\"\"\n    return {row[\"id\"]: row for row in catalogue() if row.get(\"id\")}\n\n\ndef npu_of(model: str, index: dict[str, dict] | None = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"A model's NPU share, from the device when possible, else the table.\"\"\"\n    if index and model in index and index[model].get(\"npu\") is not None:\n        return int(index[model][\"npu\"])\n    value = tiers.model_npu(model)\n    return int(value) if value is not None else 0\n\n\n# --- device state -----------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef http_load(model: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Load a model via the device's native HTTP lifecycle API.\"\"\"\n    dev = active_device()\n    if not dev:\n        return False\n    key = dev.key()\n    if not key:\n        return False\n    url = dev.admin_url(f\"/api/v1/models/{model}/start\")\n    req = urllib.request.Request(\n        url,\n        data=b\"{}\",\n        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n        method=\"POST\",\n    )\n    try:\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as resp:\n            if resp.status == 200:\n                t0 = time.time()\n                while time.time() - t0 < 180:\n                    status, running = D.probe_running(dev, timeout=5) if D else (0, [])\n                    if model in running:\n                        return True\n                    time.sleep(2)\n                return False\n    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:\n        if exc.code == 401:\n            print(f\"tiiny_ctl: HTTP 401 auth_failed for {dev.name} via {dev.key_env}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return False\n        return False\n    except Exception:\n        return False\n    return False\n\n\ndef http_unload(model: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Unload a model via the device's native HTTP lifecycle API.\"\"\"\n    dev = active_device()\n    if not dev:\n        return False\n    key = dev.key()\n    if not key:\n        return False\n    url = dev.admin_url(f\"/api/v1/models/{model}/stop\")\n    req = urllib.request.Request(\n        url,\n        data=b\"{}\",\n        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n        method=\"POST\",\n    )\n    try:\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as resp:\n            if resp.status == 200:\n                t0 = time.time()\n                while time.time() - t0 < 60:\n                    status, running = D.probe_running(dev, timeout=5) if D else (0, [])\n                    if model not in running:\n                        return True\n                    time.sleep(1)\n                return True\n    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:\n        if exc.code == 401:\n            print(f\"tiiny_ctl: HTTP 401 auth_failed for {dev.name} via {dev.key_env}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return False\n        return False\n    except Exception:\n        return False\n    return False\n\n\ndef cli_load(model: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Load a model onto the device.\n\n    Prefers HTTP lifecycle (/api/v1/models/{model}/start) which is fast,\n    needs no pty, and isolates credentials. Falls back to CLI with isolated\n    per-device configuration if HTTP lifecycle is unavailable.\n    \"\"\"\n    if http_load(model):\n        return True\n\n    if not TIINY_BIN.exists():\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the tiiny CLI is missing at {TIINY_BIN}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    addr = device_address()\n    cfg_path = cli_config_path()\n    if not cli_session_ok():\n        return False\n    cmd = [\"script\", \"-q\", \"/dev/null\", str(TIINY_BIN)]\n    if cfg_path:\n        cmd += [\"--config\", str(cfg_path)]\n    cmd += [\"load\", model, \"-y\"]\n    if addr and not cfg_path:\n        cmd += [\"--device-address\", addr]\n    try:\n        result = subprocess.run(\n            cmd,\n            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=900,\n        )\n    except Exception as exc:\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: load of {model} failed: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    if result.returncode != 0:\n        tail = (result.stderr or result.stdout or \"\").strip().splitlines()[-3:]\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: load of {model} failed: {' '.join(tail)}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    return True\n\n\ndef cli_unload(model: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Unload a model from the device, freeing its NPU share.\n\n    Prefers HTTP lifecycle (/api/v1/models/{model}/stop), falls back to CLI.\n    \"\"\"\n    if http_unload(model):\n        return True\n\n    if not TIINY_BIN.exists():\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the tiiny CLI is missing at {TIINY_BIN}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    addr = device_address()\n    cfg_path = cli_config_path()\n    cmd = [\"script\", \"-q\", \"/dev/null\", str(TIINY_BIN)]\n    if cfg_path:\n        cmd += [\"--config\", str(cfg_path)]\n    cmd += [\"unload\", model, \"-y\"]\n    if addr and not cfg_path:\n        cmd += [\"--device-address\", addr]\n    try:\n        result = subprocess.run(\n            cmd,\n            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300,\n        )\n    except Exception as exc:\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: unload of {model} failed: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    if result.returncode != 0:\n        tail = (result.stderr or result.stdout or \"\").strip().splitlines()[-3:]\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: unload of {model} failed: {' '.join(tail)}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return False\n    return True\n\n\ndef ensure_room(wave: list[str], index: dict[str, dict] | None = None) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:\n    \"\"\"Decide what must be evicted so a wave's models can all be resident.\n\n    Returns ``(to evict, kept)``.\n\n    **Why the controller decides this and not the device.** When a load does\n    not fit, the device picks what to evict, and it does not know what the\n    batch needs. Left to itself it kept the 47% generalist and evicted the\n    27% model the wave had just asked for — so the wave's own model was\n    missing and its child would have answered ``404 \"is not loaded\"``.\n\n    So the controller works out what stays before it loads anything: the\n    wave's models are sacred, and of everything else the cheapest are kept\n    while they fit in what is left. A 5% TTS model is worth keeping; a 47%\n    generalist is not, when a wave needs the pool. Everything dropped here\n    is restored by ``release``.\n    \"\"\"\n    resident = resident_ids()\n    wave_set = set(wave)\n    wave_share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in wave)\n    budget = 100 - wave_share\n\n    kept: list[str] = []\n    kept_share = 0\n    evict: list[str] = []\n    others = [m for m in resident if m not in wave_set]\n    for model in sorted(others, key=lambda m: npu_of(m, index)):\n        share = npu_of(model, index)\n        if kept_share + share <= budget:\n            kept.append(model)\n            kept_share += share\n        else:\n            evict.append(model)\n    return evict, kept\n\n\ndef hold_write(models: list[str]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Record what this agent deliberately loaded.\"\"\"\n    hf = hold_file()\n    hf.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    hf.write_text(\"\".join(f\"{m}\\n\" for m in models))\n\n\ndef hold_clear() -> None:\n    \"\"\"Release the hold.\"\"\"\n    hf = hold_file()\n    try:\n        hf.write_text(\"\")\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    try:\n        hf.unlink()\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    try:\n        if hf.exists() and hf.stat().st_size > 0:\n            raise SystemExit(\n                f\"tiiny_ctl: could not release the hold at {hf} — \"\n                \"`status` will keep reporting it as held. Truncate it by hand.\"\n            )\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        pass\n\n\ndef hold_state() -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"The models the hold file names.\"\"\"\n    hf = hold_file()\n    try:\n        return [line.strip() for line in hf.read_text().splitlines() if line.strip()]\n    except Exception:\n        return []\n\n\n# --- the lock ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef lock_read() -> dict | None:\n    \"\"\"The lock's current contents, or None if there is no lock file.\"\"\"\n    lf = lock_file()\n    try:\n        return json.loads(lf.read_text())\n    except Exception:\n        return None\n\n\ndef _lock_fd():\n    lf = lock_file()\n    lf.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    return os.open(lf, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, 0o600)\n\n\ndef _lock_write(fd: int, payload: dict) -> None:\n    data = json.dumps(payload, indent=1).encode()\n    os.ftruncate(fd, 0)\n    os.lseek(fd, 0, os.SEEK_SET)\n    os.write(fd, data)\n    os.fsync(fd)\n\n\ndef lock_acquire(owner: str, task: str, ttl: int, models: list[str] | None = None) -> tuple[dict | None, str]:\n    \"\"\"Take the lock. Returns ``(payload, error)`` — payload is None on failure.\n\n    The critical section is an ``flock`` on the lock file, so two agents\n    racing for it are serialised by the kernel rather than by hope. The lock\n    itself outlives the process: it is the file's contents, and it expires.\n    \"\"\"\n    fd = _lock_fd()\n    try:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n        except OSError:\n            return None, \"another process is holding the lock right now\"\n\n        now = time.time()\n        current = lock_read()\n        if current and current.get(\"state\") == \"held\":\n            expires = float(current.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n            if expires > now:\n                if current.get(\"owner\") == owner:\n                    # RENEW. The holder extends its own lock. Without this a\n                    # long build has to either release the device mid-build\n                    # or lose the device to an agent that takes over when the\n                    # TTL runs out.\n                    payload = dict(current)\n                    payload[\"expires\"] = now + ttl\n                    payload[\"models\"] = models or current.get(\"models\") or []\n                    payload[\"task\"] = task or current.get(\"task\", \"\")\n                    payload[\"pid\"] = os.getpid()\n                    _lock_write(fd, payload)\n                    return payload, \"\"\n                return None, (\n                    f\"the device is held by {current.get('owner')} \"\n                    f\"({current.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) \"\n                    f\"until {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))}\"\n                )\n            print(\n                f\"tiiny_ctl: taking over an expired lock from {current.get('owner')} \"\n                f\"(expired {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))}).\"\n            )\n\n        payload = {\n            \"state\": \"held\",\n            \"owner\": owner,\n            \"task\": task,\n            \"models\": models or [],\n            \"created\": now,\n            \"expires\": now + ttl,\n            \"pid\": os.getpid(),\n        }\n        _lock_write(fd, payload)\n        return payload, \"\"\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\ndef lock_release(owner: str, force: bool = False) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n    \"\"\"Release the lock. The file stays; its contents record the release.\"\"\"\n    fd = _lock_fd()\n    try:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)\n        except OSError:\n            return False, \"another process is holding the lock right now\"\n        current = lock_read() or {}\n        if current.get(\"state\") == \"held\":\n            # Strict: the owner must match exactly. An empty owner is not a\n            # wildcard — it is \"unknown caller\", and an unknown caller must\n            # not be able to release somebody else's lock.\n            if not force and current.get(\"owner\") != owner:\n                return False, (\n                    f\"the lock is held by {current.get('owner')}, not {owner}. \"\n                    \"Use --force if that is wrong.\"\n                )\n        _lock_write(fd, {\n            \"state\": \"released\",\n            \"owner\": current.get(\"owner\", owner),\n            \"task\": current.get(\"task\", \"\"),\n            \"released\": time.time(),\n            \"pid\": os.getpid(),\n        })\n        return True, \"\"\n    finally:\n        try:\n            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        finally:\n            os.close(fd)\n\n\n# --- the announcement -------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef _hot_md() -> Path | None:\n    if VAULT is None:\n        return None\n    hot = VAULT / \"hot.md\"\n    return hot if hot.exists() else None\n\n\ndef announce_set(text: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Write the announcement block into hot.md. Returns True if it landed.\"\"\"\n    hot = _hot_md()\n    if hot is None:\n        return False\n    body = hot.read_text()\n    block = f\"{LOCK_MARK_START}\\n{text}\\n{LOCK_MARK_END}\"\n    if LOCK_MARK_START in body and LOCK_MARK_END in body:\n        head = body[: body.index(LOCK_MARK_START)]\n        tail = body[body.index(LOCK_MARK_END) + len(LOCK_MARK_END):]\n        updated = head + block + tail\n    else:\n        updated = body.rstrip() + \"\\n\\n\" + block + \"\\n\"\n    updated = re.sub(r\"(?m)^updated:.*$\", f\"updated: {time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}\", updated, count=1)\n    hot.write_text(updated)\n    return True\n\n\ndef announce_clear() -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Remove the announcement block from hot.md.\"\"\"\n    hot = _hot_md()\n    if hot is None:\n        return False\n    body = hot.read_text()\n    if LOCK_MARK_START not in body or LOCK_MARK_END not in body:\n        return False\n    head = body[: body.index(LOCK_MARK_START)]\n    tail = body[body.index(LOCK_MARK_END) + len(LOCK_MARK_END):]\n    hot.write_text((head.rstrip() + \"\\n\\n\" + tail.lstrip()).rstrip() + \"\\n\")\n    return True\n\n\n# --- the plan ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef plan_write(entries: list[dict], waves: list[list[str]], owner: str, task: str) -> dict:\n    payload = {\n        \"batch\": time.strftime(\"%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ\", time.gmtime()) + f\"-{os.getpid():x}\",\n        \"created\": time.time(),\n        \"owner\": owner,\n        \"task\": task,\n        \"entries\": entries,\n        \"waves\": waves,\n    }\n    target_plan = plan_file()\n    target_plan.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    target_plan.write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=1))\n    default_plan = SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\"\n    if target_plan != default_plan:\n        try:\n            default_plan.write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=1))\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return payload\n\n\ndef plan_read() -> dict | None:\n    try:\n        return json.loads(plan_file().read_text())\n    except Exception:\n        try:\n            return json.loads((SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\").read_text())\n        except Exception:\n            return None\n\n\ndef plan_clear() -> None:\n    target_plan = plan_file()\n    try:\n        target_plan.write_text(\"\")\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    try:\n        target_plan.unlink()\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    default_plan = SHARED_HOME / \"tiiny-plan.json\"\n    if target_plan != default_plan:\n        try:\n            default_plan.write_text(\"\")\n            default_plan.unlink()\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n\n# --- commands ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef cmd_status(args) -> int:\n    dev = active_device()\n    dev_hdr = f\" — {dev.name} ({dev.address})\" if dev else \"\"\n    print(f\"TIINY DEVICE STATUS{dev_hdr}\\n\")\n\n    key = device_key()\n    key_src = f\" ({dev.key_env})\" if dev and dev.key_env else \"\"\n    print(f\"  key        : {'found' + key_src if key else 'NOT FOUND'}\")\n    print(f\"  inference  : {inference_base()}\")\n    print(f\"  management : {management_base()}\")\n\n    try:\n        resident = resident_ids()\n    except SystemExit as exc:\n        print(f\"  device     : UNREACHABLE — {exc}\")\n        resident = []\n    else:\n        print(f\"  device     : reachable\")\n\n    index = {}\n    try:\n        index = catalogue_index()\n    except SystemExit:\n        pass\n\n    if resident:\n        print(f\"\\n  RESIDENT ({len(resident)} models, \"\n              f\"{sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in resident)}% NPU):\")\n        for model in resident:\n            print(f\"    {npu_of(model, index):>3}%  {model}\")\n    else:\n        print(\"\\n  RESIDENT: none known\")\n\n    lock = lock_read() or {}\n    state = lock.get(\"state\", \"none\")\n    if state == \"held\":\n        expires = float(lock.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n        remaining = int(expires - time.time())\n        print(f\"\\n  LOCK: HELD by {lock.get('owner')} — {lock.get('task') or 'no task'} \"\n              f\"({remaining}s left)\")\n        print(f\"        models: {', '.join(lock.get('models') or []) or '(none recorded)'}\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"\\n  LOCK: {state}\")\n\n    held = hold_state()\n    print(f\"  HOLD: {', '.join(held) if held else '(none)'}\")\n\n    plan = plan_read()\n    if plan and plan.get(\"entries\"):\n        print(f\"\\n  PLAN {plan.get('batch')}: {len(plan['entries'])} task(s), \"\n              f\"{len(plan.get('waves') or [])} wave(s), owner {plan.get('owner')}\")\n    else:\n        print(\"\\n  PLAN: none\")\n\n    return 0\n\n\ndef cmd_plan(args) -> int:\n    goals = list(args.goal or [])\n    if not goals:\n        print(\"tiiny_ctl: plan needs at least one --goal\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    index = catalogue_index()\n    resident = set(resident_ids())\n\n    # Which candidate can this device actually run? A model that is not\n    # downloaded cannot be loaded, so choosing it would produce a plan that\n    # fails at the load step.\n    def candidates(tier: str) -> list[str]:\n        return [name for name, _npu, _ctx in tiers.TIER_CHOICES[tier]]\n\n    entries = []\n    for goal in goals:\n        tier = args.tier or tiers.detect_tier(goal)\n        if tier not in tiers.TIER_MODEL:\n            print(f\"tiiny_ctl: unknown tier {tier!r}. Valid: {', '.join(tiers.VALID_TIERS)}\",\n                  file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2\n        forced_model = None\n        if args.model:\n            forced_model = args.model\n        chosen = None\n        reason = \"\"\n        for name in candidates(tier):\n            if forced_model and name != forced_model:\n                continue\n            status = (index.get(name) or {}).get(\"status\")\n            if name in resident:\n                chosen, reason = name, \"resident\"\n                break\n            if status in (\"downloaded\", \"running\"):\n                chosen, reason = name, \"downloaded\"\n                break\n        if chosen is None:\n            chosen = tiers.GENERALIST\n            reason = \"no downloadable candidate — the generalist\"\n        entries.append({\n            \"goal\": goal,\n            \"tier\": tier,\n            \"model\": chosen,\n            \"npu\": npu_of(chosen, index),\n            \"reason\": reason,\n        })\n\n    # Waves: the device serves one generation-heavy stream at a time, and the\n    # models of a wave must fit inside one shared NPU pool. A batch whose\n    # models do not fit is split into waves, and each wave is a separate\n    # delegation call.\n    distinct: list[str] = []\n    for entry in entries:\n        if entry[\"model\"] not in distinct:\n            distinct.append(entry[\"model\"])\n\n    # First-fit decreasing, with the models that are already resident placed\n    # first. Resident-first matters: a wave that loads a new specialist\n    # evicts whatever does not fit, so keeping the already-loaded model in\n    # the first wave saves a load and a reload. Ties break on the larger\n    # model, because a large model is the one that constrains the packing.\n    ordered = sorted(distinct, key=lambda m: (0 if m in resident else 1, -npu_of(m, index)))\n    waves: list[list[str]] = []\n    for model in ordered:\n        share = npu_of(model, index)\n        placed = False\n        for wave in waves:\n            if sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in wave) + share <= 100:\n                wave.append(model)\n                placed = True\n                break\n        if not placed:\n            waves.append([model])\n\n    for entry in entries:\n        for number, wave in enumerate(waves, start=1):\n            if entry[\"model\"] in wave:\n                entry[\"wave\"] = number\n                break\n\n    payload = plan_write(entries, waves, args.owner or \"unassigned\", args.task or \"\")\n\n    print(f\"BATCH PLAN  {payload['batch']}\")\n    print(f\"  owner: {payload['owner']}    task: {payload['task'] or '(none recorded)'}\\n\")\n    print(f\"  {'#':<3}{'tier':<10}{'model':<42}{'NPU':>5}  {'wave':<5}{'state'}\")\n    print(f\"  {'-'*78}\")\n    for number, entry in enumerate(entries, start=1):\n        print(f\"  {number:<3}{entry['tier']:<10}{entry['model']:<42}\"\n              f\"{entry['npu']:>4}%  {entry.get('wave', 1):<5}{entry['reason']}\")\n\n    print()\n    for number, wave in enumerate(waves, start=1):\n        share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in wave)\n        fits = \"fits\" if share <= 100 else \"DOES NOT FIT\"\n        evict, kept = ensure_room(wave, index)\n        print(f\"  WAVE {number}: {share}% NPU — {fits}\")\n        for model in wave:\n            print(f\"      {model}\")\n        if evict:\n            print(f\"      evicts to make room: {', '.join(evict)}\")\n        if kept:\n            print(f\"      stays alongside: {', '.join(kept)}\")\n        print(\"      (displacement is computed from the residency now; the\")\n        print(\"       controller re-decides it when it actually loads)\")\n\n    resident_share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in resident)\n    batch_share = sum(npu_of(m, index) for m in distinct)\n    displaced = [m for m in resident if m not in distinct]\n    print(f\"\\n  batch models total : {batch_share}% NPU\")\n    print(f\"  resident now       : {resident_share}% NPU across {len(resident)} models\")\n    if displaced:\n        print(f\"  may be displaced   : {', '.join(displaced)}\")\n        print(\"      (release restores the required set when the batch is done)\")\n    print(\"\\n  The device serves one generation-heavy stream at a time: run one\")\n    print(\"  heavy child per wave, not every wave's children at once.\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef cmd_reserve(args) -> int:\n    payload, error = lock_acquire(args.owner, args.task or \"\", args.ttl, args.model or [])\n    if payload is None:\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: cannot take the lock — {error}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    held = \", \".join(args.model or []) or \"(not decided yet)\"\n    text = (\n        \"**Tiiny is BUSY — do not schedule device work.**\\n\"\n        f\"Held by `{payload['owner']}` for {payload['task'] or 'an unnamed task'} \"\n        f\"since {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S')}. Models: {held}. \"\n        f\"Lock expires {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(payload['expires']))}. \"\n        \"This notice disappears when the device is released.\"\n    )\n    announced = announce_set(text)\n\n    print(f\"lock held by {payload['owner']} until \"\n          f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(payload['expires']))}\")\n    print(f\"  announcement: {'hot.md updated' if announced else 'hot.md not reachable — skipped'}\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef cmd_load(args) -> int:\n    # GUARD FIRST. A load is a swap on a device with one shared NPU pool, so\n    # loading while another agent holds the device silently evicts whatever\n    # that agent deliberately loaded. Check before touching anything.\n    owner = args.owner or _caller_owner()\n    holder = _lock_holder()\n    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not args.force:\n        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the device is held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n              f\"({holder.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) until \"\n              f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))} — \"\n              f\"refusing to load. Use --force if that is wrong.\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    models = list(args.model or [])\n    if not models:\n        plan = plan_read()\n        if not plan:\n            print(\"tiiny_ctl: no --model and no plan. Run 'plan' first.\", file=sys.stderr)\n            return 2\n        waves = plan.get(\"waves\") or []\n        if args.wave:\n            if args.wave < 1 or args.wave > len(waves):\n                print(f\"tiiny_ctl: wave {args.wave} is outside this plan \"\n                      f\"(1..{len(waves)})\", file=sys.stderr)\n                return 2\n            models = list(waves[args.wave - 1])\n        else:\n            models = [model for wave in waves for model in wave]\n\n    if not models:\n        print(\"tiiny_ctl: nothing to load\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    before = set(resident_ids())\n\n    # Record the batch before loading, so `status` shows what is resident\n    # because of this run rather than as a leftover from an earlier one.\n    hold_write(models)\n\n    # Make room explicitly. When a load does not fit, the device picks what to\n    # evict and it does not know what the batch needs — left to itself it\n    # kept the 47% generalist and evicted the 27% model the wave had just\n    # asked for. The controller decides what stays.\n    index = {}\n    try:\n        index = catalogue_index()\n    except SystemExit:\n        pass\n    evict, kept = ensure_room(models, index)\n    for model in evict:\n        print(f\"  evicting {model} to make room for the wave\", flush=True)\n        cli_unload(model)\n    if evict or kept:\n        print(f\"  staying alongside: {', '.join(kept) if kept else '(nothing)'}\")\n\n    for model in models:\n        if model in resident_ids():\n            print(f\"  already resident: {model}\")\n            continue\n        print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n        cli_load(model)\n\n    after = set(resident_ids())\n    missing = [m for m in models if m not in after]\n    evicted = sorted(before - after)\n\n    landed = [m for m in models if m not in missing]\n    if landed:\n        print(f\"\\n  loaded      : {', '.join(landed)}\")\n    if missing:\n        # Never print \"loaded: X\" next to \"NOT RESIDENT: X\". The old wording\n        # claimed the load and then denied it in the same breath, which is\n        # exactly the ambiguity that lets a batch fall back to the paid\n        # provider without anybody noticing.\n        print(f\"\\n  NOT RESIDENT: {', '.join(missing)}\")\n    if evicted:\n        print(f\"  displaced   : {', '.join(evicted)}\")\n    print(f\"  hold        : {', '.join(hold_state())}\")\n\n    if missing:\n        print(\"\\n  A model that did not land cannot answer: the device will return\")\n        print(\"  404 \\\"is not loaded\\\" and the child will fall back to the paid\")\n        print(\"  provider. If the CLI says 'Not logged in', the device session is\")\n        print(\"  logged out and 'tiiny login' is a human step. Check that the\")\n        print(\"  model is downloaded, and that its NPU share leaves room for what\")\n        print(\"  else the wave needs.\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n    return 0\n\n\ndef ensure_model(model: str, owner: str | None = None,\n                 force: bool = False, quiet: bool = False) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n    \"\"\"Make a model resident on demand and return (success, detail).\n\n    This is the machine-readable interface used by the delegation router.\n    Calls ``cmd_ensure``'s logic directly (no subprocess, no CLI) so the\n    router can call it inside ``route_task``.\n\n    * **Idempotent.** A model already resident returns ``(True, \"resident\")``.\n    * **Guard-aware.** Refuses while another agent holds the lock.\n    * **Evicts only what this caller may evict.** Explicit eviction, not\n      device-guessed.\n    * **Verifies.** Returns ``(False, \"NOT RESIDENT: <model>\")`` when the\n      device answers 404.\n\n    Returns ``(True, \"resident\")`` when the model is already running,\n    ``(True, \"loaded\")`` when a load was needed and succeeded,\n    ``(False, \"locked: ...\")`` when the device is held by another agent,\n    ``(False, \"NOT RESIDENT: <model>\")`` when the model failed to load.\n    \"\"\"\n    owner = owner or _caller_owner()\n    holder = _lock_holder()\n    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not force:\n        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n        detail = (f\"locked: device held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n                  f\"until {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))}\")\n        return False, detail\n\n    resident = set(resident_ids())\n    if model in resident:\n        return True, \"resident\"\n\n    # Record what this call loaded.\n    hold_write([model])\n\n    index = {}\n    try:\n        index = catalogue_index()\n    except SystemExit:\n        pass\n\n    evict, kept = ensure_room([model], index)\n    for m in evict:\n        if not quiet:\n            print(f\"  evicting {m} to make room\", flush=True)\n        cli_unload(m)\n    if kept and not quiet:\n        print(f\"  staying alongside: {', '.join(kept)}\")\n\n    if not quiet:\n        print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n    cli_load(model)\n\n    after = set(resident_ids())\n    if model in after:\n        return True, \"loaded\"\n\n    return False, f\"NOT RESIDENT: {model}\"\n\n\ndef cmd_ensure(args) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Make models resident on demand — the replacement for the watchdog.\n\n    The appliance unloads its models on reboot, and nothing else puts them\n    back. A five-minute cron poll used to do it by restoring a fixed set,\n    which is what made that set the single most-drifted thing in the skill:\n    three scripts each carried a copy and each could re-assert an old one.\n\n    This is the demand-driven version. An agent that is about to delegate\n    calls `ensure <model>` and gets the model it actually needs, instead of\n    waiting for a poll to restore a set it may not want.\n\n    The contract:\n\n    * **Idempotent.** A model already resident is reported and not reloaded,\n      so calling it before every batch costs one HTTP round trip.\n    * **Guard-aware.** Refuses while another agent holds the lock, for the\n      same reason `load` does: a load is a swap, and swapping under another\n      agent's feet evicts the model its child is running on.\n    * **Evicts only what this caller may evict.** If the model does not fit,\n      the controller names what it will displace and unloads it explicitly.\n      It never leaves the choice to the device, which has no idea what the\n      next child needs.\n    * **Verifies.** A model that did not land is reported as a failure, not a\n      success, because the device answers `404 \"is not loaded\"` and Hermes\n      then falls back to the paid provider — silently.\n    \"\"\"\n    owner = args.owner or _caller_owner()\n    holder = _lock_holder()\n    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not args.force:\n        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the device is held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n              f\"({holder.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) until \"\n              f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))} — \"\n              f\"refusing to ensure. Use --force if that is wrong.\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    wanted = [m for m in (args.model or []) if m]\n    if not wanted:\n        print(\"tiiny_ctl: ensure needs at least one --model\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    resident = set(resident_ids())\n    missing = [m for m in wanted if m not in resident]\n    if not missing:\n        print(f\"  already resident: {', '.join(wanted)}\")\n        return 0\n\n    before = set(resident)\n\n    # Record what this call loaded. There is no watchdog to fight any more,\n    # so this is bookkeeping rather than an interlock.\n    hold_write(missing)\n\n    index = {}\n    try:\n        index = catalogue_index()\n    except SystemExit:\n        pass\n\n    evict, kept = ensure_room(missing, index)\n    for model in evict:\n        print(f\"  evicting {model} to make room\", flush=True)\n        cli_unload(model)\n    if kept:\n        print(f\"  staying alongside: {', '.join(kept)}\")\n\n    for model in missing:\n        print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n        cli_load(model)\n\n    after = set(resident_ids())\n    still_missing = [m for m in wanted if m not in after]\n    displaced = sorted(before - after)\n\n    print(f\"\\n  ensured     : {', '.join(wanted)}\")\n    if displaced:\n        print(f\"  displaced   : {', '.join(displaced)}\")\n    if still_missing:\n        print(f\"  NOT RESIDENT: {', '.join(still_missing)}\")\n        print(\"\\n  A model that did not land cannot answer: the device returns\"\n              \"\\n  404 \\\"is not loaded\\\" and the child falls back to the paid\"\n              \"\\n  provider. Check that it is downloaded, and that its NPU share\"\n              \"\\n  leaves room for what else must stay.\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n    return 0\n\n\ndef cmd_release(args) -> int:\n    # GUARD FIRST, and this used to be the whole bug.\n    #\n    # The old order was: clear the hold, restore the required set, THEN\n    # check the lock. So another agent running `release` cleared the hold and\n    # evicted the held model before it discovered the lock was not its own.\n    # Worse, the old code defaulted the owner to the lock's current owner, so\n    # a bare `release` adopted somebody else's lock and released it happily.\n    #\n    # Ownership is now checked before ANY mutation, and the owner is never\n    # inherited from the lock.\n    owner = args.owner or _caller_owner()\n    holder = _lock_holder()\n    if holder and _held_by_other(owner, holder) and not args.force:\n        expires = float(holder.get(\"expires\") or 0)\n        print(f\"tiiny_ctl: the device is held by {holder.get('owner')} \"\n              f\"({holder.get('task') or 'no task recorded'}) until \"\n              f\"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(expires))} — \"\n              f\"refusing to release. Use --force if that is wrong.\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    # Clear the hold before restoring, so `status` reports the restore's own\n    # result rather than the batch's record of what it had loaded.\n    hold_clear()\n    print(\"hold cleared\")\n\n    resident = set(resident_ids())\n    missing = [m for m in REQUIRED_MODELS if m not in resident]\n    for model in missing:\n        print(f\"  restoring {model} ...\", flush=True)\n        cli_load(model)\n\n    restored = set(resident_ids())\n    still_missing = [m for m in REQUIRED_MODELS if m not in restored]\n    if still_missing:\n        print(f\"  WARNING: still missing {', '.join(still_missing)}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    plan_clear()\n    print(\"plan cleared\")\n\n    ok, error = lock_release(owner, force=args.force)\n    print(f\"lock released\" if ok else f\"lock NOT released — {error}\")\n\n    cleared = announce_clear()\n    print(f\"announcement: {'removed from hot.md' if cleared else 'nothing to remove'}\")\n\n    print(f\"\\nresident now: {', '.join(sorted(restored)) or '(none)'}\")\n    return 0 if ok and not still_missing else 1\n\n\ndef cmd_catalog(args) -> int:\n    rows = catalogue()\n    index = {row[\"id\"]: row for row in rows if row.get(\"id\")}\n    current = {rid: row for rid, row in index.items()}\n\n    previous = {}\n    try:\n        previous = json.loads(SNAPSHOT_FILE.read_text()).get(\"models\", {})\n    except Exception:\n        previous = {}\n\n    print(f\"MODEL STORE: {len(current)} models\\n\")\n\n    if not previous:\n        print(\"  No snapshot to compare against. Run with --update to record one.\")\n        for row in rows:\n            print(f\"    {str(row.get('npu')):>4}%  {row.get('status'):<16} {row['id']}\")\n        return 0\n\n    new = sorted(set(current) - set(previous))\n    gone = sorted(set(previous) - set(current))\n    changed = []\n    for rid in sorted(set(current) & set(previous)):\n        before, after = previous[rid], current[rid]\n        for field in (\"npu\", \"context\", \"status\"):\n            if before.get(field) != after.get(field):\n                changed.append((rid, field, before.get(field), after.get(field)))\n                break\n\n    print(f\"  snapshot had {len(previous)} models; the store has {len(current)}\")\n    if new:\n        print(f\"\\n  NEW ({len(new)}):\")\n        for rid in new:\n            row = current[rid]\n            print(f\"    + {rid}  [{row.get('type')}] {row.get('npu')}% NPU, \"\n                  f\"status {row.get('status')}\")\n    if gone:\n        print(f\"\\n  GONE ({len(gone)}):\")\n        for rid in gone:\n            print(f\"    - {rid}\")\n    if changed:\n        print(f\"\\n  CHANGED ({len(changed)}):\")\n        for rid, field, before, after in changed:\n            print(f\"    ~ {rid}: {field} {before} -> {after}\")\n    if not (new or gone or changed):\n        print(\"\\n  Nothing changed.\")\n\n    tier_models = {name for choices in tiers.TIER_CHOICES.values() for name, _n, _c in choices}\n    tier_drift = [rid for rid, *_ in changed if rid in tier_models]\n    if tier_drift:\n        print(f\"\\n  The tier table quotes figures for these models, which just changed:\")\n        for rid in tier_drift:\n            print(f\"    - {rid}\")\n\n    if args.update:\n        SNAPSHOT_FILE.write_text(json.dumps({\n            \"source\": management_base() + \"/api/v1/models/online_models\",\n            \"read\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"),\n            \"model_count\": len(current),\n            \"models\": current,\n        }, indent=1))\n        print(f\"\\n  snapshot written to {SNAPSHOT_FILE}\")\n\n    return 0\n\n\ndef cmd_image(args) -> int:\n    kind = args.kind\n    model, reason = tiers.pick_special_model(args.description, kind)\n    index = catalogue_index()\n    row = index.get(model) or {}\n\n    print(f\"IMAGE MODEL for: {args.description}\\n\")\n    print(f\"  pick    : {model}\")\n    print(f\"  reason  : {reason}\")\n    print(f\"  role    : {tiers.SPECIAL_MODELS.get(model, (0, '', 'n/a', ''))[2]}\")\n    print(f\"  NPU     : {row.get('npu', tiers.model_npu(model))}%\")\n    print(f\"  state   : {row.get('status', 'unknown')}\"\n          f\"{' — RESIDENT' if model in resident_ids() else ''}\")\n    if model in tiers.KNOWN_GOOD_SIZES:\n        print(f\"  sizes   : {', '.join(tiers.KNOWN_GOOD_SIZES[model])}\")\n\n    alternatives = [\n        rid for rid, entry in tiers.SPECIAL_MODELS.items()\n        if entry[1] == kind and rid != model\n    ]\n    if alternatives:\n        print(\"\\n  other candidates for this kind:\")\n        for rid in alternatives:\n            status = (index.get(rid) or {}).get(\"status\", \"?\")\n            npu = (index.get(rid) or {}).get(\"npu\", tiers.model_npu(rid))\n            note = \"\" if status in (\"downloaded\", \"running\") else \"  (not downloaded — download it first)\"\n            print(f\"    {rid}  {npu}% NPU, {status}{note}\")\n\n    if args.load:\n        print()\n        hold_write([model])\n        if model not in resident_ids():\n            print(f\"  loading {model} ...\", flush=True)\n            cli_load(model)\n        if model in resident_ids():\n            print(f\"  loaded and resident. Release with 'tiiny_ctl.py release'.\")\n            return 0\n        print(\"  the model did not land\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n    return 0\n\n\n# --- entry point ------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Control the Tiiny device: plan, lock, load, release, refresh.\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\"--device\", help=\"target device name (e.g. tiiny-ant, tiiny-octo) or IP address\")\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\")\n\n    sub.add_parser(\"status\", help=\"device, resident set, lock, hold, plan\")\n\n    plan = sub.add_parser(\"plan\", help=\"pick models, check the NPU fit, write the plan\")\n    plan.add_argument(\"--goal\", action=\"append\", help=\"a task goal; repeat for a batch\")\n    plan.add_argument(\"--tier\", choices=tiers.VALID_TIERS, help=\"force one tier for every goal\")\n    plan.add_argument(\"--model\", help=\"force one model for every goal\")\n    plan.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"), help=\"who is planning\")\n    plan.add_argument(\"--task\", default=\"\", help=\"what the batch is for\")\n\n    reserve = sub.add_parser(\"reserve\", help=\"take the cross-agent lock\")\n    reserve.add_argument(\"--owner\", required=True, help=\"who is taking the device\")\n    reserve.add_argument(\"--task\", default=\"\", help=\"what it is for\")\n    reserve.add_argument(\"--model\", action=\"append\", help=\"a model the batch needs\")\n    reserve.add_argument(\"--ttl\", type=int, default=DEFAULT_TTL, help=\"lock lifetime in seconds\")\n\n    load = sub.add_parser(\"load\", help=\"load what the batch needs\")\n    load.add_argument(\"--model\", action=\"append\", help=\"a model to load\")\n    load.add_argument(\"--wave\", type=int, help=\"load only this wave of the plan\")\n    load.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"),\n                      help=\"who is loading; defaults to the active profile\")\n    load.add_argument(\"--force\", action=\"store_true\",\n                      help=\"load even if another agent holds the device\")\n\n    ensure = sub.add_parser(\n        \"ensure\", help=\"make models resident on demand (replaces the watchdog)\")\n    ensure.add_argument(\"--model\", action=\"append\", required=True,\n                        help=\"a model that must be resident; repeat for several\")\n    ensure.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"),\n                        help=\"who is asking; defaults to the active profile\")\n    ensure.add_argument(\"--force\", action=\"store_true\",\n                        help=\"load even if another agent holds the device\")\n\n    release = sub.add_parser(\"release\", help=\"restore the device and clear the lock\")\n    release.add_argument(\"--owner\", default=os.environ.get(\"TIINY_OWNER\", \"\"),\n                         help=\"who is releasing; defaults to the active profile\")\n    release.add_argument(\"--force\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"release a lock held by someone else\")\n\n    catalog = sub.add_parser(\"catalog\", help=\"diff the live Model Store against the snapshot\")\n    catalog.add_argument(\"--update\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"record the snapshot\")\n    catalog.add_argument(\"--refresh\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"refresh and record in one step\")\n\n    image = sub.add_parser(\"image\", help=\"pick the right image model for a job\")\n    image.add_argument(\"description\", help=\"what the image is for\")\n    image.add_argument(\"--kind\", default=\"image\", choices=sorted(tiers.SPECIAL_DEFAULTS))\n    image.add_argument(\"--load\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"load the chosen model\")\n\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    if getattr(args, \"device\", None):\n        set_active_device(args.device)\n\n    if not args.command:\n        parser.print_help()\n        return 2\n\n    if args.command == \"status\":\n        return cmd_status(args)\n    if args.command == \"plan\":\n        return cmd_plan(args)\n    if args.command == \"reserve\":\n        return cmd_reserve(args)\n    if args.command == \"load\":\n        return cmd_load(args)\n    if args.command == \"ensure\":\n        return cmd_ensure(args)\n    if args.command == \"release\":\n        return cmd_release(args)\n    if args.command == \"catalog\":\n        if args.refresh:\n            args.update = True\n        return cmd_catalog(args)\n    if args.command == \"image\":\n        return cmd_image(args)\n    return 2\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n","numLines":1703,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1703}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012NB5Eqzt1oM7FPVPj2Fk9k","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
